\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar Rallies Against Rivals, Helped by Yen's Weakness \b0\par\par\uc2  By John Parry \par \uc2 969 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Februar 2003\par 01:27\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  The dollar rallied against major rivals Friday, drawing cheer from the yen's weakness and bolstered by some robust U.S. economic data and rebounding stocks. \par \par \uc2  However, the greenback remains vulnerable to the ramifications of ongoing political maneuvering in the march toward a potential U.S.-led war with Iraq. \par \par \uc2  Late Friday in New York, the dollar was trading at 119.86 yen, off its session high of 120.04 yen but solidly up from 119.03 yen late Thursday in New York. The euro was trading at a key support level at $1.0750 and down from $1.0814 late Thursday. The dollar was at 1.3655 Swiss francs, up from 1.3572 francs late Thursday. Sterling was at  $1.6454, down from $1.6540. \par \par \uc2  The announcement by Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 ministry of finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  overnight that it undertook covert \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  this month to weaken the yen drove the Japanese  \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to its biggest drop against the dollar in three weeks on Friday. It left the greenback briefly hovering just above the important psychological 120 yen mark, up from a session low of 118.88 yen and helped ignite a broad-based dollar rebound. \par \par \uc2  As the world's second-biggest economy struggles with the  threat of a renewed economic downturn, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is one of the few recourses \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy makers have at their disposal to encourage growth. \par \par \uc2  In the past, the \b \highlight22\uc2 ministry of finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- the guardian of Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy -- has been much more open with its market forays. But this time, the confirmation that it has been quietly stepping into the market marks a clear shift that has got already-nervous \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders second-guessing any rapid slips in the yen. For a short while at least, this deft new strategy may continue to bear fruit, U.S.-based analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "This kind of stealth \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is certainly not ordinary," said Marcel Kasumovich, head of G10 \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy with Merrill Lynch in New York. \par \par \uc2  The shift from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's past track record of openly trumpeting \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  marks a sea change,  he said. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese monetary authorities focused on slowing the  speed of the yen's appreciation against the dollar, and -- at least in the very near-term -- "they have been successful," Mr. Kasumovich said. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese government sold about 700 billion yen, (close to $6 billion) in several rounds of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  during January and stands ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  more aggressively to rein in excessive movements in the yen, a senior ministry official told reporters in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  "Recent [\b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 ] moves had been volatile and we saw a need to take action," the official said. "Hypothetically speaking, if excessive moves had continued, we could have conducted a large-scale \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  This latest change of tack by the Japanese government --  which \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  openly to the tune of a hefty $30 billion during May and June -- is causing some major \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market participants to bet more heavily that the yen will decline. \par \par \uc2  Robert Sinche, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Citibank -- the biggest \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  bank -- said Friday that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's new approach to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  "is positive news for yen bears." He said it was a significant factor in Citibank's decision to take positions in the market Friday, based on the expectation that the euro will appreciate further against the yen. \par \par \uc2  Intraday, the euro jumped against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to global session peaks around 129.40 yen, breaking its highest levels since May 1999. \par \par \uc2  At home, the dollar savored a whiff of optimism surrounding the U.S. manufacturing sector. \par \par \uc2  The Purchasing Management Association of Chicago index of area business activity rose to 56.0 in January on a seasonally adjusted basis from a revised 51.7 in December. Economists see this report as one of the precursors of the national U.S. Institute for Supply Management's manufacturing report, for release Monday. \par \par \uc2  The median estimate of 12 economists surveyed Friday by Dow Jones Newswires and CNBC is for a reading of 52.8 for January in that report, compared to a revised reading of 55.2 in December. \par \par \uc2  Yet the fairly robust reading from Chicago Friday may be  a portent of a stronger-than-expected ISM report, some \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists suggested. \par \par \uc2  Although the dollar remains vulnerable to negative sentiment surrounding the possibility of a U.S.-led war in Iraq, its positive reaction to the Chicago data shows that "in the absence of any specific war-related news, economic news does come to the forefront," said Larry Brickman, interest-rate and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with Banc of America Securities in New York. \par \par \uc2  The strength of the Chicago report hints that once the standoff with Iraq is resolved -- peacefully or otherwise --  and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets turn their attention firmly back to economic considerations, they may bet more firmly on the possibility that the U.S. economy will pick up the pace toward the end of this year, Mr. Brickman said. \par \par \uc2  Yet in the near-term, much uncertainty lies ahead for the greenback. Traders were listening carefully for more signals about another possible United Nations resolution. President Bush and United Kingdom Prime Minister Tony Blair were meeting at Camp David late Friday in the U.S. session. \par \par \uc2  In Latin America, Brazil's real rallied 1.2% Friday to close at its best level for more than a week as markets took  a breather from tension fueled by the standoff with Iraq. Late Friday, the real was at 3.518 Brazilian reals per dollar, down from 3.56 reals per dollar late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Write to John Parry at john.parry@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  01-31-03 1927ET \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020030201dz21000b5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
