\par\par \b \uc2  INTERVIEW: Ex-BOJ Matsushima: Need Broad, Combined Policy \b0\par\par\uc2  By Hiroshi Inoue \par \uc2 754 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Januar 2003\par 05:28\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- Japan will succeed in ridding its economy of deflation only when the government and the Bank of Japan implement a coordinated, broad-based policy, according to an ex-BOJ senior official. \par \par \uc2  Injecting ever-larger amounts of cash into Japan's financial system or even adopting an inflation target won't be enough to counter widespread expectations that deflation is here to stay, said Masayuki Matsushima, a former BOJ executive director in charge of international affairs. \par \par \uc2  Not only do the government and the BOJ need to work more closely but they need to more publicly show their combined determination to beat deflation, he told Dow Jones Newswires in an interview. \par \par \uc2  The former \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official's comments are in line with the views of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which says its ultra-easy policy can stop a liquidity crunch in Japan's financial markets but can't, on its own, end deflation. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ has long believed that structural economic changes and a boost in government spending are needed to end Japan's long run of price falls. \par \par \uc2  Expected ongoing deflation has helped fuel a surge in JGB prices that has sent yields to record lows. The 10-year JGB yield set a record low of 0.75% Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "The BOJ's policy of injecting liquidity into financial markets alone hasn't been successful in igniting economic activities, Matsushima said. "The government needs to consider fiscal stimulus to solve the problems." \par \par \uc2  Should the government significantly boost spending, the BOJ may need to assist in financing the fiscal stimulus through increasing its purchases of JGBs, he added. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  already buys Y1.2 trillion of JGBs each month. \par \par \uc2  "A collaboration between the BOJ and the government is needed," added Matsushima, who retired from the BOJ last June and now serves as senior adviser at Boston Consulting Group K.K. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par      BOJ, Govt Need To Better Communicate On Policy\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Despite the BOJ's aim to fight deflation, recent comments by BOJ\par officials - that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  can't end deflation on its own and\par doesn't want to adopt an inflation target without the tools to achieve\par it - have partly undercut its deflation-fighting credentials, he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Boosting perceptions of the BOJ's resolve to end deflation are tied to\par a need for better communication with the government on a combined policy\par response, he added.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Some politicians have called on the BOJ to adopt an inflation target.\par But adopting such a plan without a corresponding government fiscal boost\par would risk damage to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's credibility, he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Matsushima also said there are similar problems regarding views on\par taking unorthodox policy steps such as purchasing foreign bonds, which\par some say may be a way to pump money into financial markets and weaken\par the yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par If the BOJ were to announce it would buy foreign bonds, the move\par wouldn't have any effect unless market participants believe the\par government supports the move, he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Supply and demand are key factors in deciding the direction of\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates, but the direction isn't determined by the\par supply-demand of the market alone," he added.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On the yen's exchange rate to the dollar, Matsushima said that while\par the U.S. favors a strong dollar policy, "they would quietly tolerate a\par weak dollar (if it) reflected U.S. economic fundamentals."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The current trend in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets is toward a weak dollar.\par Should the trend continue, the exchange could rate be "a big problem for\par a still deflationary Japan," he added. Matsushima wouldn't comment on\par key levels in the exchange rate that might prompt \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market to stop the yen's rise.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On the U.S. economy, which is considered key to Japan's own economic\par outlook, Matsushima said a moderate recovery is likely, but there are\par still risks.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Capital spending has kicked off in some areas. But spending remains\par sluggish as a whole," he said. "The key is whether personal spending\par holds up until capital spending recovers."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The length of any possible conflict between the U.S. and Iraq is also\par a cause for concern. It would be premature to expect economic "blue\par skies" after the war, he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par And high housing prices may pose a problem if long-term interest rates\par rise from current low levels, Matsushima added.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par   -By Hiroshi Inoue, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-9203;\par hiroshi.inoue@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030131dz1v0006v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppic