\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar rises on upbeat data, war concerns linger. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Daniel Bases \par \uc2 823 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Januar 2003\par 20:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Jan 31 (Reuters) - The dollar rose sharply on Friday, boosted by surprising manufacturing strength in the U.S. Midwest, though consumer sentiment retrenched slightly amid rising concerns of a potential war with Iraq. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan played a role in the dollar's climb by its admission earlier that on several occasions in January it bought $6 billion with yen in order to stem the export-crimping rise of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Traders said the market's extreme short-dollar position, upbeat economic data, solid stock market gains and support from eight European nations for the U.S. position against Iraq all contributed to the dollar's increase ahead of the weekend. \par \par \uc2  "It looks like everything is slightly on the dollar's side today," said David Schoenthal, senior managing director at Bear, Stearns & Co. Inc. "The dollar has moved just too quickly, too fast and has more to do with Iraq than what is going on economically in the United States, so there is some profit taking on the long positions." \par \par \uc2  The market is on the lookout for any news stemming from talks on Friday between U.S. President George W. Bush's and British Prime Minister Tony Blair, America's staunchest ally on Iraqi disarmament. \par \par \uc2  In midday New York trade, the euro flirted with a session low $1.0724 , trading down more than 0.80 percent compared with Thursday's New York close. \par \par \uc2  The dollar broke through to 120.06 yen , a three-week high that represented a gain of more than 0.90 percent. \par \par \uc2  On Wall Street, the Dow Jones industrial average  rose 1.65 percent as stocks climbed on the growth in Midwest manufacturing and solid earnings from entertainment giant Walt Disney Co. \par \par \uc2  LOOKING AT THE BRIGHTER SIDE \par \par \uc2  Some economists believe the Chicago business barometer gives clues to the direction of the national Institute for Supply Management report, due out on Monday. The regional Chicago purchasing managers index rose more than expected to 56.0 in January, well above the consensus estimate of 52.6. A reading above 50 indicates an expansion in activity. \par \par \uc2  However, a final reading on U.S. consumer sentiment in January did not put a drag on the greenback. The University of Michigan's consumer sentiment survey fell to 82.4, below the forecast of 83.0 and under December's reading of 86.7. \par \par \uc2  "The University of Michigan number, although below consensus, was a reasonably good number. But the Chicago PMI number was pretty good, so in a market that is overly short of dollars on a Friday led to a short-cover squeeze that pushed the dollar higher," said Tod Van Name, senior vice president of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Mizuho Corporate Bank in New York. \par \par \uc2  Falling U.S. consumer sentiment mirrored a similar view among consumers in the euro zone, which earlier cast a pall over the euro. Euro zone economic sentiment data for January showed an unexpected downturn in the new year as morale fell for the fourth month running to hit its lowest level since 1997. \par \par \uc2  Euro zone firms, which had until recently proved more upbeat than consumers, also became gloomier against the backdrop of worries over possible war in the Gulf. \par \par \uc2  However, the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's November data showed that investment capital again poured into the euro zone, helping fuel the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's sharp rise against the dollar on the back of geopolitical worries. Direct and portfolio flows amounted to 13.6 billion euros in November, according to the ECB. \par \par \uc2  According to the latest U.S. Treasury data released on Friday, November 2002 net foreign investment into the United States grew to $67.3 billion from $58.1 billion in the same period in 2001. In the January to November period of 2002, net portfolio flow totaled $492.2 billion compared to $466.7 billion in the same period in 2001. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar's apparent cyclical decline last year may have had less to do with declining foreign investment in the United States and more to do with the expanding U.S. current account deficit and speculative flows at year-end linked to geopolitical concerns," Michael Woolfolk, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of New York said in a research note. \par \par \uc2  STEALTHY MOVES \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  confirmed the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rumors, which had helped keep the dollar underpinned during recent sessions, admitting to the first selling action under Japan's recently appointed top financial diplomat Zembei Mizoguchi. \par \par \uc2  Mizoguchi succeeded Haruhiko Kuroda on Jan. 14. Under Kuroda, Japan stepped into the market on several occasions to stop the export-damaging yen rise, but most of the time Japanese monetary officials confirmed their action. \par \par \uc2  "With that type of stealth \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders are not going to be terrified that there will be a day when dollar/yen goes three big figures," said Sean Callow, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at IDEAGlobal. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030131dz1v00xcb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
