\par\par \b \uc2  GLOBAL MARKETS-War fears, economy concerns hit markets. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Jeremy Gaunt, European Investment Correspondent \par \uc2 833 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Januar 2003\par 14:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Jan 31 (Reuters) - Worries about war with Iraq combined with concern over corporate and economic health around the world on Friday to weaken stocks and keep investors hungry for safe haven assets like gold and bonds. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was steady against the euro and yen despite news that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the foreign exchanges this month. \par \par \uc2  Oil prices were level as uncertain markets across the globe awaited any news about the likely scenarios for a Gulf conflict. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said markets were particularly concerned about whether the United States would go it alone or whether it could put together a coalition and get U.N. backing for an attempt to disarm Iraq. \par \par \uc2  "It's all about diplomacy. Everybody is waiting to see what happens...in the Bush-Blair meeting at Camp David," said Audrey Childe-Freeman, European economist at CIBC World Markets. \par \par \uc2  U.S. President George W. Bush and British Prime Minister Tony Blair were to meet for talks later on Friday (2100 GMT) at the U.S. leader's Camp David retreat in Maryland. \par \par \uc2  Blair, Bush's staunchest ally in a divided Europe, was expected to back Bush's hawkish stance against Iraqi leader Saddam Hussein but also urge caution over timing. \par \par \uc2  Bush said on Thursday that diplomatic efforts to end the showdown over Iraq's suspected possession of weapons of mass destruction would last "weeks, not months". \par \par \uc2  "The general picture remains the picture of high uncertainty for the markets," Childe-Freeman said. \par \par \uc2  Defence and Middle East experts polled by Reuters said a war was highly likely to start in the next two months and should be over by the end of June. \par \par \uc2  STOCKS HIT \par \par \uc2  A sharp sell-off overnight on Wall Street that took U.S. stocks to three-month lows helped undermine sentiment on European bourses, where there were already fears that insurance companies will need to sell shares to bolster solvency. \par \par \uc2  The FTSE Eurotop 300 index of pan-European blue chips was about 1.2 percent lower, heading back towards six-year lows after a brief respite from the selling over the past couple of sessions. \par \par \uc2  The narrower DJ Euro Stoxx 50 index was down around 1.6 percent. \par \par \uc2  "The best way of putting it is that equities are cheap but there is an awful lot of uncertainty and anyone trying to say more than that is fishing in the dark," said Chris Johns, global strategist at ABN AMRO bank. \par \par \uc2  U.S. stocks were seen recovering somewhat when Wall Street opens later on Friday after dropping sharply on Thursday on both war and economy fears. Data showed U.S. growth ground to a virtual halt in the last quarter of 2002. \par \par \uc2  The Dow Jones Industrial average lost 2.04 percent, the Standard & Poor's 500 index fell 2.29 percent and the tech-loaded Nasdaq Composite ended down 2.65 percent. \par \par \uc2  Markets were awaiting the University of Michigan final January consumer sentiment survey, due at 1445 GMT, and the Chicago Purchasing Management Index, due at 1500 GMT, for a fresh outlook. \par \par \uc2  Earlier on Friday in Japan, technology and telecom stocks fell, but the tech-sensitive Nikkei average staged a last-minute rally to close 0.28 percent higher at 8,339.94. The broader TOPIX index lost 0.48 percent at 821.18. \par \par \uc2  BONDS BOUNCE, QUIET DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  The falling stock markets, general concern about weak economic growth and fear of war provided a fillip to euro zone government bonds, lowering yields. \par \par \uc2  The two-year Schatz yield was down four basis points at 2.58 percent. It hit a 3-1/2-year low at 2.54 percent last week. \par \par \uc2  The benchmark 10-year Bund yield was 3.8 basis points lower at 4.052 percent. \par \par \uc2  "Bonds are more closely reflecting the moves in the equities and generally the proximity of war," said John Lee, international rate strategist at Barclays Capital. \par \par \uc2  The dollar held above recent lows ahead of the Camp David talks on Iraq and more U.S. data. \par \par \uc2  After hitting a three-year low against the euro beyond $1.09 this week, the dollar has held above that level. \par \par \uc2  It stood at around $1.082 against the euro, little changed from late New York levels on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  It was around 119.42 yen, having risen briefly after a Japanese finance ministry official said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold yen in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  this month. \par \par \uc2  Gold was trading at around $370 an ounce, close to Thursday's close in New York. \par \par \uc2  "Gold prices...remain supported by the increasing likelihood of war in Iraq and uncertainty surrounding the strength of the recovery in the U.S. economy," said Deutsche Bank in a report. \par \par \uc2  On the oil market IPE Brent crude oil futures were trading in a narrow range ahead of new Iraq news. \par \par \uc2  The March contract was up one cent at $31.22 a barrel after settling 33 cents higher on Thursday. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030131dz1v00hlg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
