\par\par \b \uc2  GLOBAL MARKETS-Stocks fall, oil gains as timing of war debated. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Raju Gopalakrishnan \par \uc2 812 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Januar 2003\par 07:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Jan 28 (Reuters) - Japanese and Australian stocks fell on Tuesday and oil prices rose on fears a U.S-led attack on Iraq was inevitable, but a dip in gold prices and other market moves suggested some investors thought war was not imminent. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was volatile but more or less flat against the yen after a brief rally overnight on talk that the Federal Reserve, the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had either checked rates or \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It was holding on to slim gains against the euro after falling to a three-year low on Monday. \par \par \uc2  U.S. bond prices were little changed in Asian trade and oil rebounded after a steep fall on Monday. Stocks fell in Japan and Australia after the Dow average of top U.S. shares ended 1.74 percent down at 7,989.56, dipping below 8,000 points for the first time since October 15. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said a report by U.N. arms inspectors on Iraq's weapons, submitted to the Security Council on Monday, indicated that a conflict was inevitable and only the timing was in question. A key pointer would be President George W. Bush's State of the Union address at 0200 GMT on Wednesday, they said. \par \par \uc2  "The U.N. inspectors' dismal stories and the negative reaction from the U.S. suggest that war in Iraq is increasingly likely," said Marshall Gittler, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Deustche Bank in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  "However, the U.S. has to play for time to get its troops into place and the most likely time for invasion is end-February to early March. That's plenty more time for the dollar to weaken further." \par \par \uc2  Others said war could be up to three months away. \par \par \uc2  "There is still a chance of an attack in the near-term, but the overall possibility has risen that any attack will be deferred by two to three months," said Simon Games-Thomas, an oil analyst in Sydney. He noted traders had been pushing up prices of oil futures contracts as far out as June in recent sessions. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was trading at about 118.60 yen as of 0610 GMT, having ended New York trade at around 118.49 yen. The euro was at $1.0834 and 128.46 yen after edging up to three-year highs of $1.0907 and 128.97 yen. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the market grew nervous about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  after Bundesbank President and European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  member Ernst Welteke said on Monday it would be a worrying sign if the dollar fell too quickly against the euro. \par \par \uc2  GOLD EASES, TREASURIES FLAT \par \par \uc2  Spot gold was quoted around $368.25 an ounce, down from New York's last quoted price of $369.50. Gold had stormed to a six-year high on Monday on the fears of war, hitting $372.55 per ounce in European trading. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasuries were steady after a drop in U.S. trade triggered by a rumour that Iraqi President Saddam Hussein was seeking to go into exile. \par \par \uc2  Still, Treasuries were seen generally supported by safe-haven bids given a view in the bond market that Washington would attack Iraq soon. \par \par \uc2  Oil prices remained volatile. Front-month March crude stood at $32.71 per barrel at 0639 GMT, up 42 cents from Monday's close in New York, where it fell 99 cents after the Blix report. \par \par \uc2  "Some traders are looking at whether the threat of war has really subsided, and are taking positions in case the State of the Union address is really more aggressive than the previous rhetoric from Bush," said John Hirjee, senior energy analyst at Deutsche Bank in Melbourne. \par \par \uc2  In Tokyo, the Nikkei average closed almost one percent down at 8,525.39, ending lower for the third straight day. \par \par \uc2  Australian stocks closed at a six-week low as the benchmark S&P/ASX 200 index fell 2.04 percent to 2,964.4. But stocks gained in Hong Kong, Singapore, South Korea and Taiwan as bargain hunters took advantage of recent steep falls. \par \par \uc2  "There's no particular area showing any signs of strength, even the normal safe havens," said Ross MacMillan, equities analyst at Commonwealth Securities in Sydney. "This will definitely continue until the situation with the U.S. and Iraq is resolved." \par \par \uc2  There could be some resolution of that uncertainty with Bush's address, analysts said. But the overall impact of the U.N. arms inspectors' report suggested war was inevitable, they said. \par \par \uc2  "To the extent that you thought Bush needed any extra ammunition, clearly this helps him," said Dominic Konstam, interest rate strategist at Credit Suisse First Boston, of the U.N. report. \par \par \uc2  "I think everyone accepts it's inevitable that there's going to be some kind of conflict." \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030128dz1s006sh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
