\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar Gains on Yen in Volatile Session \b0\par\par\uc2 1,038 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Januar 2003\par 23:59\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Dow Jones Newswires\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar took a helter skelter ride Monday on a fusillade of remarks\par from top United Nations, U.S. and European officials about the tense\par standoff with Iraq, finishing mixed against major rivals.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  Late Monday afternoon in New York, the euro was at $1.0853, up from $1.0841 late Friday. The dollar was at 118.47 yen, up from 117.72 yen. The pound was at $1.6359, up from $1.6347 late Friday. \par \par \uc2  Although by and large \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders still expect a U.S.-led war on Iraq to occur sometime in the next few months, the stream of information and rhetorical fencing Monday left them none the wiser about timing. In a volatile trading session, the dollar came full circle against most major rivals, remaining the most visible symbol of the tide of risk aversion permeating global asset markets. \par \par \uc2  While gold soared to six-year highs, reaching more than $370 per troy ounce in intraday trade, U.S. stock markets broke below some key support levels, reflecting both discomfort with riskier global assets and the creeping effects of a dollar that continues sinking, especially against the euro. The greenback hit fresh 39-month lows against the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Monday, which briefly captured the $1.09 mark before retreating somewhat. \par \par \uc2  "People are obviously worried about the impact of war and the pressure on the dollar is probably more of a reason for gold climbing than anything else," said Ed Stapleton, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Fortis USA Financial Markets in New York. \par \par \uc2  Rounding off Monday's cacophony of monologues on the global political stage, Secretary of State Colin Powell -- perceived as one of the more dovish figures in President Bush's administration -- declared Iraq in "material breach" of U.N. resolutions. That hawkish-sounding remark weighed on the dollar. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets also paid close attention to comments by White House spokesman Ari Fleischer, indicating the administration plans to draft a second U.N. resolution aimed at authorizing war against Iraq, intensifying a sense that the U.S., along with the U.K., may be prepared to go to war over the next few weeks. \par \par \uc2  Initially, the dollar leapt across the board early in the New York morning, prompting some analysts to question whether the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But there was no concrete evidence of any \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action. \par \par \uc2  Others attributed the dollar's jump to investors betting that Chief U.N. weapons inspector Hans Blix would, during testimony to the U.N. Security Council, make the case for more time to carry out inspections in Iraq. In fact, Mr. Blix's report was somewhat more hawkish, if anything. \par \par \uc2  While he told the Security Council that Iraq had cooperated with his investigators, he said many questions about its chemical and biological weapons were still not resolved. Mr. Blix didn't directly ask the council for more time, although the director of the International Atomic Agency, Mohammed ElBaradei said several more months "would be a valuable investment in peace, because they could help us avoid a war." \par \par \uc2  Mr. ElBaradei said so far nuclear inspections of 106 sites had turned up nothing. "We have to date found no evidence that Iraq has revived its nuclear program since the elimination of the program in the 1990s," he noted. "However, our work is steadily progressing and should be allowed to run its natural course." \par \par \uc2  Even though the dollar clocked up hefty gains early in the day, the greenback reversed course in the early afternoon amid signals the U.S. may be just as disposed to seek a swift military confrontation with Iraq. \par \par \uc2  The dollar took little heart from the weapons inspectors' report, which "still leaves room for the possibility of unilateral action by the U.S. and the U.K.," said Ashraf Laidi, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at MG Financial, in New York. \par \par \uc2  Indeed, the Bush administration dismissed Iraq's response as inadequate, and U.S. Ambassador John Negroponte said he had heard nothing that gave "any hope that Iraq will disarm" voluntarily. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's nervous gyrations underscore the uncertainty surrounding the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , stemming not purely from Iraq jitters. The dollar will have to contend with confirmation hearings for Treasury Secretary nominee John Snow Tuesday morning in Washington, followed by Mr. Bush's State of the Union address Tuesday night. \par \par \uc2  That speech "is the key item we're going to be trading off for the rest of the week," said Tim Mazanec, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with Investors Bank & Trust in Boston. \par \par \uc2  Analysts will listen closely not just for the president's view of the U.S. economy, but for any indications of links between Saddam Hussein's regime and terrorist group al Qaeda. If Mr. Bush provides evidence of a connection between the two, "you could see a wave of support get behind the U.S." from more countries that might also become disposed to use military force against Iraq, said Mr. Stapleton. \par \par \uc2  He added the prospect of more broadly supported action "wouldn't be as dollar negative" as is the current perception the U.S. is almost unilateral in threatening war. \par \par \uc2  Capital flows out of the U.S. seeking higher-yielding sovereigns are also acting as weights on the dollar. The poor showing by stocks and Treasury bonds Monday came both as a reminder and reflection of waning foreign interest in U.S. assets. \par \par \uc2  In intraday trade, the S&P 500 index broke below a key 860 support level, rendering many U.S. stock holdings by European investors under water. Typically, such a move would be accompanied by a sizable rally in Treasurys, but government securities prices retreated. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's continued slide against its major counterparts this year has big consequences for foreign investors, whose disaffection with U.S. stocks may spread into the Treasury market, where they are an even more pivotal force, holding some 40% of securities outstanding. \par \par \uc2  Write to John Parry at john.parry@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  (Grainne McCarthy, Tyler Lifton and Michael Mackenzie contributed to this report) \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  01-27-03 1759ET \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020030127dz1r008y9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
