\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Options: Vols Higher; Dollar Climbs Vs Yen In Spot \b0\par\par\uc2 407 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Januar 2003\par 20:59\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Volatilities implied by one-month dollar/yen \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  options were higher midafternoon Monday in New York, compared with levels earlier in Tokyo, after the dollar climbed against the yen on fears that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  might  move to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. Euro/dollar \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2   options volatilities were little-changed. \par \par \uc2  After clocking up hefty gains earlier in the day, the dollar reversed course early afternoon Monday, amid signals that the U.S. may be just as disposed to seek a swift military confrontation with Iraq. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's gyrations so far Monday show that uncertainty surrounding the possibility and potential timing  of a U.S.-led war with Iraq remains intense and is unlikely  to abate anytime soon, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists said. \par \par \uc2  Yet the greenback made stronger and more decisive gains against the yen than against some other major rivals, buoyed  by fears that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  with the aim of weakening the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Around 2:00 p.m. EST (1900 GMT) in New York, the euro was at $1.0848, up from $1.0841 late Friday. Against the yen, the dollar was at Y118.57, markedly up from Y117.72 late Friday. Against the Swiss franc, the dollar was at CHF1.3538, up from CHF1.3510 late Friday. The pound was at $1.6356, up from $1.6347 late Friday. \par \par \uc2  In the over-the-counter market, one-month at-the-money dollar/yen options implied volatility of 10.0%-10.3%, up from 9.85%-10.15% earlier in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  One-month euro/dollar option volatilities were at 10.4%-10.8%, from 10.4%-10.6% earlier in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  Volatility measures the expected range in the value of an asset over the period of the contract. \par \par \uc2  Volatilities on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  options gauge the anticipated moves in the underlying \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with a higher volatility implying a wider trading range. \par \par \uc2  One-month risk-reversals on dollar/yen favored dollar puts at 0.4%-0.8%, compared with 0.65%-0.9% earlier in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  Risk-reversals measure the market preference for either put or call options by the premium or discount applied. \par \par \uc2  Euro calls were favored over the dollar at 0.6%-1.1%, from 1.1%-1.4% for euro calls earlier in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -By John Parry, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-2096;\par john.parry@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1077 4127 44427 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030127dz1r0018s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
