\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar bearish but cautious ahead of U.N. report. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Kazunori Takada \par \uc2 686 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Januar 2003\par 08:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Jan 27 (Reuters) - The dollar held off deeper losses in Asia on Monday as some market players cast their gaze beyond a possible war in Iraq and saw the greenback's near-term vulnerability coming to an end. \par \par \uc2  But investors weren't yet ready to throw caution to the wind as they awaited a report on Iraq by U.N. weapons inspectors later in the day and other key events this week. \par \par \uc2  Most traders expected the report, which will be submitted to the U.N Security Council, to hold no surprises, but the over-riding sentiment in the market is that a U.S.-led war on Iraq was unavoidable. \par \par \uc2  Still, some traders said that possibility has now been fully factored in. \par \par \uc2  "There really aren't any factors to further push down the dollar," said Jun Kitazawa, assistant vice president of \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  section at Brown Brothers Harriman in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  "I think the war factor has pretty much been digested and the effects of it appear to be wearing out." \par \par \uc2  By 0646 GMT, the dollar was holding firm at 117.81 yen against 117.82 yen in late U.S. trade. \par \par \uc2  The euro was little moved at $1.0838 compared with $1.0828 in late U.S. trade on Friday when it hit a three-year high of $1.0850. \par \par \uc2  The euro was also steady against the yen at 127.74 yen against 127.58 yen in late New York. \par \par \uc2  BUSH AND SNOW \par \par \uc2  Although the gap between the United States and some of its key allies over how to handle Iraq showed no sign of narrowing, many Tokyo traders expected Washington to go ahead and launch an attack. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Secretary of State Colin Powell said on Sunday that the United States was willing to attack Iraq alone if allies peeled away, while French Foreign Minister Dominique de Villepin said U.N. inspectors needed more time and that Washington should not rush alone into an attack on Iraq. \par \par \uc2  Britain, a staunch supporter of the U.S. hard line, said U.N. inspectors should be given time, but not months, to decide if Baghdad is cooperating with them. \par \par \uc2  The United States has already sent a large number of troops and several aircraft carriers into the Middle East region and waiting months for a more thorough report from the U.N. inspectors may be too costly for Washington, some analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "I think the market has already priced in the possibility of a war," said a fund manager. "If a war really happens, the dollar may fall for a short while but maybe that's it. That may be the time to close (dollar-short) positions." \par \par \uc2  Some traders also think Japanese monetary authorities may capitalise on any fall in the dollar after a war begins to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market. \par \par \uc2  "I think they will wait until the U.S. attacks Iraq and the dollar falls. That would give them a perfect justification for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Kota Kimura, assistant manager at Shinkin \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's top financial diplomat, Zembei Mizoguchi, reiterated on Monday that Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy was unchanged and that necessary action would be taken on exchange rates if needed. \par \par \uc2  Also on the market's radar screens are U.S. President George W. Bush's State of the Union address and U.S. Treasury Secretary designate John Snow's confirmation hearing in Congress, both scheduled for Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Most traders said Snow has no choice but to stick to the official U.S. line of wanting a strong dollar, given that the nation needs foreign capital to finance its current account deficit. \par \par \uc2  "There are some people who expect him to come out in favour of a weaker dollar, (but) I think they are mistaken," said Marshall Gittler, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Deutsche Bank in Tokyo. "I think those people will be disappointed." \par \par \uc2  "His job is going to be to finance the growing U.S. budget deficit," Gittler added. "Why on earth would he want to kick a major group of investors in the shins?" \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030128dz1r00c72\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
