\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -War talk makes dollar sales an easy decision. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Daniel Bases \par \uc2 940 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Januar 2003\par 23:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Jan 23 (Reuters) - Iraq war talk dominated the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets again on Thursday, giving investors an easy decision: sell the buck on concerns increased opposition to swift military action will be costly for the United States. \par \par \uc2  The dollar fell to a fresh three-year low against the euro for the sixth consecutive session and also fell to a four-year low against the Swiss franc as China and Russia, as well as Canada, joined France and Germany in opposing any rush to war. \par \par \uc2  The growing number of some of America's staunchest allies who opposed Washington's military build-up in the Gulf and declaration it will make a unilateral military strike if it feels justified is dragging the greenback lower. \par \par \uc2  "The fact that there is no cohesive multi-lateral support for military action in Iraq doesn't play well with the dollar," said Tod Van Name, senior vice president in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  group at Mizuho Corporate Bank in New York, adding: "It looks like bush is determined to go in their and bust some heads regardless of what the Europeans, or anybody else thinks." \par \par \uc2  The dollar has fallen 9 percent against the euro since December on worries a U.S.-led war will create added costs for an already sluggish economy. \par \par \uc2  On Thursday, Washington shrugged off growing opposition to a war. U.S. Secretary of State Colin Powell said the United States could find supporters for military action. \par \par \uc2  Next Monday's report from Chief U.N. Weapons Inspector Hans Blix is considered a crucial milestone in whether the United States or its allies find justification for declaring war on Iraq for failure to rid itself of weapons of mass destruction. \par \par \uc2  The dollar slipped to $1.0777 per euro , its lowest since Oct. 22, 1999, after breaching technical support at $1.0750. It also dropped to a new four-year low against the Swiss franc at 1.3576 francs . \par \par \uc2  EURO ATTRACTS FRIENDS \par \par \uc2  Dealers agreed the euro's rise was not only about risk aversion. \par \par \uc2  If flight-to-safety buying was the only factor working against the dollar, then the safe-haven Swiss franc should have been gaining on the euro. Instead, the franc is down 1 percent against the euro so far this year, attracting some flows on its own merits, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "To some extent, it's not just a dollar drop anymore. The euro is making its own modest contribution in the form of the data, which has turned more positive," said Anne Parker Mills, senior economist for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Brown Brothers Harriman in New York. \par \par \uc2  "No one is going to bet on the euro zone as an engine of global recovery, but this is another factor in favor of the euro," she said. \par \par \uc2  RUSSIA SNUBS THE DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  Also knocking the dollar lower was news Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would raise the level of its non-dollar reserves because of low returns on the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "It always makes people nervous because there has been a lot of talk about central banks diversifying away from the dollar and news of this will certainly add to that," said Steven Saywell, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Citibank in London. \par \par \uc2  But Marc Chandler, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at HSBC in New York, said the move is not so dramatic given the Russians have been raising overall reserve levels, boosting purchases of both euro's and dollars. \par \par \uc2  SHIOKAWA SURPRISES \par \par \uc2  The dollar fell versus the yen after Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa said a stronger yen was not good for domestic businesses but the government should not actively \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the remarks eased speculation about yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the near-term and opened the door for some dollar selling. But most expected the BOJ would show its hand if the market became unruly. \par \par \uc2  The dollar fell to 118.05 yen , down 0.22 percent. \par \par \uc2  "Shiokawa's comments gave the market an excuse for some profit-taking,' said Mills of Brown Brothers Harriman. "People don't want to hold the dollar now, but if the market goes against them, they will be out very quick." \par \par \uc2  Sean Callow, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at IDEAGlobal said other developments were favoring the yen on Thursday, notably a two percent rise in Japan's Nikkei stock index and data showing foreigners were big buyers of Japanese stocks last week. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bid prices at 4:38 p.m. (2138 GMT). All data taken\par from Reuters calculated from the levels at 4:30 p.m. (2130 GMT)\par in the previous New York session.\par  \par                      Last     US Close   %     YTD %   2002\par                               22 Jan.  Change  Change  Close\par -------------------------------------------------------------\par Euro/dlr             1.0754   1.0727   +0.25   +3.05   1.0436\par Dlr/yen              118.05   118.31   -0.22   -1.52   119.87\par Euro/yen             126.98   126.91   +0.06   +1.52   125.08\par Dlr/swiss            1.3615   1.3637   -0.16   -2.03   1.3897\par Stg