\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar extends losses as euro attracts. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Andrea Ricci \par \uc2 797 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Januar 2003\par 20:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Jan 23 (Reuters) - The dollar extended its losses on Thursday to post another three-year low against the euro as traders spurned a dollar tainted by geopolitical risk in favor of the single European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The dollar slipped to $1.0777 per euro , its lowest since Oct. 22, 1999, after breaching technical support at $1.0750. It also dropped to a new four-year low against the Swiss franc at 1.3576 francs . \par \par \uc2  "People are really playing for dollar weakness through the euro. It is the most reliable cross with the shallowest pullbacks and half-decent yield," said Sean Callow, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at IDEAGlobal. \par \par \uc2  The dollar has been falling steadily since early December as investors shed U.S. assets on views a war with Iraq would be costly to the U.S. economy. \par \par \uc2  On Thursday, Washington shrugged off growing opposition to a war as big powers lined up to reject military action. \par \par \uc2  China and Russia, as well as Canada, joined France and Germany in opposing any rush to war, but U.S. Secretary of State Colin Powell said the U.S. could find supporters for military action. \par \par \uc2  Callow said the White House's harsh tone was scaring away even those traders who had been looking for a correction to the dollar's sharp decline. \par \par \uc2  "U.S. rhetoric has been so hawkish on Iraq that even people who were expecting a bit of a reprieve for the dollar have just given up," he said. \par \par \uc2  EURO ATTRACTS FRIENDS \par \par \uc2  But dealers agreed that the euro's rise was not only about risk aversion. \par \par \uc2  If flight-to-safety buying was the only factor working against the dollar, then the safe-haven Swiss franc should have been gaining on the euro. Instead, it has been losing ground to the common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year. \par \par \uc2  Rather, they said the euro was starting to attract some flows on its own merits. \par \par \uc2  "To some extent, it's not just a dollar drop anymore. The euro is making its own modest contribution in the form of the data, which has turned more positive," said Anne Parker Mills, senior economist for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Brown Brothers Harriman in New York. \par \par \uc2  "No one is going to bet on the euro zone as an engine of global recovery, but this is another factor in favor of the euro," she said. \par \par \uc2  By early afternoon in New York, the dollar was off about a third of a percent against the euro at $1.0750 per euro. It fell 0.3 percent against the Swiss franc to 1.3601 francs. \par \par \uc2  RUSSIA SNUBS THE DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  Also knocking the dollar lower was news Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would raise the level of its non-dollar reserves because of low returns on the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "It always makes people nervous because there has been a lot of talk about central banks diversifying away from the dollar and news of this will certainly add to that," said Steven Saywell, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Citibank in London. \par \par \uc2  But Marc Chandler, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at HSBC in New York, said the effects of Russia's move on the dollar was likely to be muted. Because its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been increasing its level of reserves overall, both its purchases of dollars and of euros and other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were likely to rise, he said. \par \par \uc2  SHIOKAWA SURPRISES \par \par \uc2  The dollar also was easier against the yen, pushed down by comments by Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa. \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa said that a stronger yen was not good for domestic businesses but that the government should not actively \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the remarks eased speculation about yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the near-term and opened the door for some dollar selling. But most expected the BOJ would show its hand if the market became unruly. \par \par \uc2  "Shiokawa's comments gave the market an excuse for some profit-taking,' said Mills of Brown Brothers Harriman. "People don't want to hold the dollar now, but if the market goes against them, they will be out very quick." \par \par \uc2  IDEAGlobal's Callow said other developments were favoring the yen on Thursday, notably a two percent rise in Japan's Nikkei stock index and data showing foreigners were big buyers of Japanese stocks last week. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was trading at 117.85 yen , down 0.38 percent. (Reporting by Andrea Ricci; editing by Phil Berlowitz; Reuters Messaging: andrea.ricci.reuters.com@reuters.net; +1 646 223 6314) (Xtra clients: Click on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://topnews.session.rservices.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://topnews.session.rservices.com}}}\uc2  to see Top News pages in multimedia Web format. If you cannot access the pages, ask your IT department to check your Internet firewall settings. For a technical advisory, click on .). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030123dz1n00znm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
