\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar slides to new lows as euro finds favor. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Andrea Ricci \par \uc2 849 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Januar 2003\par 16:32\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  (Updates prices, adds comments, changes dateline, previous London) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Jan 23 (Reuters) - The dollar slid to a fresh three-year low against the euro for the sixth straight day on Thursday as geopolitical worries weighed on the dollar while improved sentiment toward the euro zone favored the euro. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar slipped to $1.0768 per euro  overnight, its lowest since Oct. 22,\par 1999. It also dropped to a new four-year low against the Swiss franc at\par 1.3590 francs       .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar has been falling steadily since early December as investors shed\par U.S. assets on views a war with Iraq would be costly to the U.S. economy.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But the euro's modest rally on Thursday came without benefit of any dramatic\par news on Iraq, and dealers said the euro was starting to attract some flows\par on its own merits.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "To some extent, it's not just a dollar drop anymore. The euro is making its\par own modest contribution in the form of the data, which has turned more\par positive," said Anne Parker Mills, senior economist for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at\par Brown Brothers Harriman in New York.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "No one is going to bet on the euro zone as an engine of global recovery,\par but this is another factor in favor of the euro," she said,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Data from Europe was thin on Thursday, but dealers said they were still\par heartened by news earlier this week of vastly improved expectations for\par Germany, the zone's largest economy.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Also helping the euro have been recent comments by various European\par officials indicating that they are not worried about the euro's strength,\par and indeed welcomed it as a way to keep inflation in check and allow\par growth-boosting rate cuts.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par By midmorning in New York, the dollar was trading at $1.0744 per euro, up\par 0.15 percent in the session. The dollar was off 0.16 percent at 1.3616 Swiss\par francs.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par U.S. ECONOMY STILL AHEAD\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A recent Reuters poll found that economists expect France to be the fastest\par growing euro zone economy in 2003, with the economy expanding 1.6 percent.\par Germany was forecast to grow 0.9 percent.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In contrast, growth in the United States was expected to average a far more\par robust 2.7 percent.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Still, the unevenness of U.S. growth, combined with the country's huge\par current account deficit and the prospect of war with Iraq, was weighing\par heavily on the dollar.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "When capital flows stop, countries with current account deficits tend to\par see weakness," noted Mills.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par White House economic advisor Glenn Hubbard on Thursday said the Bush\par administration was concerned about downside risks to the economy.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Hubbard said business uncertainty was worse than he had seen in years.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Dallas Federal Reserve President Robert McTeer sounded more upbeat, saying\par conditions for a recovery remained good.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par There was little U.S. data due on Thursday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par U.S. weekly jobless claims rose 18,000 to 381,000, below expectations for\par 386,000. The leading indicators index for December rose 0.1 percent versus\par forecasts for a flat reading.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par RUSSIA SNUBS THE DOLLAR\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Also knocking the dollar lower was news Russia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would raise\par the level of its non-dollar reserves because of low returns on the U.S.\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "It always makes people nervous because there has been a lot of talk about\par central banks diversifying away from the dollar and news of this will\par certainly add to that," said Steven Saywell, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Citibank\par in London.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But Marc Chandler, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at HSBC in New York, said the effects\par of Russia's move on the dollar was likely to be muted. Because its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central\par bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  had been increasing its level of reserves overall, both its purchases\par of dollars and of euros and other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  were likely to rise, he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par SHIOKAWA SURPRISES\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar also was easier against the yen, pushed down by comments by\par Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Shiokawa said that a stronger yen was not good for domestic businesses but\par that the government should not actively \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par market.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Dealers said the remarks eased speculation about yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by\par the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the near-term and opened the door for some dollar\par selling. But most expected the BOJ would show its hand if the market became\par unruly.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Shiokawa's comments gave the market an excuse for some profit-taking,' said\par Mills of Brown Brothers Harriman. "People don't want to hold the dollar now,\par but if the market goes against them, they will be out very quick."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar was trading at 117.34 yen , down 0.40 percent. (Reporting by\par Andrea Ricci; editing by Phil Berlowitz; Reuters Messaging:\par andrea.ricci.reuters.com@reuters.net; +1 646 223 6314) (Xtra clients: Click\par on\par \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://topnews.session.rservices.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://topnews.session.rservices.com}}}\uc2 \f2 \par to see Top News pages in multimedia\par Web format. If you cannot access the pages, ask your IT department to check\par your Internet firewall settings. For a technical advisory, click on .).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030123dz1n00u91\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
