\par\par \b \uc2  INTERVIEW-Only huge  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  can stop strong yen-expert. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Yuko Ohba \par \uc2 764 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Januar 2003\par 09:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Jan 23 (Reuters) - Japanese monetary authorities have signalled they want a weaker yen, but that's unlikely to happen without massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to offset the nation's huge current account surplus, an expert on balance of payments said. \par \par \uc2  And Masao Susaki, a professor of international finance at Tokyo's Senshu University, said it would go against International Monetary Fund rules if \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  were seen as a way to manipulate exchange rates to gain a trading advantage. \par \par \uc2  If left to the pure market mechanisms, Susaki said, the yen would strengthen further in the current fiscal year to March and beyond, threatening Japan's export-driven economic growth. \par \par \uc2  To prevent this from happening, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would need to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on a scale of four to five trillion yen ($34-$42 billion) to maintain a dollar/yen rate of around 120 yen, Susaki said in a recent interview. \par \par \uc2  "Japan has a big current account surplus so the dollar/yen rate would depend on the amount of capital outflow," he said. \par \par \uc2  "If capital outflows were greater than the current account figure, that would lead to a strong dollar and weak yen. \par \par \uc2  "Conversely, if the current account were smaller then that would lead to a weak dollar and a strong yen." \par \par \uc2  Susaki said this was becoming more likely as the influence on exchange rates of speculative factors, like hedge funds and investor sentiment, gives way to supply and demand factors, like the current account and capital flows. \par \par \uc2  Susaki expects Japan's current account surplus, the broadest measure of trade in goods and services, for the current fiscal year ending March to be around 12 trillion yen ($101 billion), about the same as the year before. \par \par \uc2  He expects long-term capital outflow to total six to seven trillion yen. That means more money coming into Japan and bolstering the yen. \par \par \uc2  Unless Japan's monetary authorities absorb the excess six trillion yen through yen-selling or dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the "the yen will be greatly pushed up," he said. \par \par \uc2  VIOLATING IMF RULES? \par \par \uc2  The problem for Japan is that it risks violating a key IMF agreement if it appears to be manipulating its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , something Susaki suspects has happened in the past. \par \par \uc2  The IMF permits \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  only to correct volatile swings in the exchange rate. A nation can't jump into the market to block effective balance of payment adjustments or manipulate \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates to get an unfair competitive advantage. \par \par \uc2  "I suspect that what Japanese monetary authorities have done violates the IMF agreement," Susaki said. \par \par \uc2  Since September 2001, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy a total of over seven trillion yen ($59 billion) in dollars and euros. \par \par \uc2  The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the U.S. Federal Reserve have not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  at all in that time. \par \par \uc2  For the fiscal year 2001/02, Japan's current account surplus was 11.9 trillion yen and direct investment abroad totalled eight trillion yen, a difference of 3.9 trillion yen. Under Susaki's current account theory, the yen should have strengthened. \par \par \uc2  In the same period, though, the dollar rose by about seven percent to 126.36 yen from 117.30 yen. \par \par \uc2  Also in the same period, Japanese authorities \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy a total of 3.2 trillion yen worth of dollars and euros - nearly the same as the difference between the current account and capital outflows. \par \par \uc2  JAWBONING \par \par \uc2  Tokyo has kept out of the market since June 28 last year, when the dollar fell below 120 yen to the dollar, even though the dollar dropped as low as 115.50 yen on July 16. \par \par \uc2  But Japanese authorities have repeatedly sent verbal warnings to the market in an effort to talk down the yen. \par \par \uc2  A weaker yen is key to Japan's export sector, one of the stagnant economy's few bright spots. \par \par \uc2  Despite the jawboning, the dollar has continued to slip, pressured by worries over a possible war with Iraq. \par \par \uc2  It fell to a four-month low of 117.40 yen last week. \par \par \uc2  Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi said on Wednesday the government would take appropriate action as needed - a mantra heard repeatedly in recent months from monetary officials. \par \par \uc2  The dollar began 2002 well above 130 yen and rose as high as 135.20 on January 31. Since then, concerns about the outlook for the U.S. economy, as well as worries about a possible war with Iraq, have steadily worn down the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In late Asian trading on Thursday it was trading around 118.42 yen. ($1=118.31 yen). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030123dz1n00fr6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
