\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar off lows for now but war jitters dominate. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Justyna Pawlak \par \uc2 750 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Januar 2003\par 14:32\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Jan 22 (Reuters) - The dollar won a reprieve from its downward slide on Wednesday, stepping up from three-year lows on the euro and rising against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but room for more gains was seen as scant due to growing war fears. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said profit-taking helped the greenback pull back after it set a three-year low on the euro for a fifth successive day, reaching $1.0744 per euro in Asia before the European session's position-squaring kicked in. \par \par \uc2  But worries over a quick U.S.-led attack on Iraq still plagued dollar sentiment with the latest war rhetoric from U.S President George W. Bush ringing loudly in investors' ears and unofficial reports suggesting combat may start in February. \par \par \uc2  "The threat of war is driving euro/dollar," said Francesca Fornasari, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Lehman Brothers. \par \par \uc2  "There has been no fundamental change but we see some consolidation and profit-taking," she said. \par \par \uc2  The euro broke through a key options barrier at $1.07 on Tuesday, and bounced around that level throughout Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  By 1244 GMT the dollar was up 0.2 percent versus the euro as well as the Swiss franc, which earlier retested four-year highs against the greenback. \par \par \uc2  WAR CLOCK TICKING \par \par \uc2  War fears heightened after a Russian military source said Washington and its allies had decided to attack Iraq in mid-February, despite German and French pledges to work toward preventing armed conflict. \par \par \uc2  The report, run by Russian news agency Interfax, came a day after U.S. President George W. Bush said: "It's clear to me now that he (Saddam Hussein) is not disarming. Time is running out." \par \par \uc2  Markets will be watching another speech by Bush later on Wednesday for more hints on whether he would go to war without broad backing from his allies, although the theme of his visit to St Louis is the economy. \par \par \uc2  U.S. companies' fourth-quarter results are also in focus, with some poor earnings on Tuesday depressing sentiment, and more key numbers due out on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Wall Street is seen opening softer later with heavy-hitters, including top U.S. banks, releasing results. \par \par \uc2  BRIEF RESPITE \par \par \uc2  Despite the war fears, the wobbly dollar regained its footing after the euro's approach of key levels around $1.08 faded, sparking some profit-taking. \par \par \uc2  Investor wariness of yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities also continued to loom over the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , keeping the dollar above 118. \par \par \uc2  "If you look at the dollar, it has gone a long way and the positions are starting to get stretched," said Ian Gunner, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Mellon Financial Corporation. \par \par \uc2  "I am not saying it is the end of the move. But if $1.0810 is a key level, then at $1.0744 you are close," he said. \par \par \uc2  The euro rose to 3-1/2 year highs against the yen around 127 in Asian trade on comments from Japanese Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi, before retreating slightly. \par \par \uc2  Koizumi said \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates should be stable and reflect economic fundamentals, adding that Japan would take appropriate action on rates as needed. \par \par \uc2  A spate of comments from European policy officials left the market largely unimpressed. \par \par \uc2  Early on Wednesday Bundesbank President and European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  council member Ernst Welteke left the door open for more rate cuts in the euro zone, telling the Financial Times (Deutschland) daily that "In theory, there is still room to move (on rates)". \par \par \uc2  In line with a global trend for easier monetary policy, the ECB cut its key minimum bid rate by 50 basis points last month, to 2.75 percent. \par \par \uc2  Welteke also stoked the debate as to whether the U.S. administration will abandon its "strong dollar" policy, telling the German sister paper of Britain's Financial Times that he did not believe the dollar was too weak. \par \par \uc2  Spanish Prime Minister Jose Maria Aznar sparked a brief slip in the euro, warning his European peers against letting go of the struggle to keep economic growth up to potential. \par \par \uc2  "At the moment Europe risks having a similar fate as in Japan - a situation in which there is practically no growth," Aznar was quoted as telling a German newspaper. \par \par \uc2  Norway cut its key interest rates by 50 basis points to 6.0 percent as expected, taking the Norwegian crown off a three-month low against the euro set as traders positioned for an even bigger cut. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030122dz1m00pmj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
