\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar off lows for now but war jitters dominate. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Justyna Pawlak \par \uc2 788 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Januar 2003\par 12:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Jan 22 (Reuters) - The dollar won a reprieve from its downward slide on Wednesday, stepping up from three-year lows on the euro and rising against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but the scope for further gains was seen as scant due to growing war jitters. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said profit-taking helped the greenback pull back after it set a three-year low on the euro for a fifth successive day, reaching $1.0744 per euro  in Asia before the European session's position-squaring kicked in. \par \par \uc2  But worries over a quick U.S.-led attack on Iraq still plagued dollar sentiment with the latest war rhetoric from U.S President George W. Bush ringing loudly in investors' ears. \par \par \uc2  "The war is the main theme now," said Paul Mackel, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Dresdner Kleinwort Wasserstein. \par \par \uc2  The euro broke through a key options barrier at $1.07  on Tuesday before retreating to $1.0690. \par \par \uc2  By 1115 GMT the dollar was up 0.3 percent versus the euro as well as the Swiss franc, which earlier lingered near four-year highs against the greenback . \par \par \uc2  The dollar's latest downturn set in on Tuesday after Bush said: "It's clear to me now that he (Saddam Hussein) is not disarming. Time is running out." \par \par \uc2  The sabre-rattling was followed by reports from the Russian news agency Interfax, quoting a military source as saying the U.S. had decided to attack Iraq in the second half of February meaning soon after U.N. arms inspectors submit a report on Iraq's arms programme. \par \par \uc2  Among the five permanent members of the U.N. Security Council, Britain remains a staunch supporter of the United States, but the other three - France, China and Russia - have recently said that the U.N. weapons inspectors needed more time. \par \par \uc2  Chancellor Gerhard Schroeder said on Tuesday that Germany, which took up one of the rotating seats on the Council earlier this month, would not support a U.N. resolution in favour of war against Iraq. \par \par \uc2  "War tension is negative for the dollar. The longer this mood continues, the longer the dollar will stay under pressure," said Koichi Abe, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  section manager at Aozora Bank in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  BRIEF PROFITS \par \par \uc2  The wobbly dollar regained its footing after the euro's approach of key levels around $1.08 faded and wariness of yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese authorities continued to loom over the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , keeping the dollar above 118 . \par \par \uc2  Concern about the impact of a war against Iraq on the U.S. economy and the huge U.S. current account deficit has hit the greenback in recent weeks. \par \par \uc2  But the dollar has found some support against the yen on worries that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and so protect the country's export-led economy. \par \par \uc2  "If you look at the dollar, it has gone a long way and the positions are starting to get stretched," said Ian Gunner, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Mellon Financial Corporation. \par \par \uc2  "I am not saying it is the end of the move. But if $1.0810 is a key level, then at $1.0744 you are close," he said. \par \par \uc2  The euro rose to 3-1/2 year highs against the yen around 127  in Asian trade on comments from Japanese Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi, before retreating slightly. \par \par \uc2  Koizumi said \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates should be stable and reflect economic fundamentals, adding that Japan would take appropriate action on rates as needed. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan left monetary policy unchanged on Wednesday as expected, defying pressure from government leaders for bolder action to bolster the nation's struggling economy. \par \par \uc2  EASIER EUROPEAN RATES \par \par \uc2  Bundesbank President and European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  council member Ernst Welteke left the door open for more rate cuts in the euro zone, telling the Financial Times (Deutschland) newspaper that "In theory, there is still room to move (on rates)". \par \par \uc2  In line with a global trend for easier monetary policy, the ECB cut its key minimum bid rate by 50 basis points last month, to 2.75 percent. \par \par \uc2  Welteke also stoked the debate as to whether the U.S. administration will abandon its "strong dollar" policy, telling the German sister paper of Britain's Financial Times that he did not believe the dollar was too weak. \par \par \uc2  Norway's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meets on Wednesday and is widely expected to cut interest rates by 50 basis points to 6.00 percent, in an attempt to dampen demand for its soaring \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Norwegian crown repeatedly hit record highs against the euro last year and recently struck near-six-year peaks against the dollar, as investors flocked to the oil-backed economy's high yields. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030122dz1m00mjf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
