\par\par \b \uc2  EUROPEAN MORNING BRIEFING: Choppy Conditions Prevail \b0\par\par\uc2 1,997 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Januar 2003\par 06:58\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par US SUMMARY: Investors Have A Lot To Chew On\par DJIA          Closed\par NASDAQ        Closed\par S&P 500       Closed\par Dow Future    8599.00    gain 16.00     up 0.2%\par NASDAQ Future 1024.00    gain 4.00      up 0.4%\par S&P Future    904.50     gain 1.40      up 0.2%\par 10-Yr US Treasury: 3.99% down 0.03\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- U.S. markets, closed Monday for a holiday, will likely see choppy trading to start, with stocks little changed and Treasurys opening slightly higher. \par \par \uc2  STOCKS: Stocks will swing with earnings and political developments this week as they did last week. \par \par \uc2  Analysts watching forward-looking numbers are predicting a powerful recovery in the second half of the year, despite mostly guarded profit forecasts from companies so far. Consensus analyst estimates call for about a 10% rise in profits for the first two quarters, but the figure climbs to 16% for the third quarter and 21% for the fourth. \par \par \uc2  On the political front, the United States and Britain lined up against France, China and Germany in a preview of an expected clash in the U.N. Security Council in the coming weeks over military action against Iraq. \par \par \uc2  The confrontation erupted during and after a high-level council meeting called by France and attended by 13 foreign ministers Monday to give new momentum to the global war on terrorism. \par \par \uc2  North Korea urged South Koreans on Tuesday to push for a reunification of the divided Korean peninsula and resist "U.S. imperialists." \par \par \uc2  And a Russian envoy who presented North Korea with a plan to resolve a dispute over its nuclear program returned from the insular nation Tuesday saying he held "some optimism" that a solution could be found. \par \par \uc2  In corporate news, Echostar's chairman has been talking with Rupert Murdoch's News Corp. as well as Liberty Media about possibly selling his satellite company. \par \par \uc2  BP will cut around 1,000 jobs from its U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as the British oil and natural-gas giant moves to boost efficiency after missing production targets last year. \par \par \uc2  GlaxoSmithKline received FDA approval for its Lamictal tablets as an add-on therapy for partial seizures in children aged two years and older. \par \par \uc2  BONDS: U.S. Treasury prices rose slightly overnight due to small-lots buying, including Japanese investors, bond traders said. \par \par \uc2  But trade was thin with many players taking a wait-and-see attitude amid uncertainty over the U.S. financial markets after a long weekend. The Treasury market in the New York closed Monday for the Martin Luther King Day. \par \par \uc2  "Trade was basically thin but prices rose across the board due to some bidding. Japanese life insurance companies appeared to buy long-end papers," a senior bond trader at a Japanese bank said. \par \par \uc2  Another trader said some Japanese trust banks bought some Treasurys. \par \par \uc2  Traders expect the Treasurys to be ups and downs due to economic data and development of the outlook for a U.S. attack on Iraq and for the U.S. tax cut plans but don't expect the Treasurys to break current trading range immediately. \par \par \uc2  Possible revision of the U.S. tax cuts and its impact on the stock market is also closely watched by traders. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ASIAN SUMMARY: Short-Covering Boosts Japan Shares\par USD-Yen             118.78       gain  0.72    up 0.6%\par AUD-USD             0.5886       loss  0.0027  dn 0.5%\par Nikkei 225          8695.15      gain  135.33  up 1.6%\par Hang Seng           9560.30      gain  8.28    up 0.1%\par JGB Yield           0.8300% up 0.0050\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Tokyo stocks were higher Tuesday toward the end of\par the session on short-covering, followed by broader participation,\par dealers said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Mizuho Holdings, one of Japan's largest banks, jumped 4,000 yen or 3.3\par percent, to 124,000 yen after local media reported it would raise its\par capital by selling new shares to several business partners.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Nihon Keizai Shimbun, a major business daily, said the step was to\par help the bank write off bad loans.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par UFJ, another major bank, also rose in the morning session, but rivals\par Sumitomo Mitsui Financial and Mitsubishi-Tokyo both declined.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A trader said that Japan's life insurers are expecting to see a weaker\par yen on the basis of future Japanese macroeconomic policy, including the\par new governor of Bank of Japan.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par He added that Japanese life insurers would not quickly take profits\par because their purchases of Treasurys are based on changes of asset\par allocations.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Japan's influential lawmakers called on the BOJ for adopting inflation\par target policy and for buying more Japanese government bonds. The BOJ's\par decision of buying more JGBs would result in a weaker yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Japanese Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi said Tuesday he hopes the\par Bank of Japan will take more effective monetary policy measures to help\par boost prices and tackle deflation.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Keeping pressure on the BOJ, which started its two-day policy board\par meeting Tuesday, Koizumi stressed the importance of bringing about\par positive growth in prices "as soon as possible."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par EUROPEAN OUTLOOK: Cautious Mkts To Start Little Changed\par Euro-USD            1.0653      loss  0.0021  dn 0.2%\par Stlg-USD            1.6072      loss  0.0012  dn 0.2%\par USD-Franc           1.3715      gain  0.0029  up 0.2%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ZURICH (Dow Jones)--European shares and government bonds should start\par little changed, with the euro locked in a narrow range around $1.0650.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par STOCKS: European shares should open little changed amid the usual\par geopolitical uncertainties, which will make for volatile trading again.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par European stocks were mostly lower Monday, with U.S. markets shut for\par Martin Luther King Jr. Day, as ongoing uncertainty over a possible war\par with Iraq and concerns over the economic outlook kept market gains in\par check.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par According to market strategist Anais Faraj at Nomura Securities, the\par rest of the week is likely to be dominated by speculation over Iraq and\par the looming "deadline" of the preliminary report from U.N. weapons\par inspectors.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Markets will need plenty of good earnings news this week if equities\par are to hold current levels," he said in a note published Monday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par According to analysts at Deutsche Bank, the two questions that\par dominate market behavior are still: "Are we facing war in Iraq?" and\par "When will we see an economic recovery?"\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par They are advising clients to adopt a more defensive slant to their\par portfolio weightings. "We suspect uncertainties may increase before they\par ease," they said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In corporate news, Fiat said that its car division missed its\par fourth-quarter break-even target and confirmed it is considering\par splitting off the unit.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Infineon's loss narrowed in the latest quarter as gains at its\par memory-chip unit offset weakness at its communications \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par British retailer Kingfisher is expected to sell a portfolio of retail\par parks later this week for an estimated $1.13 billion, according to a\par person familiar with the situation.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par BONDS: Euro-zone government bonds are expected to open little changed,\par although a rise in U.S. Treasurys overnight should provide some\par underpinning.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Euro-zone government bond prices were slightly higher in thin trade\par Monday, with little new to move the market.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Looking ahead, the main-market moving news is likely to be Iraq, with\par the Jan. 27 deadline for the U.N. weapons inspectors' report\par approaching.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "If both the U.S. and Iraq flex their muscles again this week, the\par flight to quality will continue to support government bonds,"\par Commerzbank analysts said in a research note.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Germany's ZEW economic expectations survey, due at the earlier time of\par 1000 GMT Tuesday, will start a sparse data week. ZEW has moved the\par release time forward from 1400 GMT.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A Dow Jones Newswires' survey forecasts the January index unchanged\par from the previous month at 0.6, although analysts are very split. Out of\par nine economists polled, four forecast a further dip, two predicted no\par change, while three expect a rise.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Another weak ZEW reading is likely to be viewed as further reason for\par the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to cut interest rates.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ECB executive board member Sirkka Hamalainen in an interview with Dow\par Jones Newswires, sounded fairly dovish, saying the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is more\par convinced now that inflation is slowing.\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : The euro seems well entrenched above $1.06 for now, but if\par players become dispirited over failures to get the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  over $1.07,\par a slight downward correction could occur.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The likelihood of a U.S.-led strike against Iraq is a primary factor\par working against the dollar. On Monday, Secretary of State Colin Powell\par told members of the United Nations Security Council that they cannot\par back down from difficult choices regarding Iraq. His comments came the\par day after Defense Secretary Donald Rumsfeld suggested the U.S. would\par allow Saddam Hussein to take refuge in another country should he give up\par his dictatorship.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Elsewhere, expectations that the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may be close to\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market to weaken the yen - an action BOJ\par officials have threatened on many occasions in recent months - kept the\par dollar propped up above the Y118 mark, traders said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Indeed, the dollar leapt nearly one yen in Asia Tuesday as a flurry of\par shortcovering triggered stop-loss buying.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The euro also pushed higher against the yen, as Japanese trust banks\par and U.S. funds bought that pair as well.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par CALENDAR: Tuesday, Jan. 21: UK, German Prices; ZEW\par ET/GMT\par 0245/0745 France   Dec Consumer Spending (seen +0.7% MM)\par 0300/0800 Italy    Jan Consumer Confidence (seen 106.8)\par 0300/0800 Italy    Nov Industrial Orders (seen +2.5% MM)\par 0430/0930 U.K.     December M4 Lending (seen +GBP9.8 Bln)\par 0430/0930 U.K.     December Preliminary M4 Money Supply\par 0430/0930 U.K.     December Public Sector Finances\par 0430/0930 U.K.     December Retail Price Index (seen +0.2%)\par 0430/0930 U.K.     December BBA Lending\par 0500/1000 Germany  ZEW Jan Economic Indicator (seen +0.6%)\par 0515/1015 E.U.     European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  allocates bids\par 0600/1100 U.K.     Bank of England auctions EUR1 Bln\par 0745/1245 U.S.     BTM-UBS Warburg Chain Store Sales\par 0830/1330 U.S.     December Housing Starts (seen -1.2%)\par 0830/1330 U.S.     December Building Permits (last -1.9%)\par 0900/1400 U.S.     Redbook Retail Sales for Jan. 18 Week\par  N/A      Germany  Bundesbank confirms 30-year notes\par  N/A      Germany  Jan CPI (seen +0.7%  MM, +0.9% YY)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Dennis Baker; Dow Jones Newswires; 41-1-212-2181;\par dennis.baker@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Logitech International S.A. (LOGI): 3Q Earnings\par Consensus net profit (DJ, 4 analysts): US$37.8M (US$33.2M)\par Consensus sales: US$332.7M (US$299.1M)\par Note: If Christmas business proves weak, Logitech might reduce its FY\par '03 sales guidance of $1.07B, but thanks to good cost controls its '03\par EBIT target of $120M should still be achievable. Logitech to also give\par FY '04 guidance. In addition, investors should focus on development of\par new products such as mobile phone headsets.\par Casino (F.CGD): FY Revenue\par Consensus sales (DJ, 17 analysts): EUR23B (EUR22B)\par Note: CIC says to monitor Geant hypermarkets in France, which account\par for 30% of group sales: forecasts a 2.5% rise in same-store sales and\par 2.2% full-year growth. Expects continued slowdown in Casino\par supermarkets. Also eyes Leader Price, with a 7% rise in same-store\par sales expected.\par MAN (G.MAN): FY '02 Revenue, New Orders\par Consensus sales (DJ, 15 analysts): EUR15.46B (EUR16.30B)\par Note: New orders seen in line with company's forecast of EUR15.7B vs\par '01's EUR15.68B. "We don't expect any more negative surpri