\par\par \b \uc2  THE SKEPTIC: BOJ  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Is Just A Whisper Away \b0\par\par\uc2  By Nicholas Hastings \par \uc2 499 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Januar 2003\par 17:06\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN \par \par \uc2  LONDON -(Dow Jones)- "Why are we waiting?" must be the favorite refrain of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers in Tokyo as they look each day for Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  At under JPY118 to the dollar, the yen is back up into danger territory that will have Japanese exporters gritting their teeth in frustration. \par \par \uc2  The latest answer doing the rounds is that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  is waiting either for Treasury Secretary nominee John Snow's confirmation hearing in Washington or the appointment of a new Bank of Japan Governor to replace Meino Hayami. \par \par \uc2  It's hoped that the former will provide some reassurance on the strong dollar policy and that the latter will represent a more accommodative monetary stance that would back any attempts to lever the yen lower. \par \par \uc2  But pay no heed. \par \par \uc2  The only reason the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  hasn't asked the Bank of Japan to get in there and start furiously selling the yen is that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hasn't fallen far enough yet. There's no point in launching an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  exercise until the Japanese have convinced the market they mean business. \par \par \uc2  Remember, apart from the standard trade flows that continue to expand in the yen's favor any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will also have to counter the repatriation of foreign profits that customarily occurs before the end of the financial year in March. \par \par \uc2  It's not until the dollar is down closer to Y115.00 that the market will know it has a real fight on its hands. After all, that's where the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  launched its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  exercises in September 2001 and June 2002. \par \par \uc2  At the rate the dollar is currently falling this could all come long before either Snow's hearing has taken place at the end of this month or Hayami's succession has been resolved next month. \par \par \uc2  Neither should really matter. \par \par \uc2  Yes, it would be nice if the U.S. wasn't abandoning its strong dollar policy, as some fear this might suggest that any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't have U.S. backing. \par \par \uc2  But with the U.S. Automotive Trade Policy Council already criticizing Japan's attempts to weaken its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Snow's hardly going to be in a position to show his support, even though Japan's success in pulling itself out of recession is just as important to the U.S. as it is to Japan, in the long run. \par \par \uc2  Similarly, while a more responsive Bank of Japan Governor who is more willing to engage in an anti-deflationary battle more than Meino ever was will certainly provide key policy support for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it's already clear Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi has every intention to appoint such a figure. \par \par \uc2  It's now merely a matter of who and when - and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't need to wait for that. \par \par \uc2  -By Nicholas Hastings, Dow Jones Newswires; 44 20 7842 9493; nick.hastings@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  5090 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030117dz1h000zr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
