\par\par \b \uc2  Yen to strengthen despite any  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 -HSBC. \b0\par\par\uc2 443 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Januar 2003\par 10:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Jan 17 (Reuters) - The yen is fundamentally strong because of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's current account surplus, and despite any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  it will strengthen against the U.S. dollar this year, HSBC's London-based \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist said on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Global economic stagnation meant the United States was unable to offer the superior rates of return needed to fund its growing current account deficit, and so the dollar had to weaken, David Bloom told journalists after an investor briefing in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  Japanese officials have repeatedly said in recent weeks they were ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , trying to talk down the yen and protect a fragile-export led recovery. The dollar fell to four-month lows around 117.50 yen  this week, raising \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  worries. \par \par \uc2  "They've always wanted a weaker yen, and they always get a stronger one," Bloom said. \par \par \uc2  "We're in the new stagnation. (The U.S.) no longer has this attraction of superior rates of return and this leaves the U.S. dollar incredibly vulnerable." \par \par \uc2  HSBC's end-2003 yen forecast is 114 to the dollar, a level it was last at in early 2001. \par \par \uc2  The United States would still outperform Japan in growth terms - HSBC expects U.S. growth of 2.0 percent this year and 2.1 percent in 2004 against contractions of 0.5 percent and 0.2 percent in Japan - but the trend was for a weaker dollar. \par \par \uc2  "This idea that just because you've got weak growth, you get a weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is wrong," Bloom said, pointing out the yen had been strong despite 12 years of economic underperformance. \par \par \uc2  "The bottom line is they've got a huge current account surplus, and they haven't got much capital leaving Japan from the Japanese investor, which means the world needs yen." \par \par \uc2  Reuters polling this week found median forecasts for the U.S. trade deficit in 2003 and 2004 of $473 billion and $503 billion respectively. Japan was expected to post a current account surplus of 13.6 trillion yen ($115.3 billion) this year and 13.4 trillion yen in 2004. \par \par \uc2  Bloom argued that if the yen weakened, it would make Japanese exports more competitive and increase the current account surplus - which would in turn cause the yen to strengthen. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan was not the only Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  that did not want to see the dollar weaken, Bloom said, and their efforts would certainly slow the dollar's decline. \par \par \uc2  "So I think the yen will strengthen, but its strengthening will be limited by the \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies of the authorities in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  "The yen is a fundamentally strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ". \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030117dz1h00hab\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
