\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar falls as U.N. finds warheads in Iraq. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Andrea Ricci \par \uc2 701 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Januar 2003\par 23:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Jan 16 (Reuters) - The dollar tumbled to fresh three-year lows against the euro and four-year lows versus the Swiss franc on Thursday after U.N. weapons inspectors in Iraq found empty warheads designed to carry chemical agents. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the discovery raised the likelihood of military action against Iraq and sparked selling of dollars for the safe-haven Swiss franc and the euro. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar fell on this story about U.N. weapons inspectors finding empty warheads in Iraq, breaking the euro through stops that have been in the $1.06 area," said Ralph DelZenero, managing director of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Bank One in Chicago. \par \par \uc2  The euro made short work of $1.0599, this year's high and an area of very stiff resistance, to reach $1.0627 , its highest level against the dollar since Oct. 27, 1999. \par \par \uc2  The dollar also fell through technical levels against the Swiss franc to a new four-year low at 1.3720 francs . \par \par \uc2  "The dollar took out some important chart points, and with a real story behind it, and that takes on a good deal of significance," said David Gilmore, partner at \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Analytics. \par \par \uc2  Gold bolted to six-year highs on reports of the warheads cache, while stock prices fell. \par \par \uc2  The dollar stayed weak even after Iraq called the find a "storm in a teacup," and the United States said the discovery was not the hard evidence it was looking for to prove Iraq possessed chemical weapons. \par \par \uc2  By late afternoon, the euro was trading at $1.0616, up 0.66 percent on the day. The dollar was off 0.78 percent to 1.3741 Swiss francs. \par \par \uc2  The dollar fell as far as 117.68 yen, short of its recent lows at 117.52 yen . By late afternoon in New York, it was trading at 117.78 yen, down 0.28 percent on the day. \par \par \uc2  Concerns about possible Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  limited losses, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "There seems to be some dollar-buying interest around 117.50/60 yen, where people are saying the Bank of Japan is looking intently," said Vincent Palazzolo, chief dealer at SMBC in New York. \par \par \uc2  POOR OUTLOOK FROM PHILADELPHIA FED \par \par \uc2  Dealers paid little mind to a mild U.S. inflation report and a sharp drop in jobless claims. But a somber report from the Philadelphia Federal Reserve contributed to the dollar's heavy tone. \par \par \uc2  The Philadelphia Fed's main manufacturing index came in at 11.2 in January, little changed from 11.3 in December and well above the consensus forecast of a 7.6 reading. \par \par \uc2  Some economists had been expecting a far better number after a surprisingly strong report from the New York Fed earlier this week. The report also disappointed because, while there were some signs of strength in new orders, the companies surveyed gave a much gloomier outlook on employment prospects. \par \par \uc2  "The soft data merely reinforces (the dollar's) weaker position," said Bob Lynch, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNP Paribas in New York. \par \par \uc2  Data from Europe also were uninspiring, with Germany growing an anemic 0.2 percent in the fourth quarter of 2002. \par \par \uc2  On Friday, the U.S. government releases industrial production and capacity use data for November while the University of Michigan unveils its preliminary sentiment survey for January. Both the United States and Canada will release November trade figures. \par \par \uc2  FORINT IN FOCUS \par \par \uc2  The Hungarian forint was the biggest mover on Thursday as the government fought to hold down the surging \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by slashing interest rates and staging huge market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  within the upper limits of its band. \par \par \uc2  The Hungarian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut its key interest rates by 100 basis points, the second such cutting of interest rates in two days, bringing rates down to 6.5 percent in an attempt to force speculators to abandon upward pressure on the forint. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dropped to 250.00 per euro from 234.69 on the move, a 6 percent fall. \par \par \uc2  Foreign capital has been pouring into the country to take advantage of Hungary's high rates and its security as a future European Union member. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030116dz1g016ap\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
