\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar Ends Little Changed vs. Yen and Euro \b0\par\par\uc2  By John Parry \par \uc2 999 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Januar 2003\par 23:25\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -- After being kicked early in the day by a diplomatic row between the U.S. and North Korea and some worries about the quality of U.S. corporate earnings, the dollar crawled back Wednesday to end New York trading a shade firmer against its key rivals. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. unit struggled with stocks' descent, pressured largely by a scaling back in capital spending plans by semiconductor maker Intel Corp., but ultimately bounced back  a bit even with the 1.35% fall of the Dow Jones Industrial Average. \par \par \uc2  One factor aiding the dollar was the euro's retreat in the New York afternoon after credit-ratings agency Standard & Poor's downgraded its outlook for Italy's debt rating, in the process focusing attention again on the fiscal positions   of several European economies. Among the other strongly performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Hungarian forint surged despite frantic efforts by Hungary's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rein it in. \par \par \uc2  S&P switched its outlook on Italy to negative from stable, in part reflecting the "persistence of large structural fiscal deficits and the lack of a well-defined medium-term fiscal strategy." The agency expects Italy's budget deficit to remain above 3% of gross domestic product for the next three years and said it would downgrade Italy's   rating within one to two years if the country fails to address its budgetary situation. \par \par \uc2  Stubbornly high fiscal deficits, which have exceeded the   3.0% limit outlined in the European Union's Growth and Stability Pact, are clearly not just an Italian problem. \par \par \uc2  The euro was pushed somewhat lower in the wake of S&P's announcement, not least because S&P's statement fed concerns   about the ratings of other euro zone countries, amid persistent speculation over the past few months that Germany's prized triple-A rating may be in some sort of jeopardy. \par \par \uc2  Late Wednesday in New York, the dollar was trading at 118.10 yen, well off its session lows and up a shade from 118.06 yen late Tuesday. The euro was at $1.0548, off its $1.0592 high for the day and slightly lower than $1.0550 late Tuesday. Against the Swiss franc, the dollar declined to 1.3848 francs, from 1.3858 francs late Tuesday. Sterling was at $1.6011, down from $1.6048. \par \par \uc2  The dollar had been pressured earlier after U.S. equity markets opened lower, a decline that only gained pace with reports that North Korea was rejecting President George W. Bush's offer of aid, energy and ultimately diplomatic and security agreements to the politically and economically isolated country. \par \par \uc2  The Bush administration's "loudmouthed supply of energy and food aid are like a painted cake pie in the sky", a North Korean Foreign Ministry spokesman said in a report by the country's foreign news outlet, KCNA. \par \par \uc2  There are "lingering concerns about where we are geopolitically," weighing on traders' minds, and that's placing a dim setting behind the dollar, said Jason Bonanca,  \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst with Credit Suisse First Boston in  New York. \par \par \uc2  Another factor pushing the dollar lower, contributing to  nervousness surrounding the possibility of a U.S.-led war on Iraq, came from a report by the BBC that the U.S. has formally requested assistance from the North Atlantic Treaty  Organization if there is such a war to oust Saddam Hussein. \par \par \uc2  Although the dollar came sporadically under pressure against the euro and the Swiss franc, a classic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  of refuge during times of war, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy was uppermost in traders minds as they traded the yen. \par \par \uc2  With the greenback trading around a four-month low against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears on the rise, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's new \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  point man, Zembei Mizoguchi, reiterated that Tokyo is ready to take steps against excessive exchange rate moves. \par \par \uc2  "Markets react to various factors, and sometimes they go  too far. We're prepared to take action if necessary," the new Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs said. \par \par \uc2  Still, his remarks, combined with a similar message from   Japanese economy and banking minister Heizo Takenaka, only  moderately supported the dollar against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the course of the global session. \par \par \uc2  Thomas Molloy, a trader with Bank Leumi in New York, said he expects Japanese policy makers "are waiting for an opportunity when the market gets itself excessively short" of dollars and bets more strongly that the yen will appreciate. This would arguably give the Japanese authorities "more bang for their buck." Until the dollar were to slip below the 117-yen mark to around 116.85 yen, "I  would not be losing any sleep," Mr. Molloy added. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the Hungarian forint soared, at least partially defying intensive efforts by the Hungarian \b \highlight22\uc2 central   bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to check its ascent, by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and making a surprising 100 basis-point interest-rate cut. Driven higher by a strong bid for Hungarian debt, the euro was at 234.02 forint late Wednesday  in New York. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's action to defend the band in which the forint trades against the euro baffled some market participants and did little to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Venezuelan bolivar meanwhile moved firmly in the other direction, sliding about 6.1% to about 1716.25 bolivar  to the dollar Wednesday, from 1610.75 Tuesday. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2   dropped as low as 1750.23 during the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's second daily dollar auction, but strengthened after the institution  bought the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  with about $75 million more than its preset $30 million, traders said. Investors are fleeing to dollars as Venezuela's economy melts down due to an ongoing 45-day-old general strike against President Hugo Chavez's leadership. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -By John Parry; Dow Jones Newswires\par    john.parry@dowjones.com; 201-938-2096\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Copyright (c) 2002 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par All Rights Reserved.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 01-15-03 1725ET\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020030115dz1f009kj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
