\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Options:Volatilities Ease As Dlr Partially Recovers \b0\par\par\uc2 430 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Januar 2003\par 20:43\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Volatilities implied by one-month euro/dollar and dollar/yen \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  options were modestly lower midafternoon Wednesday in New York, compared with levels earlier Wednesday in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  Volatilities have eased following the euro's failed attempt to attain the $1.06 mark in the spot market earlier in the New York day, briefly reaching global session highs around $1.0592. That pop for the euro occurred amid a broad dollar sell-off on a raft of concerns surrounding U.S. companies' earnings and heightened tension over the diplomatic row with North Korea, with the threat of a potential war with Iraq also looming on the horizon. \par \par \uc2  By midafternoon Wednesday in New York, the dollar had recovered somewhat and volatilities eased a bit. \par \par \uc2  Nonetheless, if the euro should splinter above $1.06, that would send volatilities higher, while any retreat of the dollar against the yen back to an area between Y117.50 and Y117.80 would be perceived to heighten the risk of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  via the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  in an effort to weaken the yen, said an options salesperson at a bank in New York. \par \par \uc2  Midafternoon Wednesday in New York, the dollar was trading at Y118.00, well off its session lows but down from Y118.06 late Tuesday. The euro was at $1.0555, up modestly from $1.0550 late Tuesday. Against the Swiss franc, the dollar had declined to CHF1.3844, from CHF1.3858 late Tuesday. Sterling was at $1.6011, down from $1.6048. \par \par \uc2  In the over-the-counter market, one-month at-the-money dollar/yen options implied volatility of 10.4%-10.7%, down from 10.5%-11.0% earlier in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  One-month euro/dollar option volatilities were at 9.4%-9.8%, down from 9.60%-10.1% earlier in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  Volatility measures the expected range in the value of an asset over the period of the contract. \par \par \uc2  Volatilities on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  options gauge the anticipated moves in the underlying \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with a higher volatility implying a wider trading range. \par \par \uc2  One-month risk-reversals on dollar/yen favored dollar puts at 0.8%-1.2%, compared with 0.9%-1.3% earlier in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  Risk-reversals measure the market preference for either put or call options by the premium or discount applied. \par \par \uc2  Euro calls were favored over the dollar at 0.7%-1.1% Wednesday afternoon in New York. \par \par \uc2  -By John Parry; Dow Jones Newswires; john.parry@dowjones.com; 201-938-2096 \par \par \uc2  1077 4127 44427 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030115dz1f00183\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
