\par\par \b \uc2  Dow Jones Asia-Pacific Top Stories Sked For Jan. 15 \b0\par\par\uc2 2,071 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Januar 2003\par 10:04\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Following are the top Asia-Pacific stories featured on Dow Jones Newswires as of 0900 GMT, along with items expected later in the day. Please direct any questions or comments to asianewseds@dowjones.com or by phone on 65-6415-4020. \par \par \uc2  MACRO/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /FIXED INCOME \par \par \uc2  TOKYO: Moody's Investors Service said Wednesday it is unlikely to retain its A2 domestic debt rating on Japan in the long term if the country's policy framework isn't changed. But Moody's won't likely reassess the rating until 2004, said Thomas Byrne, the rating agency's lead sovereign analyst for Japan. ("=INTERVIEW: Moody's Byrne" first take ran at 0613 GMT) \par \par \uc2  SEOUL: Standard & Poor's currently holds no bias toward an upgrade or downgrade to South Korea's A- sovereign rating, a senior official from the credit rating agency told Dow Jones Wednesday. ("S&P Official Confirms No Bias Toward S Korea Rating Move" ran at 0146 GMT; note "CORRECT:S&P/Korea Rtg:Rating Hike Possible, Not Rate Hike" at 0420 GMT) \par \par \uc2  MANILA: The Philippine government is considering issuing another tranche of dollar-linked peso notes this year, following strong response for the three-year dollar-linked peso notes it priced Wednesday. ("=WRAP: Philippines May Issue New Dlr-Linked Notes Later 03" ran at 0836 GMT) \par \par \uc2  BANGKOK: Thailand's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to leave its policy-signal interest rate unchanged at Monday's monetary policy committee meeting, maintain an easing bias in view of global uncertainties and continue buying dollars on weakness, economists said. ("=PREVIEW:Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  To Keep Policy Rate, Bias Unch" ran at 0554 GMT) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO: With the dollar trading around a four-month low against the yen and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears on the rise, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's new \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  point man, Zembei Mizoguchi, reiterated Wednesday that Tokyo is ready to take steps against excessive exchange rate moves.("Mizoguchi/Kuroda" first take ran at 0855 GMT; see also "Japan MOF Mizoguchi" that moved at 0026 GMT) \par \par \uc2  =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: Call it the calm before the storm. After the dollar's overnight fall to a four-month low against the yen of Y117.55, Tokyo market participants braced themselves for the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's response as Mizoguchi starts his first full day as the MOF's top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  official. But the dollar's subsequent recovery back above Y118 has made the MOF much less likely to act for now. ("=\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Asia: Wary Market Ponders When Japan MOF Would Act" ran at 0640 GMT) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO: The Bank of Japan says it held shares worth Y244.5 billion as of Jan. 10, up from Y150.1 billion Dec. 31, indicating it bought Y94.4 billion worth of shares from commercial banks in the 10-day period in an effort to help shield banks' balance sheets from stock price falls. ("BOJ Share Buys" moved at 0123 GMT) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO: Japan's current account surplus rose 9% on year in November to Y1.168 trillion, MOF says, a slightly narrower surplus than expected, feeding concerns Japan's economy is getting less support from export markets. ("Japan November Curr Acct" first take ran at 0007 GMT) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO: The net fund outflow from Japan through securities investment soared in December as Japanese investors poured money into safe-haven bond investments overseas amid intensifying geopolitical concerns. A net Y1.876 trillion of funds was shifted out of Japan in December, well up from the Y33.8 billion outflow in November,MOF's preliminary data showed.("Japan/Portfolio" first take ran at 0024 GMT) \par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI: High-level government approval will now be required before Chinese entities can borrow money overseas, according to rules unveiled Wednesday that aim to clarify who has the right to borrow while releasing the central government from repayment obligations. ("China/Approval/Foreign Borrowing" first take ran at 0236 GMT) \par \par \uc2  =\b \highlight22\uc2 CENTRAL BANK\b0 \highlight\uc2  WATCH: The Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's largely hands-off policy on the exchange rate may come under severe test this year should the peso face extreme stress from persistent concerns including a burgeoning fiscal deficit. ("=\b \highlight22\uc2 CENTRAL BANK\b0 \highlight\uc2  WATCH: Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Policy To Face Test" ran at 0443 GMT) \par \par \uc2  =AUSTRALIA WATCH: Over the past four years selling Australian dollar rallies has ultimately proved rewarding, but those trying this tactic now may find standing contrary to market sentiment too painful. ("=AUSTRALIA WATCH: AUD Contrarians May Get A Hiding" ran at 0311 GMT) \par \par \uc2  SEOUL: South Korean Minister of Finance and Economy Jeon Yun-churl said Wednesday that the Bank of Korea's growth forecast of 6.2% for 2002 will likely be achieved, but repeated that economic growth will slow to a 5% range this year. ("S Korea Min/GDP" first take ran at 2353 GMT) \par \par \uc2  SEOUL: The \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market participatns should not "underestimate the government's will to stabilize the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market," Kwon Tae-shin, director general of the international finance bureau, told Dow Jones. ("Korea Fin Ministry/\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 " first take ran at 0306 GMT) \par \par \uc2  WELLINGTON: New Zealand recorded a November trade deficit of NZ$434 million, slightly wider than the earlier estimate of NZ$418 million, as exports continued to fall in the latest month, data published Wednesday show. ("DATA SNAP: NZ's 12-Month Trade Deficit Revised Wider" ran at 2207 GMT) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE: Singapore's retail sales index rose 2.2% on year in November, thanks to continued higher sales in some key components of the index, the Department of Statistics said Wednesday. The index rose 7.3% from October, its second straight monthly increase following two consecutive months of declines. ("Singapore Nov/Retail Sales" first take ran at 0518 GMT) \par \par \uc2  KUALA LUMPUR: Malaysian toll highway operator Expressway Lingkaran Tengah Sdn. Bhd., or Elite, Wednesday said it will issue 800 million ringgit in Islamic bonds to refinance existing borrowings, resulting in savings of MYR22 million per year. ("Malaysia's Elite/Bond" ran at 0202 GMT) \par \par \uc2  =\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  MARKET ROUNDUP: Fears that Japanese authorities would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to boost the dollar dominated Asian trading Wednesday, keeping the dollar well supported against the yen. ("Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 " moved at 0530 GMT; Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  comment usually runs around 1000 GMT) \par \par \uc2  EQUITIES \par \par \uc2  TOKYO: Seibu Department Stores and its main lender Mizuho Corporate Bank Ltd. may soon test the Japanese government's will to clear up the nation's huge bad loan mess. Seibu will likely be the first company to apply for restructuring under the Industrial Revitalization Corp., a government body expected to be formally launched this spring, the Nihon Keizai Shimbun reported Wednesday. But market experts here say that if the IRC - already being called the "zombie hospital" - were to help the ailing retailer and its main lenders, foreign investors may think the government is backtracking on reform and non-performing-loan cleanup efforts. (story should run soon) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO: In the latest response to government pressure on banks to speed up bad loan write-offs, Sumitomo Mitsui Banking Corp. aims to raise around Y150 billion by issuing preferred shares to U.S. investment bank Goldman Sachs Group. The core unit of Sumitomo Mitsui Financial Group hopes the move will help bolster investor confidence in its prospects, especially as worries resurface about a potential financial crisis in the run-up to the March fiscal year end. (Wrap expected around 1000 GMT) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO: Ailing Japanese supermarket operator said Wednesday it will sell four group-operated hotels to an affiliate of Goldman Sachs Group for a total of Y45.4 billion to help reduce its huge interest-bearing debt burden. ("Daiei/Hotels/Goldman Sachs" ran at 0747 GMT) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO: Japan Airlines System will raise its domestic fares from April to make up for an expected Y4-billion increase in annual airport-use fees, a company official said Wednesday. The move follows a similar price hike by its rival All Nippon Airways. ("JAL To Raise Domestic Fares From April; Details Not Set" ran at 0257 GMT) \par \par \uc2  WELLINGTON: A dose of positive news for New Zealand's Fletcher Challenge Forests Ltd. has turned somewhat sour after the stock exchange asked the securities regulator to probe possible insider trading activity. The blue chip's shares rose sharply one day before the announcement of a NZ$140 million capital return and an agreement to sell forest cutting rights for US$65 million. ("=WRAP: Watchdog Begins Probe As NZ Fletcher Shares Soar" ran at 0010 GMT) \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG: Interview with Intel Vice President Of Sales Jason Chun following higher-than-expected fourth-quarter results announced overnight; likely to talk about where Asia fits into the strategy. (story likely around 0930 GMT) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO: Oracle Corp. Japan suffered steep declines in its parent revenue and profits for the six months ended Nov. 30, due to sluggish demand for its mainline database management software products amid a downturn in corporate spending in Japan. The Tokyo subsidiary of U.S software giant Oracle Corp. said its parent pretax profit plunged 36.1% in the first half from a year ago. ("Oracle Japan" first take ran at 0619 GMT) \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG: Dah Sing Financial's financial controller Gary Wang tells Dow Jones that Abbey National's move to sell its 5.5% stake will likely benefit the share price if the sale boosts the Hong Kong bank's public float to about 47% of total shares. (story expected by 1030 GMT) \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG: Hong Kong Exchanges & Clearing Wednesday scrapped the 0.25% minimum commission for trading equities, which had been scheduled to be removed from April 1, saying the move is "in the best interest of the public." This will leave commissions to be set freely between brokers and their clients. ("HK Exchanges/ Commissions" first take ran at 0357 GMT) \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG: The government's effort to retreat from the property market and to divest its HK$30 billion commercial portfolio is likely to become the first step in establishing the city's real estate investment trust market. (story expected to run around 1000 GMT) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE: Singapore Airlines' December load factors due out Wednesday afternoon, and the market will be waiting to see if the figures will show growth for 12 months running. Load factors rose 2.3 percentage points in November 2002. (story expected around 0900 GMT) \par \par \uc2  =ASIAN STOCK FOCUS: High oil prices and an expected surge in production in the years ahead should continue to pump up the shares of CNOOC Ltd., China's dominant offshore oil and gas producer. ("=Asian Stock Focus: CNOOC Still A Buy After Stellar Year" ran at 0005 GMT) \par \par \uc2  SEOUL: South Korea's LG Industrial Systems swung to a net profit of 4.1 billion won for 2002 from a loss of KRW16.3 billion in the previous year, as it slashed debt and interest expenses. ("Korea LG Indus Earnings" first take ran at 0248 GMT) \par \par \uc2  SEOUL: Woori Bank's 2002 net profit surged 78% to 1.266 trillion won, helped by non-operating income from the sale of its credit card \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  to parent Woori Finance Holdings. ("S Korea/Woori Bk/Earnings" first take ran at 0446 GMT) \par \par \uc2  MUMBAI: Deutsche Asset Management, which launched mutual fund \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in India this week, says the country's regulatory environment is stifling growth in areas that could bring the most attractive returns to investors. ("=INTERVIEW: Rules Hamper Investment Potential In India" ran at 0557 GMT) \par \par \uc2  ADELAIDE: An Australian information technology company, GBST Holdings Pty. Ltd., said it's considering challenging Iress Market Technology Ltd.'s monopoly position in the area of share market information. ("=INTERVIEW/GBST/Iress" first take ran at 0409 GMT) \par \par \uc2  KUALA LUMPUR: Malaysian Resources Corp. Bhd. scheduled to hold 0800 GMT briefing; MRCB may clarify whether or not it's bidding for a stake in property company UDA Holdings, after UDA denied this late Tuesday. (story likely to run after 0900 GMT) \par \par \uc2  ENERGY/COMMODITIES \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE: Sumitomo Chemical and Royal Dutch/Shell group are considering setting up an ethylene plant in Singapore with capacity of one million tons a year. The companies are targeting 2007 for the start of \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with the plant serving as a supply center of petrochemical products for Asia. (Wrap likely to run after 0900 GMT) \par \par \uc2  =CHINA ENERGY WATCH: China is dusting off plans, once rejected as impractical, to import crude oil from Kazakhstan via a 4,300-kilometer pipeline crossing some of the world's most inaccessible and rugged terrain. ("=CHINA ENERGY WATCH: Oil Hunt Goes On - The Kazakh Option" ran at 0757 GMT) \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030115dz1f000kd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
