\par\par \b \uc2  Higher  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Buys Of US Assets Herald Weaker Dlr \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tyler Lifton \par \uc2 961 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Januar 2003\par 21:52\par \uc2  Dow Jones Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Central banks, those large, ultraconservative investors charged with steering their nations' monetary policies, are buying more U.S. securities. \par \par \uc2  On the surface, this appears to be a good sign for the greenback, but may actually be the opposite. \par \par \uc2  According to analysts at UBS Warburg, increasing purchases of U.S. assets by foreign central banks - as captured in the weekly custodial holdings of the Federal Reserve Bank of New York - has historically been an indication of dollar weakness. \par \par \uc2  That's because those banks are buying dollars in exchange for their respective \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from gaining too much on the greenback, a situation that would damp the export capability of foreign countries. \par \par \uc2  "Central banks tend to counter trends in global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets more actively than is realized," according  to the UBS Warburg report, which was authored by Mansoor Mohi-uddin, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist in London, and Christian Bruner, a member of the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  quantitative advisory group. \par \par \uc2  "Thus when the dollar has been strong in the past, central banks have sold the greenback to stop their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from depreciating too quickly and when the dollar  has been weak central banks have increased their purchases of U.S. assets," they wrote. \par \par \uc2  The recent growth in foreign custody holding at the New York Fed has been significant. For the week ending January 8, the New York Fed held $861.1 billion of marketable U.S. securities for foreign central banks. That's up from $729.2 billion the year prior, or 18.1%. \par \par \uc2  And the buying parade by central banks has even picked up steam in recent weeks, when anecdotal evidence has indicated that private investors have begun to pull back from U.S. stocks and bonds, a trend that has resulted in the  dollar's unusually large descent in January. \par \par \uc2  Proprietary portfolio flow data from the Bank of New York, among the world's largest custodial banks, show net outflow from U.S. fixed-income securities and inflow into euro-zone bonds since mid-November, said Michael Woolfolk, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at the bank. Reflecting this trend, the euro closed at $1.0095 on November 15, but is currently trading up around $1.0557. \par \par \uc2  U.S. government data from the Treasury Department is released on a two month lag. \par \par \uc2  But while foreign private investors appear to have been selling U.S. bonds during the holiday season, central banks were buying. For the week ending November 13, the New York Fed held $809.9 billion in U.S. assets for foreign central banks, meaning they have increased their holdings by $51.1 billion in less than two months. \par \par \uc2  Central banks, particularly in Asia, are "Quite concerned about the weakness of the dollar," Woolfolk said. \par \par \uc2  One strategy they can employ to "cushion the dollar's decline is by going into the market and buying U.S. Treasurys," he said. \par \par \uc2  Of the $861.1 billion in foreign custody holding at the New York Fed, $694 billion is in Treasurys and $167.1 billion is in agency securities. \par \par \uc2  Still, even the recent uptick in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  holdings at the New York Fed may not be enough to save the dollar from the further declines it is expected to suffer. \par \par \uc2  "If the U.S. is running a current account deficit close  to $40 billion a month and private sector capital inflows dry up, then even if central banks step up their purchases of U.S. assets, until such purchases reach $10 billion a week, the dollar will in theory continue to fall," the UBS Warburg analysts wrote. \par \par \uc2  Indeed, the increase in \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buying comes at a time when the U.S. is finding it increasingly difficult to attract portfolio flows sufficient to fund its growing current account deficit, which currently checks in at some 5% of gross domestic product. \par \par \uc2  With foreign direct investment drying up, enough portfolio flows arrived on U.S. shores in 2002 to keep the dollar from falling even more than it did. But given the greenback's steep descent at the end of the year - and its inability to back off of multiyear lows against the euro, the Swiss franc and the pound early in 2003 - that may no longer be the case. \par \par \uc2  And that means that central banks may likely keep on buying U.S. assets, even if it's in vain. \par \par \uc2  The rising trend of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buying of U.S. securities "suggests that central banks are continuing to see strong outflows from the U.s. and that the dollar remains on its weakening trend," the UBS Warburg report states. But at this stage their purchasing of U.S. assets "still looks to be insufficient to cause the dollar to reverse trend across the board." \par \par \uc2  Given the enormous size of the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by a given \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is becoming increasingly ineffective, said Bank of New York's Woolfolk. \par \par \uc2  Take the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , for instance, which has repeatedly threatened \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent months without actually pulling the trigger. It has not done so in part because it is broad dollar weakness that has defined the exchange rate between the dollar and the yen. As such, the BOJ is less capable of influencing that exchange rate over the longer term via \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  That may lead to more custodial holdings at the New York  Fed. \par \par \uc2  "Alternatively what (the BOJ) could do is to fight the natural real money flows that the current account surplus (in Japan) generates" with counterbalancing portfolio flows  into U.S. securities, Woolfolk said. \par \par \uc2  -By Tyler Lifton, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-2248 \par \par \uc2  -tyler.lifton@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1074 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020030115dz1f00b41\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
