\par\par \b \uc2  No hiding place for Japan's new  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  guru. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Shinichi Kishima \par \uc2 623 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Januar 2003\par 11:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Jan 15 (Reuters) - Changing a long habit, Japan's new top financial diplomat Zembei Mizoguchi now comes to work through the main foyer of the Finance Ministry building instead of his usual side entrance. \par \par \uc2  It may not be long before \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders learn whether the change signals his intent to communicate more activity with the market - verbally or perhaps with billions of dollars of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to dampen the yen's sharp recent rise. \par \par \uc2  "We will show our stance by taking action (in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market) when it is really needed," Mizoguchi said at his inaugural news conference on Wednesday, flanked by his predecessor Haruhiko Kuroda. \par \par \uc2  The 57-year-old Mizoguchi assumed the position of vice finance minister for international affairs on Tuesday after serving as the head of the international bureau, treading the exact same path as Kuroda and his predecessor, Eisuke "Mr. Yen" Sakakibara. \par \par \uc2  Although the Bank of Japan actually makes the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  transactions in the market on the government's behalf, it is the Finance Ministry - and especially its top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  official - that sets exchange rate policy and decides whether to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Mizoguchi said he will maintain the policy stance of Kuroda, who oversaw a record amount of \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  during his 3-= year stint which spanned the euro's fall to record lows in 2000 and the September 11 attacks in 2001. \par \par \uc2  With the yen surging more than six percent to four-month highs against the dollar this week, threatening to undermine Japan's export-reliant economic recovery, Mizoguchi may soon be under pressure to back his words with action. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market since July, but the yen has steadily risen in recent weeks despite Japan's wobbly economy and interest rates at virtually zero. Government officials have made little secret of their displeasure. \par \par \uc2  "They (exchange rates) overshoot sometimes, and this could have undesirable effects," Mizoguchi said, citing the impact of a higher yen on Japanese business and consumer sentiment. \par \par \uc2  THROUGH FRONT ENTRANCE \par \par \uc2  Until last week, Mizoguchi's favourite path to his office was through the side entrance. \par \par \uc2  That did little to spare him from the daily gaggle of financial reporters seeking comments on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, though his chat time tended to be shorter than for Kuroda, who used the front entrance. \par \par \uc2  Mizoguchi's standard statements have been that exchange rates should reflect fundamentals, excessive swings are undesirable and that authorities will take appropriate action if necessary. \par \par \uc2  "I do feel sorry about repeating the same comments every time, but our policy is so simple," Mizoguchi said with a grin. \par \par \uc2  Mizoguchi will accompany Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa to a Paris meeting of finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chiefs from the Group of Seven at the end of the month. \par \par \uc2  He said he expected neither the U.S. monetary authorities, under new Treasury Secretary John Snow, nor European authorities to change their stance on global policy coordination. \par \par \uc2  Mizoguchi said the G7 has had a longstanding agreement to cooperate on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates when necessary and that a member country could also act unilaterally if it thought that was appropriate. \par \par \uc2  Most \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders expect any imminent yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be a solo effort by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , even if Washington insisted it was sticking to its "strong dollar" policy. \par \par \uc2  Four out of five of Mizoguchi's predecessors gave the green light for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  within a month of their appointment, according to Yasunari Ueno, chief market economist at Mizuho Securities. \par \par \uc2  Oddly enough, Sakakibara was the only exception despite his market-mover reputation. He won his moniker after engineering the dollar's reversal from historic lows around 80 yen soon after becoming head of the international bureau in 1995. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030115dz1f00lx3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
