\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-Mizoguchi says  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  policy same as Kuroda's. \b0\par\par\uc2 872 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Januar 2003\par 10:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Jan 15 (Reuters) - Japan's newly appointed vice finance minister for international affairs, Zembei Mizoguchi, said on Wednesday he intended to adhere to the policy on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates followed by his predecessor, Haruhiko Kuroda. \par \par \uc2  "I worked together with Mr Kuroda as head of a bureau that was in charge of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Therefore I stand by the same policy," he told a news conference also attended by Kuroda, who is stepping down as Japan's top financial diplomat. \par \par \uc2  Mizoguchi restated the ministry line that it would take appropriate action on excessive movements in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates. \par \par \uc2  "Excessive movements have an adverse impact on the economy as they affect corporate and consumer confidence," he said. \par \par \uc2  "Excessive movements must be avoided. Our view is unchanged that we will closely monitor the markets and take appropriate action against excessive moves," he said. \par \par \uc2  But Mizoguchi added that Japanese authorities were not seeking to move the market through verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "We will show our stance by taking action (in the market) when needed. We do not intend to move the markets through words," he said, adding that there was some misunderstanding about this. \par \par \uc2  Mizoguchi said it was not that officials wanted to speak about exchange rates. It was just that they kept getting asked about them by reporters and they responded as there was strong interest in Japan about \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates and policy. \par \par \uc2  Kuroda added: "I felt it would be rude not to address the reporters who were waiting for me," referring to the ritual of reporters waiting at the MOF's entrance to seek comments. \par \par \uc2  "It is not like I wanted to talk about our policy every week, as obviously it would not change. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  actively under Kuroda's regime. Most recently, it spent some four trillion yen ($34 billion) on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  during April-June 2002, the largest amount spent in any quarter since the MOF started disclosing such data in 1991. \par \par \uc2  DOLLAR AT FOUR MONTH LOW \par \par \uc2  Tokyo last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on June 28, 2002, when it enlisted the help of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the New York Federal Reserve to sell yen on its behalf. \par \par \uc2  In late Asian trading on Wednesday, the dollar was hovering around 118.15 yen after hitting a four-month low of 117.55 yen in post-Asian trading on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Mizoguchi said he expected the market to reflect the relative strength of U.S. economic fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  "When we look at the relative strength of the major economies - Japan, United States and the euro zone - the U.S. economy has overwhelming strength," he said. \par \par \uc2  "Recent economic indicators show some divergence, but when we look at the underlying movements, the United States is strong and \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  should reflect that over the medium term." \par \par \uc2  He also referred to an agreement within the Group of Seven industrial powers that they would cooperate if necessary and that a member could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on its own if it felt it was needed. \par \par \uc2  Asked about views that U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy may change under Treasury Secretary designate John Snow, Mizoguchi said he did not see any change. \par \par \uc2  "I don't see any change based on what has been released and on comments," he said. \par \par \uc2  "The G7 has a long history of discussing \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. Fundamentally, I don't expect the thinking (on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy) of the United States and Europe will change." \par \par \uc2  Asked about an upcoming meeting of G7 finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors to be held in Paris, Mizoguchi said he expected the most important topic would be how the various nations can overcome slowdowns in their economies. \par \par \uc2  He also said the G7 would closely watch for potential financial instability in emerging economies, such as South America and Turkey. \par \par \uc2  Mizoguchi will accompany Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa to the G7 meeting to be held on January 31 and February 1. \par \par \uc2  BOJ EASING \par \par \uc2  Mizoguchi also repeated the ministry's stance that it wanted the Bank of Japan to consider additional monetary easing measures to tackle deflation, including adoption of an inflation target. \par \par \uc2  "The government and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will work as one to overcome deflation. Fiscal policy and monetary policy will work together," he said. \par \par \uc2  "It is important to pursue measures, including the option of an inflation target, that will promote effective monetary easing," Mizoguchi said. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ has been flooding the money market with liquidity, pushing short-term interest rates to virtually zero, but that has had little effect in stemming a persistent fall in prices. \par \par \uc2  As such, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is coming under increasing pressure from the government to take more radical measures. \par \par \uc2  Economics and Financial Services Minister Heizo Takenaka has actively called for the BOJ to adopt an inflation target. \par \par \uc2  Under such a regime, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would set a target for inflation over a certain period and then adopt radical policies - such as buying stocks or property - to achieve it. \par \par \uc2  But BOJ Governor Masaru Hayami has said adopting an inflation target under current conditions would be "a reckless gamble" as there were no surefire means to achieve such a target when prices were falling, and that the government must also take measures to bolster the economy. ($1=118.15 yen). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030115dz1f00jrc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
