\par\par \b \uc2  Dow Jones Asia-Pacific Top Stories Sked For Jan. 9 \b0\par\par\uc2 2,104 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Januar 2003\par 10:07\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Following are the top Asia-Pacific stories featured on Dow Jones Newswires as of 0900 GMT, along with items expected later in the day. Please direct any questions or comments to asianewseds@dowjones.com or by phone on 65-6415-4020. \par \par \uc2  MACRO/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /FIXED INCOME \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE: Singapore's economic prospects are expected to brighten in the latter half of the year, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  warned Thursday that the economy will remain vulnerable to global uncertainties as long as growth is dependent on traditional sources. (Wrap should run soon) \par \par \uc2  =ASIA DEBT: It's been a somewhat nerve-racking start to the new year for Asia's primary debt market. Just as bankers were about to breathe a sigh of relief after the Philippines' global bond offering survived through the brouhaha surrounding its current account data, Thailand said it will likely drop its plan to issue up to $1 billion in 10-year global bonds. (Asia Debt will run around 1000 GMT) \par \par \uc2  SEOUL: The Bank of Korea Thursday decided to keep the overnight call rate target unchanged for the eighth month in a row at 4.25%. The decision was widely anticipated as South Korea's economy is sending mixed signals with exports remaining strong but growth slowing for domestic consumption. (Wrap expected to move around 1000 GMT) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO: \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Kuroda flexes his muscles again, warning that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is prepared to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets if necessary to curb yen strength after the dollar fell sharply overnight. ("Japan MOF Kuroda" first take ran at 0144 GMT) \par \par \uc2  BEIJING: China's new leadership aims to boost farm incomes for the 800 million people still relying on agriculture for a living by improving financing and guaranteeing long-term land use rights. ("China Adopts Four-Point Proposal To Boost Farm Incomes" ran at 0219 GMT) \par \par \uc2  =BOJ WATCH: If you think the Bank of Japan will aggressively ease monetary policy to offset the deflationary impact of a government plan to clean up banks' balance sheets, you might want to reconsider. Sources familiar with \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  thinking say the BOJ views the disposal of bad loans as Japan's most significant domestic economic issue but doesn't expect the impact of the disposal process will be severe. ("=BOJ Watch:NPL Cleanup Push May Not Spur Policy Offensive" ran at 0826 GMT) \par \par \uc2  =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: Once again, the dollar has begun the new year by dipping against most Asian and European units, as bearish sentiment seeps into the market. ("=\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Asia: It's Trapping Season For Dlr Bears - 4CastWeb" ran at 0236 GMT) \par \par \uc2  KUALA LUMPUR: After more than a year, Malaysia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be ready to cut its policy interest rate as soon as this quarter as it anticipates gloomier days ahead. ("=Malaysia May Cut \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Rate Soon - Analysts" ran at 0342 GMT) \par \par \uc2  =ASIA DEBT: Australian dollar interest rate swaps have staged a robust rally in recent months, taking the long end of the curve to its lowest levels in more than six years. ("=ASIA DEBT: Australian IRS Offer Trading Opportunities" ran at 0554 GMT) \par \par \uc2  SEOUL: The International Monetary Fund Thursday noted the rapid expansion in the South Korean financial sector's "off-balance sheet activities," and warned that it carried risks. In a statement, the IMF said: "The recent growth in off-balance sheet activities, particularly in the derivatives market, is usually consistent with financial market development, but carries potentially important risks." ("IMF/Korea Derivative Mkt" first take ran at 0135 GMT) \par \par \uc2  SEOUL: The Bank of Korea said Thursday it is keeping its core inflation target at 2%-4% for 2003, but raised its medium-term target to 2.5%-3.5% from the 2.5% set last year. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said domestic and external uncertainties have weakened financial market sentiment, but added that the country's economic upswing continued to be steady. ("Bk of Korea/Inflation" first take ran at 0312 GMT) \par \par \uc2  BANGKOK: Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Pridiyathorn Devakula said Thursday that donors to an expired International Monetary Fund rescue program have agreed in principle to a plan to pay off an outstanding balance of $4.8 billion by July. ("=WRAP: Thai IMF Program Donors Agree To Early Repayment" ran at 0654 GMT) \par \par \uc2  JAKARTA: The Indonesian government will cut value-added tax on 20 items to mitigate the impact of the recent hikes in domestic fuel prices and utility rates. ("Indonesia To Cut Value-Added Tax On Select Items" ran at 0224 GMT) \par \par \uc2  TAIPEI: Taiwan's High Tech Computer Corp., which makes Smartphone for Europe's Orange and the iPaq for H-P, plans to sell up to US$70 million in 5-year, zero coupon overseas convertible bonds, according to application terms filed with market regulators. HTC plans to sell the bonds possibly in the first quarter, a source familiar with the deal said. ("Taiwan High Tech Plans 5-Yr Overseas Conv Bond 1Q -Source" ran at 0752 GMT) \par \par \uc2  =\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  MARKET ROUNDUP: The dollar fell slightly against the yen in Asia Thursday as Japanese institutional investors continued repatriating yen for window-dressing purposes before Japan's fiscal year end in March. ("Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 " moved at 0536 GMT; Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  comment usually runs around 1000 GMT) \par \par \uc2  EQUITIES \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE: The battle for control of NatSteel Ltd. will go to the wire with no clear indications whether hotelier Ong Beng Seng will succeed in getting the 50% shares needed for the bid to succeed. What does the impasse mean for NatSteel? The deadline for NatSteel shareholders to accept the offer is Friday 0730 GMT. (story expected around 1100 GMT) \par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI: Shanghai Pudong Development Bank, the second-largest mainland-listed bank in terms of asset size, Thursday announced a higher-than-expected 21% rise in net profit for 2002 to 1.28 billion yuan, which analysts say may be due to lower provisions in the fourth quarter of that year. (Wrap should run soon) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO: Toshiba plans to begin this spring mass production of the world's smallest semiconductors with circuit widths of 90 nanometers, well ahead of its global rivals. Such semiconductors will double the level of integration of the latest system chips, enabling the size of such products as DVD players, game machines and servers to be halved and their operating speeds doubled. ("Toshiba To Mass-Produce World's Smallest Chips In Spring" ran at 0335 GMT) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO: The Financial Services Agency has warned Aozora Bank that it improperly released customer information to Cerberus Partners, its fourth-largest shareholder, Aozora President Hiroshi Maruyama said Thursday. While admitting improper decision-making among executives with regard to the customer data disclosed, Maruyama said his bank has already strengthened its internal controls to prevent any recurrence. ("Aozora Bank Pres" first take ran at 0507 GMT; see also "Cerberus/Aozora" first moved at 0505 GMT) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO: Japan's largest non-life insurance group Millea Holdings and unlisted Asahi Mutual Life Insurance said Thursday they are considering scrapping a plan to integrate \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2004, as weak market conditions cloud Asahi's transformation into a stock entity, a prerequisite for the integration. ("Millea Hldg, Asahi Mutual Mull Scrapping Integration Plan" ran at 0252 GMT) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE: DBS Group Holdings will make a final payment of HK$15.3 billion Friday for the purchase of Hong Kong's Dao Heng Bank Group, a spokesman said Thursday. The payment is for the final 28.4% stake in Dao Heng, Hong Kong's No. 6 lender by assets. ("=EXCLUSIVE: DBS/HK Dao Heng" first take ran at 0804 GMT) \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG: BT Group Asia-Pacific President Graham Moore denies - and qualifies - talk that the U.K. telecom giant is exiting the region, following moves to sell off stakes in local telecom firms as part of its debt-reduction program. ("=INTERVIEW: BT Sees Asia As Key; Will Sell Mobile Stakes" ran at 0838 GMT) \par \par \uc2  BEIJING: Shareholders will be able to claim losses incurred on their investments due to false, misleading and irregular financial statements by listed companies, China's Supreme People's Court announced Thursday. (Wrap expected around 0930 GMT) \par \par \uc2  BEIJING: Private equity firm Warburg Pincus has targeted China's looming new wave of private banks as one of the their investment focuses in the coming year, company executives said Thursday. (story likely around 0930 GMT) \par \par \uc2  WELLINGTON: The attempted takeover of New Zealand's largest meat exporter Richmond Ltd. hinges on the next move of a millionaire leather maker who has bought shares in the company over the past two months. ("=WRAP: Takeover Of NZ's Richmond Hangs In The Balance" ran at 0457 GMT) \par \par \uc2  =ASIA FUND VIEW: Japan should continue to be a magnet for U.S. and European hedge funds seeking Asian exposure. A huge, well-researched market combined with the freedom to short stocks - the favored strategy of hedge fund managers - make it the destination of choice in the region. Japan accounts for about two-thirds of the estimated US$16 billion invested in hedge funds in Asia. ("=Asia Fund View: Japan To Remain Asia's Hedge Fund Haven" ran at 0715 GMT) \par \par \uc2  MUMBAI: More business, more money - that is the simple reason why India's software companies are expected to post strong third-quarter results this month, analysts say. ("=More Business Means Good 3Q For India's Software Firms" ran at 0720 GMT; see also "India Mphasis BFL" that ran from 0600 GMT and "India Hughes Software/Earnings" at 0842 GMT) \par \par \uc2  SEOUL: Bancassurance, which will be permitted in South Korea starting August, poses a threat for smaller domestic insurers and may speed up restructuring in the insurance industry, experts say. ("=Bancassurance Poses Threat To Small S Korean Insurers" ran at 0810 GMT \par \par \uc2  MUMBAI: Leading Indian two-wheeler makers will report higher on-year earnings for the third quarter ended December on the back of strong sales, but analysts will be watching profit margins closely. (story likely to run after 0900 GMT) \par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI: Motorola unveiled the global portfolio of mobile phones this week in Shanghai it will be launching this year, which emphasize personalizing the phones with ringtones, wallpaper screens, and even programs. (story should run soon) \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG: Beijing Holdings Ltd., the parent company of Beijing Enterprises, expects the Chinese government to make a decision in the first half on where to build a Universal Studios theme park, an official from Beijing Holdings said Thursday. ("Beijing Hldg Expects Govt's Universal Studios Decision 1H" ran at 0446 GMT) \par \par \uc2  TAIPEI: Taiwan's Chailease Finance Co. bought a 70% stake in U.S. privately held GP Holdings for US$33.68 million and the acquisition should bring structured finance expertise to the local firm, Lawrence Liu, manager at Chailease said Thursday. ("Taiwan's Chailease/US GP Hldgs" first take ran at 0444 GMT) \par \par \uc2  SYDNEY: Australia's St Barbara Mines, a junior gold mining company, said Thursday it will merge with Toronto-listed Geomaque Explorations and fellow Australian miner Midas Gold. ("Australia's St Barbara Mines Merges With Geomaque, Midas" ran at 0144 GMT) \par \par \uc2  SEOUL: South Korea's KT Corp. will cut this year's capital spending to about 2.6 trillion won from the KRW3 trillion it spent last year, a company spokesman said Thursday. ("S Korea's KT Sees 03 Capex Cut By KRW400B To KRW2.6T" ran at 0212 GMT) \par \par \uc2  SEOUL: South Korea's Kia Motors hopes to boost its domestic market share to 30% in 2003, from 26.4% in 2002, the carmaker said Thursday. ("Korea Kia Motors Eyes Local Mkt Share At 30% '03 Vs 26.4%" ran at 0343 GMT) \par \par \uc2  SYDNEY: Australian food group Goodman Fielder has caught the attention of both the Australian Stock Exchange and the Australian Securities & Investments Commission, with its disclosure of a potential tax liability. ("Exchange, Regulator Look Into Goodman Fielder Disclosure" ran at 0748 GMT) \par \par \uc2  TAIPEI: Interview with top officials at Chinese Petroleum Corp., Taiwan's state-owned former oil monopoly, likely to touch on impact of international oil prices on business and government's privatization plan for the company. (Interview may run after 0900 GMT) \par \par \uc2  =ASIAN STOCK FOCUS: Elections and thirsty political gatherings are usually good news for liquor stocks. But maybe not for La Tondena Distillers this time around. ("=Asian Stock Focus: Little Kick From Polls For La Tondena" ran at 0334 GMT) \par \par \uc2  ENERGY/COMMODITIES \par \par \uc2  NEW DELHI: The Organization of Petroleum Exporting Countries' sole agenda item at its Jan. 12 meeting is how the group can stabilize the oil market and ensure stable supplies, OPEC President and Qatar Oil Minister Abdullah bin Hamad Al-Attiyah said Thursday. ("OPEC Pres: Stable Oil Market Needed, Speculators Active" ran at 0707 GMT) \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030109dz19000jr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
