\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1- \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  verbal  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  effect seen fading. \b0\par\par\uc2 427 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Januar 2003\par 04:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Jan 9 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , prompted by a strengthening yen, kept up its verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Thursday but the effect appeared to be wearing out as authorities are yet to follow up their threatening words with action. \par \par \uc2  Japanese authorities are worried that a strong yen could hurt Japan's frail export-led economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  But while they have repeatedly sounded off warnings, they have not taken action even as the dollar slid to a four-month low of 118.30 on December 30 on worries over possible war with Iraq. \par \par \uc2  "I think it's their strategy," said Toru Umemoto, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Morgan Stanley in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  "It may be that they want to control the market just by words because if actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  does not have (long-lasting) effect they are going to have to continue \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  Many in the market also say that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would have little lasting impact because recent movements in the market are more a reflection of weak sentiment for the dollar than a vote of confidence in the Japanese economy. \par \par \uc2  Japan has kept out of the market since June 28, when the yen fell below 120 to the dollar, even though the dollar dropped as low as 115.50 yen on July 16. \par \par \uc2  As of 0305 GMT, the dollar was trading around 119.02 yen  after falling to 118.70 yen earlier in the session. It fell to a low of 118.65 in New York on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  On Thursday, top financial diplomat Haruhiko Kuroda said again that the government was ready to act as needed. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think it's an appropriate direction, considering the economic situation," Kuroda, vice finance minister for international affairs, told reporters when asked for his view of the yen's rise. \par \par \uc2  "We are now carefully watching and analysing the moves. We are prepared to respond as needed," he said. \par \par \uc2  But some traders said that if \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  actually did \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it would catch the market by surprise, thus having a greater-than-usual impact. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  is becoming like the boy who cried wolf," said a U.S. bank trader. "I think it will surprise everyone if the wolf actually came." \par \par \uc2  The dollar began 2002 well above 130 yen and rose has high as 135.20 on January 31. \par \par \uc2  Since then, concerns about the outlook for the U.S. economy, as well as worries about war with Iraq and rising tensions over North Korea, have steadily worn down the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030109dz19006y4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
