\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar softer, steadies on yen after Kuroda. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Chisa Fujioka \par \uc2 541 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Januar 2003\par 04:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Jan 9 (Reuters) - The dollar slipped against most \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday due to tensions over Iraq and North Korea, but it steadied on the yen after verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from a top Japanese Finance Ministry official. \par \par \uc2  Suffering broad-based selling in early trade, the dollar clawed back to 119.04/09 yen by midday after an early low of 118.70 yen and a level of 119.06 in late New York. \par \par \uc2  "It seems speculators were out to test the Japanese authorities, but the threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was very alive on the move lower and lots of talk of stops around 118.50," said Hiroyuki Watanabe, manager of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Shinsei Bank. \par \par \uc2  Traders in Tokyo were eyeing 118.30 yen - the four-month low seen on December 30 - as a possible level at which authorities could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen, a tool \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has used on and off to help its exporters during times of slow growth. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in June last year. \par \par \uc2  Haruhiko Kuroda, vice finance minister for international affairs, rattled the yen in early trade, saying the government was ready to act as needed on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think it (the yen's recent rise) is an appropriate direction, considering the economic situation," he told reporters. \par \par \uc2  "We are now carefully watching and analysing the moves. We are prepared to respond as needed." \par \par \uc2  PRESSURE ON DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  The euro pushed to $1.0513/16 from $1.0495, not far from its three-year high of $1.0535 overnight. \par \par \uc2  Traders said mounting geopolitical worries kept pressure on the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with the dollar's trade-weighted index having hit a fresh three-year low on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Safe-havens Swiss franc and gold have scored hefty gains in recent sessions due to the nuclear standoff with North Korea and fears of war in Iraq. \par \par \uc2  Some analysts expected the dollar to draw support in the longer-term, given the chances are good that the U.S. economy will stage firm growth later this year. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar's losses should be quite limited going forward," said Daisuke Uno, chief market analyst at Sumitomo Mitsui Banking Corp. \par \par \uc2  "The market can't go on ignoring the fact that U.S. fiscal policy will work its way through the economy over time," he said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar did gain earlier in the week as stocks rose on President George W. Bush's proposed $674 billion economic package, which includes eliminating taxes on dividends. \par \par \uc2  But Wall Street has pared gains since, with a slip on Wednesday coming on concerns over a string of fourth-quarter earnings due next week. \par \par \uc2  "We are going to need much stronger figures on U.S. corporate earnings and employment for investment flows to take on a new direction," said Watanabe at Shinsei Bank. \par \par \uc2  The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meets later in the day, but the market widely expects no change in interest rates after a 50 basis point cut in December. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par  All \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  news ...        Debt...      Indicators...\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reports..\par  \par \f2