\par\par \b \uc2  GLOBAL YIELD: Bush Tax Plan Music To Fans Of Tsy Retreat \b0\par\par\uc2  By John Parry \par \uc2 965 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Januar 2003\par 20:48\par \uc2  Dow Jones Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- The fiscal chickens are coming  home to roost in the Treasury bond market, vindicating a growing number of global bond fund managers who have positioned to garner returns from non-U.S. sovereign debt over the past year. \par \par \uc2  After President George W. Bush formally unveiled a $670 billion tax cut plan Tuesday, expectations of increased issuance are likely to put pressure on longer-dated Treasury bonds. \par \par \uc2  That development adds to an already compelling shopping list of reasons why global bond fund managers are trimming  Treasury allocations and looking more favorably on other major industrialized bond markets. \par \par \uc2  Germany, the centerpiece of Europe's economy, is barely chugging along, inflationary pressures in the euro-zone are likely to ebb and the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will come under pressure to continue cutting interest rates. All three factors are set to drive German bunds - the proxy for European government bonds -  to outperform U.S. Treasurys this year, strategists expect. \par \par \uc2  Based on expectations that the U.S. economy may accelerate in the second half, leaving any economic rebound in the euro-zone trailing by several months, "European bonds probably look better than Treasurys even without that fiscal stimulus package," says Jack McIntyre, global fixed-income analyst with BrandyWine Asset Management, Wilmington, Del. \par \par \uc2  Now, market expectations that Congress will ultimately give the nod to the lion's share of Bush's hefty proposal, add another incentive to view Treasurys in general and the benchmark 10-year note in particular in a more wary light, he adds. \par \par \uc2  The yield spread of 10-year German bunds over U.S. Treasurys, which stood at 30 basis points Wednesday in New York, will squeeze shut and perhaps reverse over the  next few months as bunds rally, global bond strategists  expect. Bond prices and yields move inversely. \par \par \uc2  Another persuasive reason to bid up non-U.S. government bonds is the continuing decline of the dollar. That issue was underscored by the greenback's battering Wednesday, partly on the back of Bill Gross, managing director at PIMCO, formerly known as Pacific Investment Management Co., arguing in his January outlook piece for a weaker U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Newport Beach,  Calif.-based PIMCO is the world's largest bond fund. \par \par \uc2  After plumbing its biggest fall for 15 years in 2002, losing 15% against the euro and 10% against the yen, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists forecast the euro could climb to around $1.10 by the end of this year, from $1.0490 early Wednesday afternoon in New York. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Deficits Denting Dollar\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par By pressing for aggressive fiscal easing, the Bush administration\par risks inflaming the perception - already infecting the dollar on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign\par exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets - of a spiraling twin deficit conundrum. While the U.S.\par current  account deficit is running close to 5.0% and widening, the\par budget deficit may rise to around 2.5% of GDP, from some 1.5% last year,\par some analysts estimate.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The fiscal package, which is I suppose the `deficit  package' is a\par negative for the dollar," said Mary Ann Hurley, senior Treasurys trader\par in Seattle with  brokerage D.A Davidson. "Despite the amount it has lost\par to both the euro and the yen, the dollar probably has further to\par depreciate, especially versus the euro: European bonds remain very\par attractive," she added.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The yield spread between U.S. government bonds and comparable European\par sovereigns is still very wide in several maturities, making an even more\par compelling case to switch into bunds and other European debt, Hurley\par says.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Two-year German bunds, a maturity likely to get an immediate rally\par from further ECB rate cuts were trading at 2.68% Wednesday morning in\par New York, a wide 98 basis points above equivalent Treasurys. After\par stalling for 13  months, the ECB slashed its refi rate 50 basis points\par to 2.75% in December.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Against the yen, expectations for the dollar's performance are more\par variegated, with the possibility that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in an effort\par to weaken its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  hanging over traders' heads.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Still, disillusion surrounding the outlook for longer-dated Treasurys\par associated with the Bush tax plan  could also undercut the dollar's\par performance against the yen, some warn.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Bush's budget has not been well received by Japanese investors,"\par noted Marshall Gittler, a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2   strategist with Deutsche Bank in\par Tokyo in a research note. "They reason that he is trying to support the\par stock market at the expense of the bond market," with plans to scrap\par taxes on dividends, Gittler wrote. "Since Japanese overwhelmingly hold\par U.S. bonds, not stocks, they don't like the idea," he added.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par As of October, Japan held $359 billion worth of Treasury securities,\par according to Treasury Department data. The second biggest foreign\par investor, China, held $90 billion.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Japanese accounts may start to fret about a pessimistic scenario for\par the U.S., where far from revamping domestic  economic growth, the tax\par cuts might just force interest rates higher.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Still, cogent reasons for both groups of investors to remain loyal to\par the Treasury market remain which could at least mitigate any waning of\par the Asian attraction to the U.S. sovereign. Most notably, Japanese\par investors still enjoy a clearly advantageous "carry trade".\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The benchmark 10-year Japanese government bond was yielding a paltry\par 0.9% Wednesday, a whopping 307 basis-points below the equivalent\par Treasury. Japanese accounts commonly borrow in yen, where short term\par rates are effectively zero and invest in Treasurys in order to  collect\par the extra yield. This strategy - one of the few  winning trades for\par Japanese institutions, who face dismal investment returns in their\par domestic market - is unlikely to undergo a major shift anytime soon.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -By John Parry; Dow Jones Newswires\par    john.parry@dowjones.com; 201-938-2096\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  4083 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020030108dz18008c1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
