\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar takes breather, eyes Iraq, US jobs data. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Charlotte Cooper \par \uc2 596 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Januar 2003\par 12:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Jan 8 (Reuter) - The dollar paused a cent above last week's three-year low against the euro on Wednesday as the market hesitated ahead of key U.S. jobs data and awaited fresh developments regarding Iraq. \par \par \uc2  Initial enthusiasm ahead of a massive U.S. fiscal stimulus package unveiled on Tuesday to kickstart the economy faded, with analysts and traders doubting its effectiveness and querying its long term impact on the U.S. current account and budget deficit. "There are so many uncertainties out there. On the one hand you have this big package but on the other we still think the U.S. economy is in trouble," said Jesper Dannesboe, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Dresdner Kleinwort Wasserstein. \par \par \uc2  "The package is dollar-positive in itself but it's not enough to tip the balance in favour of the dollar on a longer term basis." \par \par \uc2  By 1100 GMT the dollar was trading around $1.0400 to the euro, slightly firmer from late New York levels. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen it was holding around 120.18, down from the previous session's 120.60 high but still two yen above last week's four-month low. \par \par \uc2  UNCERTAINTIES AHEAD \par \par \uc2  The dollar spun to multi-year lows against several other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the end of 2002 but traders say the market had become so short of dollars that the greenback was due a reprieve. It continued to inch off recent troughs against sterling and the Swiss franc. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said growing expectations of conflict between U.S.-led forces and Iraq in the first quarter of the year and a vigil ahead of Friday's U.S. employment report for December contributed to the market's reluctance to head in one direction. \par \par \uc2  The report is expected to show U.S. unemployment held steady at six percent. Jobs figures are closely watched as they are considered a good leading indicator of consumer demand. \par \par \uc2  "Non-farm payrolls are the next big move. The market is pretty much treading water in a typical new year start with no one wanting to commit themselves," said a senior trader at a UK bank. \par \par \uc2  U.S. equities' anaemic response to the Bush proposals also served to dampen sentiment. Major U.S. indices closed slightly lower on Tuesday having rallied more than two percent on Monday in anticipation of the package. \par \par \uc2  U.S. stock futures were indicating a weak start on Wall Street later. \par \par \uc2  The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Bank of England both announce interest rate decisions on Thursday but few in the market expect either to lower rates. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 JAPAN INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  THREATS \par \par \uc2  The threat of Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the yen's unwanted appreciation against the dollar was also helping to stabilise the greenback. \par \par \uc2  Japan's top financial diplomat, Haruhiko Kuroda, reiterated on Wednesday that Tokyo's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy had not changed, suggesting the country remained ready to step in to stem rapid \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves. \par \par \uc2  Kuroda's predecessor, Eisuke Sakakibara, said that Japanese authorities could step into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if the dollar falls below 120 yen. \par \par \uc2  "Solo \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is possible if the dollar falls below 120 yen," Sakakibara, once known as "Mr Yen" for his influence over \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and still regarded as influential in financial circles, told Reuters in an interview. \par \par \uc2  "In that case, the United States won't be against it," he said. \par \par \uc2  The comments followed remarks from Japan's Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa on Tuesday that the dollar at 125 yen would be a stable rate. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030108dz1800m8c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
