\par\par \b \uc2  Broad Dlr Drop Checks  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2 's  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Hand For Now \b0\par\par\uc2  By John Parry \par \uc2 1,042 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Januar 2003\par 21:04\par \uc2  Dow Jones Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's finger is on the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  trigger many analysts believe, but remarkably, there's been no opening salvo yet. \par \par \uc2  After the yen has firmed some 4% against the dollar over \par \par \uc2  the past month, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders have been on high alert,  waiting for Japan to act in an effort to weaken its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to potentially dramatic effect in thin, holiday season markets. \par \par \uc2  "You have to respect the fact that the Japanese don't really want the dollar below Y120.0 and so there is definite   risk" of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says Greg Anderson, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with ABN AMRO in Chicago. \par \par \uc2  Yet in spite of the dollar's slip into a `danger zone' against the yen where the Japanese government is increasingly likely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a bid to protect its fragile export market, the finance ministry has stayed its hand so far. \par \par \uc2  There are several plausible reasons for this. For one, the sheer breadth of negative dollar sentiment amid geopolitical concerns centering on a possible war with Iraq is just too powerful a tide for the Bank of Japan to turn back, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists reckon. The BOJ acts on the finance ministry's behalf in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Recent \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market moves reflect "dollar weakness rather than yen strength," said Alan Ruskin, research director with 4Cast financial consultancy in New York. The Japanese government may calculate that any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  now might have more bark than bite, with the effects soon evaporating, he added. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ even might be prepared to wait until the dollar fell to around Y115.0 before \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 , some strategists expect. Even then, if the yen doesn't show broader strength,  also gaining steeply against the euro, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might still refrain from acting. To force the BOJ's hand, the euro would have to slip to between Y120.0 and Y121.0 from Y124.68 early Monday afternoon in New York, Ruskin said. \par \par \uc2  At the same time, many economists believe Japanese exporters still have a competitive edge at current exchange rates. Many point, for instance, to the inroads made last year by Japanese auto makers into the U.S. market, even when  the yen was often weaker than it is now. \par \par \uc2  That's not to say that the Japanese government's aims have changed in any way: the finance ministry clearly remains firmly committed to seeking a weaker yen, through rhetoric or actual \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market actions, with the aim of boosting the competitiveness of Japanese exports and preventing the world's second biggest economy from contracting, analysts believe. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ last stepped in during May and June 2002, firing  several rounds of yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  totaling to a sizable $30 billion, but with only short-lived effect. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Teetering On The Danger Zone\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par After the dollar's tumble earlier Monday of around a full yen against\par the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , for the second time in a week it has reached the\par cusp of the Y118.30-Y118.70 range where most of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to\par support the dollar occurred last summer.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par After hitting a session low around Y118.57 earlier in the global\par session, the dollar has managed to recover to Y118.99 early Monday\par afternoon in New York but is still down   from Y119.75 late Friday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In light of recent statements about where Japan's government would\par like to see its domestic \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the yen's strength appears alarming.\par Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa's staggered \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets\par in December by  remarking that that Y150.00 or Y160.00 would be an\par appropriate level for the dollar; worlds away from its recent ranges.\par The last time the dollar was even close to that level against the yen\par was when it reached a peak of around Y147.00 in August 1998.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Although against this backdrop, Japan's reluctance to act appears even\par more surprising, finance ministry officials  may be holding their fire\par until the appointment of a new Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governor in order that any\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  efforts would appear "in the context of broader policy,"\par says Anne Parker Mills, senior U.S. economist with Brown Brothers\par Harriman in New York.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Incumbent BOJ governor Masaru Hayami's term expires in March. Should\par Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi choose as his  successor someone\par aggressively committed to anti-deflation measures - including purchases\par of foreign bonds - the likely  effect would be for the yen to weaken on\par the appointment announcement, according to Robert Sinche, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par strategist with Citibank in New York.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par At the same time, the U.S. is facing its own uncertainty  surrounding\par top policymakers ahead of the confirmation hearings for Treasury\par Secretary nominee John Snow, expected this month or in February, which\par in turn may be sowing some seeds of doubt in Japan about President\par George W. Bush's attitude toward the dollar.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The problem is that the Japanese don't have a personal  relationship\par with anybody in the Bush team at this point, so my guess is they are\par feeling their way through the dark," in trying to evaluate how the U.S.\par administration would respond to Japan's efforts to send the yen lower,\par said  Anderson, at ABN Amro.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par U.S. manufacturers have been vociferous in pressing for a weaker\par dollar to help sell more goods abroad. Under former  Treasury Secretary\par Paul O'Neill the U.S. government perpetuated the so-called "strong\par dollar" policy and no official change in this stance is expected.\par Nevertheless, some \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market participants wonder whether Snow - an\par industrialist like O'Neill - might prove subtly less adamant  in\par adhering to that policy than his predecessors.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Uncertainty surrounding the finer points of the U.S. administration's\par stance on the dollar is a key factor that has checked \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from\par undertaking \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  during the past few weeks,\par Anderson said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Many market participants believe the U.S. would be more supportive of\par Japan's weak yen policy if the government at the same time embarked on\par an aggressive anti-deflation policy, while tackling the deep structural\par problems in the nation's banking system.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -By John Parry; Dow Jones Newswires\par    john.parry@dowjones.com; 201-938-2096\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020030106dz160053d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
