\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar jumps on strong U.S. manufacturing data. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Kyle Peterson \par \uc2 873 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Januar 2003\par 18:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  (Updates prices, adds economist's comments and other details, changes dateline previous NEW YORK, changes byline) \par \par \uc2  CHICAGO, Jan 2 (Reuters) - The dollar vaulted higher against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday, distancing itself from multiyear lows against European units after data showed a resumption of U.S. manufacturing growth. \par \par \uc2  After last year's spate of disappointing economic figures, the Institute for Supply Management surprised markets. Its December manufacturing index came in at 54.7, sharply higher than the 49.2 in November, and beating expectations for 50.3. A reading above 50 suggests expansion in the manufacturing sector. \par \par \uc2  The upside surprise accelerated the dollar's stab higher - after more than a month of declines linked primarily to uncertainty over international conflicts and the U.S. economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  "It will be interesting to see how sustainable the dollar rally is," said Lara Rhame, U.S. economist at Brown Brothers Harriman in New York. \par \par \uc2  She noted that the broad trend has been dollar-negative for the last month. "It seems like people aren't selling the dollar because they are worried about the current state of economy ... They seem to be selling the dollar because they are worried about what impact a war will have on the economy going forward." \par \par \uc2  The ISM report showed that activity in the U.S. factory sector grew in December after three months of declines. The news surprised analysts who had called for a lower reading in the wake of relatively weak regional manufacturing data released earlier this week. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The euro traded near $1.0370  vs. the dollar, down from its 2002 close at\par $1.05 and more than a cent below its strongest level since November 1999.\par Against the yen, the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  traded at 119.75 yen       , up from its\par 2002 close at 118.74.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par WAR CONCERNS STILL HOLD SPOTLIGHT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Economists said that while the ISM numbers speak well of a U.S. economic\par recovery, they will not make investors forget that the United States appears\par increasingly likely go to war with Iraq.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The mobilization of U.S. forces for a possible war and continuing\par brinkmanship with a nuclear-armed North Korea have upended the dollar, which\par last year hit multiyear lows against all major European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . On a\par trade-weighted basis against America's trading partners, the greenback\par suffered its sharpest decline in 15 years.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar is seen vulnerable to military action in the Persian Gulf as risk\par tends to disrupt the international flow of capital, making it harder for the\par United States to fund its huge current account deficit. That condition is\par exacerbated by widespread investor pessimism.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Markets largely ignored U.S. data released earlier on Thursday showing\par 403,000 initial jobless claims in the week ended Dec. 28, compared with\par 390,000 the week before.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par EUROPE DATA WEAK, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  FEARS NAG\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A survey of manufacturing activity in the euro zone released earlier showed\par the sector shrank for the fourth month running in December as bosses cut\par jobs and output slowed.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Reuters Eurozone Purchasing Managers' Index fell more than expected, to\par 48.4, in December, the steepest deterioration in business conditions in the\par region for almost a year.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Concern that Japan may step into the market and sell yen kept traders on\par their toes. Earlier this week, Japanese authorities said they were prepared\par to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  despite the country's week-long New Year holiday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But analysts are increasingly doubtful that Japan will throw good \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign\par exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserve money at a bad situation. Given that the dollar has been\par sold against virtually every major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the last month, market\par observers view attempts to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as an exercise in futility.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "I think in the near term the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is probably unlikely. I think\par they will try to keep up some verbal \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," said Michael Malpede,\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  analyst at Refco.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "I don't see any strong incentive at the current levels for the Japanese to\par do anything as long as the general dollar tone is negative," he said. "But\par if they see a turn in the dollar, they may be willing to go with it."\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bid prices at 1637 GMT. All data taken from Reuters\par calculated from the levels at 5:30 p.m. EST (2230 GMT) in the\par previous New York session.\par Last  US Close    %      YTD     2002\par 01 Jan.  Change %Change    Close\par -------------------------------------------------------------\par Euro/dlr    