\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar Ends Lower Against Euro, Edges Higher vs. Yen \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tyler Lifton \par \uc2 990 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Dezember 2002\par 23:17\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -- As the dollar coughs and wheezes its way across the finish line of 2002, the rapidity of its decline in recent weeks has some analysts believing that the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be set for a rebound in 2003, if only temporarily. \par \par \uc2  Despite the dollar's tough performance in the year that was, history remains on its side in the next few weeks, analysts contend. \par \par \uc2  "Our base case scenario is to get somewhat of a seasonal  rally in the U.S. dollar," in January, said T.J. Marta, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst with Citibank in New York. \par \par \uc2  Typically, seasonal factors tend to put the dollar on its back foot against the euro at the end of the year into early January, but then work in its favor soon after. These factors include a pickup in global liquidity, which helps to   fund the U.S. current-account deficit, as well as a reduction in the repatriation flows seen at the end of the year which move capital away from the U.S., Marta said. \par \par \uc2  Given looming crises in Iraq and North Korea and worries   about the U.S. economic recovery, the dollar has fallen sharply against its major counterparts in past weeks, setting the stage for a partial recovery when \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets return to full speed next week. \par \par \uc2  But on the year's final day for North American \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading, the greenback dropped once again to multiyear lows against the euro, the Swiss franc and the pound. Against the yen, it managed to take back some of the ground it lost Monday. Traders described volumes as very light, typical for New Year's Eve. \par \par \uc2  Late Tuesday in New York, the euro was trading at $1.0494, up from $1.0476 late Monday. The dollar was worth 118.70 yen, slightly higher than 118.51 yen late Monday. Against the Swiss franc, the dollar was valued at 1.3816 francs, down from 1.3873 francs Monday and close to its low for the day at 1.3813 francs. The pound was trading at $1.6099, up from $1.6041. \par \par \uc2  Among the evidence supporting a dollar bounce early on in 2003, analysts cite data showing that speculators have very large bets already in place favoring European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The total of speculative bets that are bullish on the Swiss franc - a traditional safe haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  - is currently higher than it was even after the terrorist attacks of Sept. 11, 2001, according to the latest data from   the Commodity Futures Trading Commission. \par \par \uc2  "The large bearish positioning currently leveled against  the dollar argues for a correction in the early part of 2003," Jes Black, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at MG Financial in New York wrote in a report Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  For those who maintained bets against the dollar, 2002 likely proved a very profitable year. The euro gained 14.9% from its valuation at the end of 2001, while sterling was up   9.7% against the greenback in the same time period. The dollar was down 9.7% versus the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the year. Against the Swiss franc, the dollar declined 17.4% from Dec. 31, 2001. \par \par \uc2  In the first two weeks of the year during the last 26 years, the dollar rose 20 times, fell five times and was unchanged once against the German mark and the euro in more recent years, according to Larry Greenberg, international economist at Ried, Thunberg & Co. in Westport, Conn. \par \par \uc2  "It's a seasonal time when there should be some profit taking and the dollar could recoup some of its losses," he said. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, the dollar has spent much of the last few weeks propped up primarily by concern that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen against the dollar. The growing potential for Japan to take action soon is another factor that could help the dollar. \par \par \uc2  On Monday, as Japanese financial markets were closing up   shop until next week, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders pushed the dollar to  its weakest level against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in more than three months. Those losses only increase the potential for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  sometime soon. \par \par \uc2  In fact, the greenback received a bit of a boost Tuesday   from Japanese press reports that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  stands poised to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  even as financial markets remain closed for the New Year's holiday this week. Japan is reportedly considering asking European and U.S. monetary authorities to   sell yen for dollars soon. \par \par \uc2  "It's easy for me to imagine the Bank of Japan stepping   in" at any moment, said Jason Bonanca, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Credit Suisse First Boston in New York. \par \par \uc2  While Bonanca believes the long-term down trend for the dollar is still firmly in place - given the weakness of the U.S. economy and the looming military confrontation in Iraq - he concedes that its direction during the first couple of weeks of the year is very difficult to predict, given the rush of pent up activity expected after the New Year. \par \par \uc2  "There's going to be a lot of people piling back into the market" beginning January 2, Bonanca said. After the last two weeks, when many \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  professionals were on vacation even as the dollar was diving, there is "a lot of pent up kinetic energy" in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, he said. \par \par \uc2  According to a report released Tuesday, the Conference Board's consumer confidence index fell to 80.3 in December from a revised 84.9 in November. That result was well below the 85.3 reading that economists had been expecting, and just above the 79.6 reading seen in October, which was the worst level since 1994. \par \par \uc2  The gloomy report deprived the dollar of any chance for a last-day rally, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 2002 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  12-31-02 1717ET \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020021231dycv00669\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
