\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar Mixed Against Rivals Amid Persistent Fears of War \b0\par\par\uc2  By John Parry \par \uc2 833 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Dezember 2002\par 00:20\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -- The dollar was narrowly mixed against its major rivals late Friday in New York, as a nervous hiatus took hold in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets following the greenback's battering earlier in the week amid persistent concerns about  a possible war in the Middle East. \par \par \uc2  "Coming into the close of the week, the market is characterized by dollar weakness that is driven by geopolitical fears," said Thomas Molloy, a trader with Bank   Leumi in New York. \par \par \uc2  With Japan, Europe and the U.S. all closed  at least part of next week, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders were tinkering with positions ahead of year end, with many major market participants sidelined. \par \par \uc2  Albeit entering a traditionally quiet time of year for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, there is a danger that amid scant trading volumes, the greenback could fall prey to an intensification of negative sentiment. That stems mainly from worries about a potential war with Iraq, which were rippling across markets from oil, where crude was ending the   week above $30 per barrel to gold, which remained in sight  of the fresh five and a half year highs it had hit Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Late Friday in New York, the euro was trading at $1.0265, modestly down from $1.0273 late Thursday. Against the yen, the dollar was at 120.45 yen, modestly lower than 120.54 yen late Thursday. Against the Swiss franc, the dollar was trading at 1.4246 yen, somewhat higher than 1.4229 Thursday yen. The pound was trading hands at $1.6022,  a bit lower than $1.6033 Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Despite its modest retreat Friday, the Swiss franc stands to benefit from a steady bid from investors making a classic "safe haven" shift out of the dollar, seeking a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that has traditionally become a harbor in times of global conflict. \par \par \uc2  Traders will be on tenterhooks over the coming days and weeks to see whether Iraq can provide U.N. inspectors with information about its weapons programs that will satisfy the   U.S. government. \par \par \uc2  On Thursday, U.S. Secretary of State Colin Powell blasted Iraq's declaration that it holds no weapons of mass destruction, saying the statement Iraq handed over to the United Nations is riddled with deception. But Powell stopped   short of saying that this alone was cause for war. \par \par \uc2  Any onset of conflict could drive the dollar yet lower across the board. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, with the greenback still hovering just above the key 120.0 yen level, it is flirting dangerously with a so-called discomfort zone for the Japanese government, where the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets increases. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa reiterated his argument late Thursday that the dollar should be valued at around 150.0 yen to the yen on a purchasing power parity basis, continuing a steady chorus of senior Japanese officials calls for a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . These remarks may cause a few sweaty palms on lightly-staffed trading desks heading into year end, as traders wonder whether \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 ministry of finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  will orchestrate \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  aimed at weakening the yen via the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "If you want to be more effective in your \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,   do it when markets are illiquid, you'll get more bang for your buck," said Marcel Kasumovich, head of G10 \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at Merrill Lynch & Co. in New York. That makes Christmas week a potentially attractive time for the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  to enter the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  In Europe, the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  again was forced to digest another round of disappointing economic news from the   region. This time, it was a report showing that French consumer spending tumbled in November due to a steep decline   in clothing and textile spending and lower purchases of automobiles. \par \par \uc2  "We had some pretty discouraging news out of the euro zone and out of Japan last night," said Lara Rhame, senior economist at Brown Brothers Harriman in New York.  "The fact   that the dollar could not benefit from that reinforces the  dollar bearish sentiment that's weighing on the markets," Rhame said. \par \par \uc2  While the major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were trapped in tight ranges,   some more lively price action occurred in some peripheral \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  including the Brazilian Real. \par \par \uc2  The real, which has gained around 8% over the last week,   at first rallied Friday to hit its best point in three months, before news that a key party won't join President-elect Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva's coalition cut into gains. Late Friday, the real was at 3.49 real per dollar, compared with 3.48 real late Thursday, bucking the pattern of five days of successive stronger closes. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    By John Parry; Dow Jones Newswires\par    john.parry@dowjones.com; 201-938-2096\par    