\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar Gains on Euro, Falls Against Other  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2  By Tyler Lifton \par \uc2 670 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Dezember 2002\par 23:57\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -- Though the dollar rebounded somewhat from significant day-earlier losses against the euro, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was mostly weaker Wednesday in a listless session that saw the stock market fall and signals of war with Iraq intensify. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's modest strength against the euro followed a  volatile session Tuesday prior in which comments from the White House reiterating the so-called strong dollar policy provided the only leg of support in an otherwise broadly based move down by the greenback. \par \par \uc2  "The spike up above $1.03 was speculatively driven," Michael Woolfolk, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Bank of New York, said of the nearly three-year high to which the euro had rocketed  Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  As a result of that "fast money" action, he said it was perfectly logical that the euro took a breather Wednesday as  some of those trades were unwound to book profits. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere the dollar was dragged down. Lacking much activity in the markets as trading books begin to pare exposure ahead of year-end, analysts pointed to sagging equity prices and heated rhetoric over Iraq's weapons declaration as the reasons for the dollar's softness. \par \par \uc2  "There's been some escalation in the amount of Iraq rhetoric in the last couple of days," said Nick Bennenbroek, international economist at Brown Brothers Harriman in New York. "Typically those types of geopolitical headlines have been dollar negative," he said. \par \par \uc2  In particular, U.K. Foreign Secretary Jack Straw said Saddam Hussein's assertion that he has no weapons of mass destruction is an "obvious falsehood" and his 12,000-page declaration to the U.N. falls short of the world body's demands. \par \par \uc2  In the U.S., Secretary of State Colin Powell said the Iraq declaration to the U.N. on its arms programs "is troublesome" and the U.S. is "not encouraged" that Baghdad has gotten the message that the international community expects it to disarm. Powell will present Thursday the U.S.'s view of the Iraqi weapons declaration to the U.N. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the Dow Jones Industrial Average lost 88.04 points to finish at 8447.35, down 1%, while the Nasdaq Composite Index shed 30.55 points, or 2.2%, to end at 1361.50. \par \par \uc2  Late Wednesday in New York, the euro was trading at $1.0264, down from $1.0280 late Tuesday, while the dollar was valued at 121.08 yen, down from 121.38 yen. Sterling rose to $1.6003 from $1.5979 late Tuesday and got as high as $1.6026 at one point Wednesday, its highest level since the first quarter of 2000, partially on news that United Kingdom unemployment in November was the lowest since 1975. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, the dollar lost a bit of ground even though the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains. Given the increasingly illiquid conditions in financial markets heading into the winter holiday break, the effect of any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be multiplied, making it a potentially attractive time of year for Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  to take action. \par \par \uc2  But Japan's challenge in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  arena remains that  it is mostly broad dollar weakness defining the market, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Indeed, the dollar received scant support Wednesday from  a report showing that the U.S. deficit in international trade in goods and services narrowed to $35.07 billion during October, down from a revised $37.10 billion in September. The West Coast dock workers' strike artificially depressed imports, which largely accounted for the deficit's  contraction, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  More important, said Mr. Woolfolk, is the fact that non-seasonally adjusted trade numbers, which are more pertinent to \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , showed the U.S. trade deficit deteriorated to a near record $45.6 billion in October from a revised $42.9 billion in September. \par \par \uc2  -By Tyler Lifton, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-2248; -tyler.lifton@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  12-18-02 1757ET \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020021218dyci00bkp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
