\par\par \b \uc2  DOLLAR OUTLOOK: Downside Risks To Dog Dlr In Early 2003 \b0\par\par\uc2  By John Parry \par \uc2 1,154 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Dezember 2002\par 20:48\par \uc2  Dow Jones Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- The dollar is shrouded in a pall of negative sentiment heading into early 2003. \par \par \uc2  Even should the acute geopolitical uncertainty surrounding war with Iraq, tensions with North Korea and the  persistent threat of global terrorist activity recede somewhat - hardly a surefire bet - the greenback may still suffer from the hefty U.S. current account deficit, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2   strategists said. \par \par \uc2  After falling some 13% against the euro and 8% against the yen in 2002 to date, some strategists expect the dollar to continue retreating against major rivals, with the possible exception of the yen. \par \par \uc2  All six analysts consulted by Dow Jones Newswires expect  the euro to remain above parity with the dollar at the end of January, with the lowest forecast for $1.00 and the highest at $1.06. Wednesday afternoon in New York, the euro was at $1.0245. \par \par \uc2  "The bottom line is that we are now in a new cycle of dollar weakness...it's a dollar phenomenon", said Michael Rosenberg, head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research with Deutsche Bank in New York. He expects the euro to be at $1.04 by the end of January. \par \par \uc2  "This is a big move already and it has very strong legs, all based on the massive U.S. current account deficit and interest rates that are too low to finance it," Rosenberg added. \par \par \uc2  Paul Mackel, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with Dresdner Kleinwort  Wasserstein in London agrees that the U.S. "will have trouble financing the current account deficit," now running  at more than 5.0% of gross domestic product. \par \par \uc2  Mackel expects foreign investors' appetite for U.S. assets to steadily wane over the coming year, partly because  he anticipates that issuance of U.S. corporate bonds will shrink. Corporate paper, which has been one of the key attractions for foreign investors in 2001 and 2002, is no longer acting as such a strong magnet for capital flows to fund the hefty U.S. financing gap, he said. \par \par \uc2  Another reason why global demand for dollar-denominated assets is diminishing are the paltry rates of return investors can now obtain from short-dated U.S. fixed income instruments. \par \par \uc2  The Federal Reserve has axed interest rates to their lowest levels in more than four decades, leaving the fed funds rate now at 1.25%. With the ammunition for monetary easing virtually spent in trying to ramp up economic growth,  the U.S. government is "relying on fiscal policy more, but  (the effects) will take a bit of time to come through," said Mozina. In that context, "the weakening dollar could be a safety valve," helping to boost U.S. exports, he added. \par \par \uc2  President George W. Bush's choice of John Snow as Treasury Secretary earlier this month, with confirmation hearings expected in January, has contributed to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders' perception that the so-called `strong dollar policy' may be fraying a little around the edges. \par \par \uc2  In spite of a White House official's reaffirmation of the policy Tuesday and although no change in publicly stated  policy is expected, the nomination of Snow, a railroad executive, "is not the hardline stuff that dollar bulls would really want," Mozina said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. government "can't abandon the slogan (that they support a strong dollar) but the fact is the U.S. administration has allowed a gradual weakening of the dollar  this year", said Shuji Takano, senior manager of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Aozora Bank in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's demise has taken hold in spite of the apparent advantage the U.S. economy enjoys over its main rivals. While many \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts expect that U.S. economic growth may pick up the pace in 2003 and is highly likely to outstrip the euro-zone's lagging economy, they still forecast the greenback will continue retrenching against the euro. Over the near term, the biggest single millstone around the dollar's neck is the risk of the U.S. playing a leading role in a possible war. \par \par \uc2  If the U.S. does go to war with Iraq, as is beginning to   appear increasingly likely, the dollar's fortunes hinge on  the speed and outcome of such a military conflict. If the U.S. manages to remove Iraq's leader Saddam Hussein in a short, sharp war, that would likely boost the greenback, said Michael Derks, chief global strategist with Commonwealth Bank in London. But a more protracted U.S.-led conflict, especially if this eroded international support for President George W. Bush, would "get decidedly awkward for the dollar.," Derks says. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or lack of it, could skew the dollar's performance either way. \par \par \uc2  Analysts' projections for the dollar against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  are decidedly mixed: ranging from Y115.0 to Y125.0 at the end of January. Wednesday afternooon in New  York, the dollar was changing hands at Y121.10. \par \par \uc2  Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which acts via the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  during \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , considers a weaker yen essential to maintain domestic exporters' competitiveness. The world's second biggest economy is struggling to avoid slipping back into economic contraction after its struggle to expand during 2002. \par \par \uc2  Toward the end of Japan's fiscal year on Mar. 31, if Japanese companies' earnings and the economic outlook deteriorate, such detrimental effects "will start to kick in, sending the dollar back up again," against the yen, says Masamichi Koike, senior vice president and head of spot  \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading, Sumitomo Mitsui Banking Corp. in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Table of One-Month and Three-month Forecasts\par                            End Jan '03  End March '03\par    David Mozina, head of G10 strategy, Banc Of America Securities, New\par York:\par    euro/dollar             $1.03        $1.04\par    dollar/yen              Y125.0       Y126.0\par    euro/yen                Y128.75      Y131.5\par    Mike Rosenberg, head of global \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  research, Deutsche\par Bank, New York:\par    euro/dollar             $1.04        $1.05\par    dollar/yen              Y123.0       Y125.0\par    euro/yen                Y127.92      Y131.25\par    Michael Derks, chief global strategist, Commonwealth Bank in London:\par    euro/dollar             $1.00        $1.04\par    dollar/yen              Y125.0       Y127.0\par    euro/yen                Y125.0       Y132.0\par    Paul Mackel, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist, Dresdner Kleinwort Wasserstein in\par London:\par    euro/dollar             $1.02        $1.03\par    dollar/yen              Y122.0       Y120.0\par    euro/yen                Y124.0       Y124.0\par    Masamichi Koike, senior vice president and head of spot \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign\par exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trading, Sumitomo Mitsui Banking Corp. in Tokyo:\par    euro/dollar             $1.06   