\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar Rebounds Against Yen, But Suffers From Oil Spike \b0\par\par\uc2  By John Parry \par \uc2 938 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Dezember 2002\par 02:32\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -- The dollar rebounded against the yen Monday,  lifted by fears that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in an effort to weaken the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but was trapped in narrow ranges against the euro and other major rivals as higher oil  prices sullied the greenback. \par \par \uc2  In a confirmation that the broad bearish sentiment that beset the dollar late last week was more than transient, the  euro held above the key technical resistance level of $1.02  which it shattered on Friday. Even a powerful U.S. stock market rally that boosted the Dow Jones Industrial Average to 8627.40, or 2.2%, failed to lift the dollar's spirits much. \par \par \uc2  Late Monday afternoon in New York, the dollar was at 121.35 yen, not far below its intraday global session high around 121.45 yen, as traders fretted about the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and up from 120.54 yen late Friday in New York. The euro was at $1.0226, unchanged from late Friday. Against the Swiss franc, the dollar was at 1.4424 Swiss francs, moderately down from 1.4443 francs late Friday. Sterling was at $1.5918, up from $1.5892. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar is not moving higher and yet you've got the stock market up sharply," said Michael Rosenberg, head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research with Deutsche Bank in New York. "That's telling me that the dollar is in trouble," he said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was also hurt by the ongoing social conflict in Venezuela, one of the world's biggest oil producing countries, which pushed crude oil prices above $30 per barrel for the first time in two months Monday. This is "negative for the dollar, because a spike up in oil prices is a reflection of geopolitical risk which could undermine the U.S. economic recovery," said Paresh Upadhyaya, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2   analyst with Putnam Investments in Boston. \par \par \uc2  Firing rubber bullets, police scattered opposition protesters from one of several highway blockades in the Venezuelan capital of Caracas on Monday. Supporters of Mr. Chavez fought back with rocks and debris. \par \par \uc2  The confrontation with security forces marked an escalation of tensions in Venezuela's political crisis after  organizers of a two-week-old opposition strike aimed at toppling Mr. Chavez opened a day of blockades at dawn. Crude  prices rallied in reaction to the general strike, which is disrupting deliveries to the U.S. and elsewhere. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. imported 1.453 million barrels a day of crude oil from Venezuela in October, the latest month for which figures are available, making Venezuela the fourth-largest source of imports for the U.S., according to the U.S. Department of Energy's Energy Information Administration. Hints of worries that higher oil prices could put upward pressure on inflation were reflected in the slip of longer-dated Treasury bond prices. \par \par \uc2  Among rival oil producing countries, Norway cashed in on  tighter supply in the global market, as the Norwegian krone  rallied. The euro slipped to 7.2876 Norwegian kroner late Monday, from 7.3289 kroner late Friday, while the dollar retreated to 7.1270 kroner from 7.2113 kroner late Friday. \par \par \uc2  With much of the market's focus on the yen, the dollar's  latest stumble has once again dramatically raised the odds of the Bank of Japan taking advantage of thin trade during the holiday season by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, U.S.-based analysts reckon, particularly in the wake of some  stern rhetoric from Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "They might want to time their next \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  for a period of maximum impact," when trading volumes are low, said Anne Parker Mills, senior U.S. economist with Brown Brothers Harriman in New York. Already, "there are pretty good odds of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the yen does not retreat pretty  quickly and a high probability" if the dollar slumps below 120.00 yen, Ms. Mills said. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese finance ministry considers speed of moves to be as important as absolute levels when deciding on \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Even so, there is little doubt that a steeper  slip of the dollar to a key technical support area at 119.10 yen would hit "very critical lower boundary" that likely could precipitate BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said Jan Poser, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research with Bank Sarasin in Zurich, Switzerland. \par \par \uc2  Some analysts believe the BOJ might also seek to act soon in order to take advantage of the relative limbo in the  U.S. Treasury, before the Senate confirmation of John Snow to replace Paul O'Neill as Treasury secretary. Although there's little to suggest that Mr. Snow will publicly embrace a weaker dollar, many analysts believe he will continue to keep the so-called "strong dollar policy" vague,  which could potentially work against any Japanese efforts to bolster the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Among Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the real rallied strongly, ending 3.4% stronger, as corporate inflows and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  repurchase agreement sales pushed the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to its strongest level so far this month. The real was at 3.600 Brazilian reals per dollar late Monday compared with 3.730 reals per dollar late Friday. In part the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  firmed as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders lent their approval to President-elect Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva's recently announced choices of a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor, finance minister and other members of his ministerial team. \par \par \uc2  In its final meeting with Arminio Fraga directing monetary policy, Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  appears set to raise interest rates on Wednesday to beat back inflation stemming from a weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  12-16-02 2032ET \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020021217dych000m9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
