\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar on defensive in Asia, overshadowed by Iraq. \b0\par\par\uc2 332 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Dezember 2002\par 00:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Dec 16 (Reuters) - The dollar was on the defensive in early Asian trading on Monday as growing tension with Iraq undermined sentiment, but traders said some bargain-hunting was likely after steep losses last week. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. military said U.S. and British warplanes attacked Iraqi air defences on Sunday after Iraqi artillery fired at allied aircraft patrolling the "no-fly" zone in southern Iraq. At 2303 GMT, the dollar was quoted at 120.60/67 yen, compared with 120.45/53 in late Friday trading in New York. \par \par \uc2  "We would like to see how Japanese monetary authorities show their concern about the sharp gain by the yen (on Friday)," said a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at a Japanese bank. \par \par \uc2  But Japanese Economics Minister Heizo Takenaka said on Sunday the government need not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in markets to weaken the yen. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is due to hold a two-day policy-setting meeting starting on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Most economists expect no change in monetary policy. \par \par \uc2  The euro was at $1.0233/36 and 123.40/45 yen compared with 1.0236/46 and 123.39 yen in New York. \par \par \uc2  European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Wim Duisenberg said on Saturday the bank might need to react with monetary tools in case of a protracted war against Iraq. \par \par \uc2  Bankers say an outbreak of war would create a policy dilemma for the ECB as it could further slow the global economy while fuelling inflation through higher oil prices. \par \par \uc2  Some traders said portfolio reshuffling by global investors into euro assets out of U.S. assets was supporting the euro. \par \par \uc2  Late last week, the U.S. Commerce Department said the U.S. current account deficit in the third quarter was $127.04 billion, compared with $127.61 billion in the second quarter. \par \par \uc2  Traders say that given that the United States must finance those deficits with global investors' money, the portfolio reshuffling could be a threat to U.S. markets. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021215dycf00w3o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
