\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 2- \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2 's Takenaka - no need for  \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2 631 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Dezember 2002\par 04:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Dec 15 (Reuters) - Japanese Economics Minister Heizo Takenaka said on Sunday the government need not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to weaken the yen. \par \par \uc2  Speaking on a local television programme, Takenaka said the current policy of monetary easing being followed by the Bank of Japan would have the same effects as a weakening of the yen. \par \par \uc2  Japanese officials have been talking down the yen in recent weeks, raising the possibility that the country might have adopted a deliberate policy of weakening the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa had set markets abuzz earlier in the month when he was quoted by Japanese media as saying 150-160 yen to the dollar would be a suitable exchange rate. \par \par \uc2  On Friday, the dollar had struggled for direction in Asian trade, and was changing hands in late trading at around 122.75/77 yen  from a low of 122.24 yen. \par \par \uc2  A weaker yen would benefit Japan's exporters, by making their products cheaper in overseas markets. It was exports that had led Japan's slight economic recovery earlier this year, but the growth has faltered in recent months as concerns grow over the strength of demand in the United States, Japan's largest single market. \par \par \uc2  Takenaka had said last week that he hoped the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be more aggressive in its efforts. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is due to hold a two-day policy-setting meeting starting on Monday. Most economists expect no change in monetary policy. \par \par \uc2  TOUGH TIMES \par \par \uc2  Takenaka also said on Sunday that deregulation of the Japanese economy had not gone far enough and the government must overcome resistance to push deregulation through. \par \par \uc2  The coming fiscal year will be a tough one for Japan as the government pushes ahead with planned structural reforms and measures to speed up the disposal of banks' bad loans, he said. \par \par \uc2  "Next year we will see that Japan is slowing changing," Takenaka said, adding that there was no choice but to push on with his reform plans. \par \par \uc2  "If we do nothing, there will be a state of financial panic," he said. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese government, in a draft version of its annual economic outlook, said on Friday that economic growth rates would be below one percent in the next few years due to structural reforms and the tough global economic environment. \par \par \uc2  In a second television interview on Sunday, Takenaka said that he was trying to push through his bank reform plans as fast as possible, adding that outright nationalisation was not on the immediate agenda. \par \par \uc2  "Nationalisation was unavoidable when dealing with bankrupt institutions," he said, referring to the government's takeover of the former Long Term Credit Bank of Japan in 1998. \par \par \uc2  "There are no bankrupt banks at the moment and we must take measures to ensure that there will be none." \par \par \uc2  Takenaka has come under increasing fire from parts of the media and nationalist politicians who fear that his reforms may lead to foreign firms taking over more banks. The LTCB was sold to U.S. investment fund Ripplewood in 2000, becoming Shinsei Bank, the first in Japan's cosy financial world to be run by foreigners. \par \par \uc2  Takenaka also said that there are still problems with Japan's tax system that need to be worked through. \par \par \uc2  On Friday the government agreed plans for net tax cuts of 1.8 trillion yen ($14.68 billion) in the next fiscal year. The plan includes an easing of the burden on companies but an increased burden for ordinary tax payers. \par \par \uc2  Defending the plan, Takenaka said that tax reform was something that would take several years, and Friday's proposals were only covering the first year, and that they would be a cue for further debate in coming years. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021215dycf001p5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
