\par\par \b \uc2  INTERVIEW: Japan Koizumi: Need Deflation-Fighter At BOJ \b0\par\par\uc2  By James Simms \par \uc2 1,070 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Dezember 2002\par 12:27\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi said Wednesday the nation needs a bold deflation-fighter to lead the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the current governor's term ends in March, underscoring the concerns falling prices continue to eat away at the economy and worsen the bad loan problem at banks. \par \par \uc2  "A person with an aggressive will to overcome deflation would be fitting for the job of BOJ governor," Koizumi told Dow Jones Newswires and CNBC Asia-Pacific in an interview. \par \par \uc2  He said it isn't the time to name potential candidates to succeed Masaru Hayami, but indicated he wants someone more committed to halt 37 months of unbroken consumer price declines. \par \par \uc2  Hayami has said there's little more the BOJ can do to fight deflation, rejecting the adoption of binding inflation targets while insisting only structural reform of the banking system and economy can finally slay the deflation demon. \par \par \uc2  Koizumi said, though, that he's not making inflation targets a litmus test for candidates seeking the post. \par \par \uc2  "Aside from whether they use the term 'inflation target' or not, the appropriate person for BOJ governor is someone who has a strong understanding of what kind of monetary policies are needed to overcome deflation." \par \par \uc2  One person considered a prime candidate for the job, former BOJ Deputy Governor Toshihiko Fukui, sounded a conciliatory tone Wednesday but didn't give the impression he'd take a more aggressive stance than Hayami. \par \par \uc2  Fukui told Dow Jones Newswires in a separate interview that the BOJ will maintain a bias toward easing policy to avert any concerns of tightening liquidity in financial markets and promote expectations of a stable financial environment. \par \par \uc2  "The BOJ has a role in calming anxiety in financial markets such as stemming concerns about a lack of liquidity or the impact of financial institutions' disposal of bad loans," Fukui said. \par \par \uc2  Persistent price falls lessen the incentive for consumers to spend and businesses to invest now and push up the real repayment burden of Japan's legions of debtors, while falling land prices - down 70% in urban areas since the rupture of the late 1980s "bubble" - erode the value of collateral backing much of the loans on banks' books. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par               Long Slog On Deflation\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Since March 2001, the BOJ has pinned short-term rates essentially at\par zero and flooded the banking system with excess cash, promising to\par maintain this "quantitative easing" until consumer prices decisively\par stop falling.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But at almost every opportunity, politicians and government\par bureaucrats hound the BOJ to ease, arguing that only by ramping up money\par supply can price falls be stopped. The onus on the BOJ is especially\par acute because, with the worst public debt position in the industrial\par world, the government is constrained from massive fiscal stimulus.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Indeed, Koizumi strongly hinted in the interview he may abandon the\par government's target of defeating deflation by March 2004, blaming the\par severity of the problem and its global spread.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The forecast may change - the economy is a living thing - that's\par quite possible," he said in his trademark staccato style. "Japanese\par people are getting a taste of deflation for the first time in the\par postwar period," the prime minister said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par He rejected the suggestion that he has repeatedly pushed back targets\par for various reforms, saying there is no turning back on such measures as\par cleaning up the bad loans crippling the banking system. The inability of\par banks to price lending to risk, as well as lack of demand from healthy\par firms is undermining the effectiveness of easy monetary policy.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Even though it's impossible to overcome deflation rapidly, we're\par getting a shared understanding" of what needs to be done. "With this\par understanding, we have been taking steps and if we steadily implement\par these steps ... I believe we can definitely overcome this problem."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Koizumi stressed that deflation isn't Japan's problem alone but is a\par global issue, citing the large gap between labor costs in industrialized\par and developing countries - particularly China.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Government officials have recently taken to warning that China is\par "exporting" its deflation to Japan and other nations, with Shiokawa and\par others urging China to let the value of its yuan \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rise.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                   Talking Yen Down\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par One deflation-fighting weapon in the government's arsenal is \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign\par exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  - weakening the yen's value would make Japan's exports and\par overseas business \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  more profitable in yen terms and would push\par up import prices here.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the interview, Koizumi took weak-yen jawboning to the highest level\par of government but also made clear \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  won't \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par markets to force the yen down.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The government doesn't intend to take a stance of depreciating the\par yen," Koizumi said. "But under current circumstances I fully understand\par the view that a slight yen fall is preferable to a yen rise."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Given Japan's burgeoning public debt, credit-rating rating downgrades\par that have put Japan below some of its aid recipients and other woes,\par "The current high level of the yen is hard to understand," Koizumi said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "In such circumstances, it wouldn't be strange for the yen to weaken,\par but far from weakening, the yen remains quite strong," the premier said.\par "Traditional economics don't apply here."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par It's unusual for the prime minister to speak so specifically about the\par yen. Other Japanese officials have recently sought to talk the yen\par lower, with Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa saying relative\par purchasing power would argue for the dollar to be around Y150 to Y160.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The government \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets in May and June,\par selling a record Y4 trillion to keep the dollar above Y115 but hasn't\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to push the dollar above Y120. Around 1130 GMT Wednesday, the\par dollar traded at Y123.25.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Koizumi dodged a question on whether Japan is in an economic crisis,\par saying only that economic conditions are "severe" but insisting that\par "the worst thing is to be pessimistic and lose confidence."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par He acknowledged that exports, the key prop in this year's feeble\par recovery, were weakening, but said personal consumption is improving.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "If there is light, there is shadow. If there is good, there is bad,"\par the prime minister said. "Now we can see some bright signs."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -By James Simms, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929;\par james.simms@dowjones.com\par    (Hiroshi Inoue, Miyako Takebe and William Mallard contributed to\par this item)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020021211dycb000nc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
