\par\par \b \uc2  Yen hurt by Japan jawboning, dollar awaits data. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Chisa Fujioka \par \uc2 800 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Dezember 2002\par 08:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Dec 6 (Reuters) - The yen was back on the defensive on Friday as Japanese officials sought to talk down the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but it avoided fresh lows in a market watchful for key U.S. data later in the day. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa, who earlier this week sparked yen-selling across the board by saying the yen should be trading around 150-160 yen to the dollar, said the yen was too strong given purchasing power parity. \par \par \uc2  Top financial diplomat Haruhiko Kuroda reiterated his usual line that the yen's recent weakness against the greenback was a reaction to its earlier excessive rise and he expected weakness to continue. \par \par \uc2  The chorus was joined by Financial Services and Economics Minister Heizo Takenaka, who said it was plausible for \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates to move towards purchasing power parity in the long term. \par \par \uc2  But traders said these were basically a replay of previous comments and thus did not ignite a massive sell-off in the yen. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar is well-bid, but we're going to need fresh factors to push it past recent highs," said Noriyuki Kato, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at ING in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  By late Tokyo trade, the dollar was at 125.19/25 yen compared with 124.85 in New York late on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Earlier, it came close to an overnight six-month high of 125.73, but eased back after a bout of profit-taking and news that a small plane had crashed into the Federal Reserve Building in Miami, Florida. \par \par \uc2  Fears of an extremist attack receded when police said the crash was being treated as an accident and there was nothing suspicious. \par \par \uc2  The euro advanced to 125.34 yen from 124.90 yen, with the market relieved after the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  delivered a 50 basis point rate cut on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  On the dollar, the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was supported at $1.0013 from a late U.S. level of $1.0006. \par \par \uc2  JOBS, IRAQ \par \par \uc2  After Japanese jawboning this week, traders said the focus of the market was shifting to U.S. non-farm payrolls data due at 1330 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Economists polled by Reuters forecast an increase of 38,000 jobs in the November report, compared with a loss of 5,000 in October. The jobless rate was seen rising, however, to 5.8 percent from 5.7 percent. \par \par \uc2  Others were also cautious of taking on big dollar positions with the U.S. stock market limp and the spectre of a U.S. attack on Iraq looming with the expiry of a United Nations deadline for Baghdad to itemise its weaponry by Sunday. \par \par \uc2  Still, traders noted that Japanese exporters, key buyers of yen in Tokyo, had stayed on the sidelines recently to see how far the dollar would rise. \par \par \uc2  While any sharp weakening of the yen was bound to irk \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's trading rivals, traders said Japanese verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would continue to be a cause for concern. \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa also griped about China on Friday, saying he thought the Chinese yuan was too weak given the country's economic fundamentals. But he also said he did not necessarily want the yuan to be revalued. \par \par \uc2  "In today's world, with market economies at its core, \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be left for the market to decide," Shiokawa told a news conference. \par \par \uc2  "But if we see an imbalance (among \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ), we need to make an effort to fix it... From my standpoint, I think it's appropriate to compare by looking at countries' purchasing power parities," he added. \par \par \uc2  Many analysts said it was natural for Japan to want to see a stronger yuan but it was unlikely to happen. \par \par \uc2  "Japanese industries are in many ways suffering from competition from China and it's...sucking many manufacturers out of the country," said Marshall Gittler, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Deutsche Bank in Tokyo \par \par \uc2  "But from the Chinese point of view, they are suffering from deflation so why on earth would they want to revalue their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  upward?" \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa denied saying he wanted China to revalue the yuan, and also said he did not intend to make any proposal to the Group of Seven (G7) leading industrial economies on action to fix \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  imbalances. \par \par \uc2  On Shiokawa's comment linking the yen rate to purchasing power parity, some economists have noted that his calculation was based on the consumer price index and that the market rate has rarely been in line with that. Calculations based on the wholesale price index indicate a much stronger rate for the yen. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par  All \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  news ...        Debt...      Indicators...\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reports..\par  \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par All spots\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Tokyo spots\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Europe spots\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Volatilities\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Tokyo \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market info from BOJ\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  World \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  news\par  Official 