\par\par \b \uc2  WRAPUP 3-Japan officials cheer yen lower as economy trips. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Stuart Grudgings \par \uc2 783 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Dezember 2002\par 10:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Dec 5 (Reuters) - Japanese officials cheered on the dollar and other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as they marched higher against the yen on Thursday after another batch of data showed the flagging economy could use a boost from exports. \par \par \uc2  The yen hit a one-month low against the dollar, three-year lows against the pound and euro and a five-month trough against the Australian dollar, as the belief took hold that Tokyo was turning to a weak-yen policy to protect a feeble recovery. \par \par \uc2  Top financial diplomat Haruhiko Kuroda led the cheerleading, calling the yen's recent fall "appropriate". \par \par \uc2  "I think it's in the process of correcting an excessive rise," said Kuroda, vice finance minister for international affairs. \par \par \uc2  As foreign and Japanese institutional investors dumped the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it slid to around 125.20 against the dollar, bringing its losses against the dollar to five percent since November 10. \par \par \uc2  The yen also hit its lowest level against sterling and the euro since 1999, touching 196.40 and 125.35 respectively. \par \par \uc2  Sentiment for the yen was not helped by a government survey showing that firms cut back spending on plant and equipment for the fourth straight quarter in July-September, suggesting again that Japan's economic recovery was petering out. \par \par \uc2  Capital expenditure by Japanese firms fell by 13.9 percent in the quarter from a year earlier, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said. \par \par \uc2  Another ministry survey showed large companies reporting worsening business conditions in the current quarter to December outnumbered those seeing an improvement. It was the seventh quarter in a row that pessimists outnumbered optimists. \par \par \uc2  The business sentiment index for large companies came in at minus 5.9, which was an improvement from the previous quarter but significantly below a forecast of minus 0.3 in the previous survey three months ago. Smaller companies were faring worse. \par \par \uc2  The index measures the percentage of firms expecting business to improve minus the percentage that expect it to worsen. \par \par \uc2  EXPORT WORRY \par \par \uc2  With recent economic data suggesting that exports may be cooling, the weak capital expenditure figures showed that domestic demand had yet to take over as the engine of growth, leaving Japan vulnerable to a global downturn, economists said. \par \par \uc2  "It was a touch below our forecasts. Obviously things are still pretty weak," said HSBC Securities economist Peter Morgan. \par \par \uc2  After rising strongly earlier this year, Japanese exports have faded, falling for four out of the last five months. \par \par \uc2  The darkening export picture has increased concerns that Japan is about to fall into its fourth recession in a decade. \par \par \uc2  Gross domestic product (GDP) data last month showed Japan's economy managed to extend its recovery from its worst post-war recession into a third quarter, but at a slowing pace. \par \par \uc2  With interest rates already near zero and government debt close to 140 percent of GDP, the government has few conventional options left to get the economy back on track. \par \par \uc2  Against this background, Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa set off a wave of speculation that Tokyo wanted a sharply weaker yen when he was reported as saying last weekend that the yen should be trading at 150-160 per dollar. \par \par \uc2  He later said he meant only that this level would be appropriate if the yen's purchasing power was compared to that of the dollar. But the yen has been falling ever since, dropping about two percent since Monday. \par \par \uc2  Skipping over the fact that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  more often on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market than any other major country in recent times, Shiokawa said on Thursday that he was against the idea of governments manipulating the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  "Our basic policy is that they (rates) should be determined in markets," he told parliament. \par \par \uc2  GOOD PRETEXT TO SELL \par \par \uc2  While official rhetoric always gives markets a good pretext for selling the yen, analysts said that more fundamental reasons could be seen behind the move, including the stagnant economy and the prospect of more radical monetary easing next year. \par \par \uc2  "I think what's really going on is that there are more expectations that the Bank of Japan is once again going to increase liquidity supply," said Jeff Young, chief Japan economist at Nikko Salomon. \par \par \uc2  Young sees the yen moving to a 130-135 range against the dollar by next April, helped by the view that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may embark on more radical easing after present Governor Masaru Hayami steps down early next year. \par \par \uc2  But analysts also questioned how far Tokyo could weaken the yen before irritating other countries, including the United States, whose carmakers and other major exporters were unhappy about the yen's level even before its latest tumble. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021205dyc5008c6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
