\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar up on talk of Japan 'weak yen' campaign. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Mariko Hayashibara \par \uc2 787 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Dezember 2002\par 08:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Dec 2 (Reuters) - The dollar rose to a one-month high against the yen on Monday as Japanese officials' appeared to renew a campaign for a weaker yen, with a brighter outlook for the U.S economy lending a hand. \par \par \uc2  The dollar edged up nearly one yen from late New York levels on Friday as the market interpreted remarks from Japanese officials to mean that the authorities in Tokyo were getting serious about talking the yen down again. \par \par \uc2  Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa was quoted in a Japanese newspaper as saying that the yen was excessively strong and that it should be around 150-160 per dollar. \par \par \uc2  Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief, Haruhiko Kuroda, wrote a commentary in London's Financial Times, calling for coordinated reflation policies from the United States, Europe and Japan and urging the Bank of Japan (BOJ) to set an inflation target of three percent as well as increase liquidity in the money market. \par \par \uc2  "Both remarks are far from realistic and this shows that there's a build-up in the sense of crisis among Japanese monetary authorities," said Tomoko Fujii, an economist at Nikko Salomon Smith Barney. \par \par \uc2  "Luckly, the dollar is rising now, but if it falls again (against the yen), I think the monetary authorities are ready to step into the market," Fujii said. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said Kuroda's call for the BOJ to adopt an inflation target was a factor for a weaker yen as the BOJ would have to take more radical easing steps to trigger a rise in prices. \par \par \uc2  Most traders dismissed Shiokawa's reported range as too far off current levels to be a realistic target but speculative bids helped trip some stop-loss buying, pushing the dollar to as high as 123.45 yen its best since October 29. \par \par \uc2  As of 0648 GMT, the dollar was quoted at 123.35/38 yen compared with 122.44 in late New York trade on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The market showed little reaction to Reuters/Nomura/JMMA Purchasing Managers' Index released on Monday, which showed Japanese manufacturers managed to raise production marginally in November thanks to a slower fall in exports. \par \par \uc2  But deflation worsened and overall business conditions deteriorated, the survey showed. \par \par \uc2  U.S. OPTIMISM \par \par \uc2  Analysts said any impact from Shiokawa's reported comments would prove short-lived unless the Japanese authorities stepped in with actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Japanese officials have tended to favour a weaker yen to bolster exports. \par \par \uc2  "Verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is ineffective," said Toru Umemoto, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Morgan Stanley in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  "What we need for a weaker yen is policy coordination, meaning the disposal of banks' bad debt, an inflation target from the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) and unsterilised \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is on track for such action, but it's not quite there yet," Umemoto said. \par \par \uc2  He expects the dollar to rise to 128 yen by the end of 2002. \par \par \uc2  Adding to the dollar's buoyancy were hopes that the week's set of U.S. economic data would cement the view that the U.S. economy was on a steady path for recovery. \par \par \uc2  Monday sees the Institute for Supply Management's U.S. manufacturing index, expected to rise to 51.3 in November against 48.5 in October. Jobs data for November are due on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Another focus is how enthusiastic U.S. consumers will be about their Christmas shopping and some surveys showed that sales got off to a good start over the weekend. \par \par \uc2  "I think the dollar's rise today is a bounce in reaction to the excessively pessimistic view on the U.S. economy a while ago," said Kota Kimura, assistant manager at Shinkin \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Treasury business division. \par \par \uc2  "Whether the U.S. economy is in a recovery phase after the Fed's aggressive rate cut, we will have to wait until next year, so I'm taking a neutral stance on the dollar," he said. \par \par \uc2  EUROPEAN RATE CUT \par \par \uc2  The yen was down against the euro, with the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 122.44/51 yen versus 121.85 in New York. Against the dollar, the euro hovered at 99.29/32 cents from 99.49. \par \par \uc2  The euro was slightly easier despite expectation that the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) would cut interest rates when its board meets on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  ECB council member Ernst Welteke said on Friday that euro zone price stability was not in danger. \par \par \uc2  "A 25 basis point rate cut is a done deal," said Umemoto at Morgan Stanley, adding that if the ECB were to cut by 50 basis points, it would prove supportive for the euro. No action would be a sell-factor for the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he said. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021203dyc20060v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
