\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar retreats after disappointing manufacturing report. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Atiya Hussain \par \uc2 796 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Dezember 2002\par 23:40\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Dec 2 (Reuters) - The dollar veered off one-month peaks against the euro and the yen on Monday after unexpectedly weak U.S. manufacturing data dented investors' hopes that the outlook for the economy was brightening. \par \par \uc2  With the yen broadly weaker because of Japanese officials' efforts to talk down their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar's retreat was sharpest against the euro. The yen fell to its lowest levels since August 1999 against the euro and set one-month lows against the dollar as investors speculated that Tokyo, in an effort to boost Japan's export-reliant economy, was stepping up a campaign to weaken the yen. \par \par \uc2  Confounding market expectations of a reading above the 50 mark that separates expansion from contraction, the Institute for Supply Management's manufacturing index edged up to 49.2 in November. The disappointing reading prompted dollar-bulls, who had hoped that a recent string of stronger-than-expected U.S. data heralded a pick-up in the world's biggest economy, to dump the dollars they earlier bought up. \par \par \uc2  "I think it definitely put a damper on the dollar. The psychology was for a stronger number and that flipped around how everyone was feeling after the strong retail sales reports over the weekend," said Greg Schwake, vice president of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Commerzbank in New York. \par \par \uc2  The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  shot up to one-week peaks not far from the psychologically important $1.00 level after data showed the U.S. factory sector contracted for the third straight month in November - rebounding nearly 1-1/2 cents from its lows. \par \par \uc2  In late U.S. trading, the euro was around $0.9970 , up 0.30 percent from the previous U.S. close, and not far from session peaks above $0.9980. \par \par \uc2  The ISM report also pulled the plug on an early stock market rally, overshadowing news from Wal-Mart Stores Inc. (WMT.N) that the Friday after Thanksgiving produced a single-day company sales record. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said continuing sluggishness in U.S. factories may not hamper the dollar for very long. \par \par \uc2  "A month ago, this number would not have looked that bad. Clearly you're seeing stabilization. Overall, I don't think it will be enough to change this overall bullish sentiment on the dollar," said Guillermo Estebanez, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of America. \par \par \uc2  Investors are awaiting the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy-setting meeting and the closely watched U.S. employment report due on Friday before making up their minds about the dollar's prospects, Estebanez added. \par \par \uc2  The market widely expects the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cut rates by up to 50 basis points when it meets on Thursday. ECB officials have been sending strong signals that they are ready to ease for the first time in over a year. \par \par \uc2  On Monday, the November Reuters Eurozone Purchasing Managers' Index held below the critical 50 level that divides expansion from contraction. Additionally, euro zone retail sales posted their biggest monthly drop in September since March 2000. \par \par \uc2  JAPANESE JAWBONING \par \par \uc2  The yen was down more than 1.50 percent against both the dollar and the euro in late U.S. trading as investors took notice of overnight comments from Masajuro Shiokawa, Japan's finance minister. Shiokawa was quoted in a Japanese newspaper as saying the yen was excessively strong and should trade around 150-160 - far lower than its current levels around 125 per dollar. \par \par \uc2  The euro was up nearly 2 percent on the day, trading above 124 yen , not far from the day's highs near 124.35 yen. The dollar was trading around 124.50 yen , up 1.60 percent but off the day's peak above 125 yen. \par \par \uc2  Identifying a target for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates is dangerous for any finance minister, but \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's tradition of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  meant Shiokawa's comments were taken particularly seriously by the market. \par \par \uc2  "That seems to be a little bit of science fiction," said Bank of America's Estebanez. "It's a very significant statement to make, but it's not something that you can target, or even envision," he added. \par \par \uc2  Some say a weak-yen policy is one of the few options left for Japan to dig itself out of its economic slump. Interest rates are already near zero and the government's huge debts restrict its ability to spend its way out of trouble. \par \par \uc2  Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief, Haruhiko Kuroda, added to dealers' sense that Japanese officials are increasingly worried. In a commentary in London's Financial Times, Kuroda called for coordinated reflation policies from the United States, Europe and Japan, urging the Bank of Japan to set an inflation target of 3 percent as well as increase liquidity in the money market. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021203dyc2000dk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
