\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar strength sapped by downbeat ISM data. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Daniel Bases \par \uc2 756 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Dezember 2002\par 20:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Dec 2 (Reuters) - The dollar dropped from a five-week high against the yen and four-week high against the euro on Monday after a weaker-than-expected November U.S. manufacturing report dampened early enthusiasm for the greenback. \par \par \uc2  Masajuro Shiokawa, Japan's finance minister, sparked early dollar buying against the yen after a Japanese newspaper quoted him saying in a speech on Sunday that the yen was excessively strong and should trade around 150-160 yen. It gave no time-frame for the desired decline from current levels around 125 yen. \par \par \uc2  Early in the U.S. session, the Institute for Supply Management's manufacturing index disappointed the market, showing U.S. manufacturing activity contracted for the third consecutive month instead of expanding, as predicted. \par \par \uc2  "I think it definitely put a damper on the dollar. The psychology was for a stronger number and that flipped around how everyone was feeling after the strong retail sales reports over the weekend," said Greg Schwake, vice president of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Commerzbank in New York. \par \par \uc2  The ISM report knocked the dollar off its ascent and pulled the plug on an early stock market rally, overshadowing news from Wal-Mart Stores Inc. (WMT.N) that the Friday after Thanksgiving produced a single-day company sales record. \par \par \uc2  "The ISM report stopped the dollar from expanding its gains made earlier, but I don't think the dollar is finished moving higher and by the latter end of this week dollar strength should resume," said Ralph DelZenero, managing director of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Bank One in Chicago. \par \par \uc2  In early afternoon, the dollar traded at 124.58 yen , a gain of 1.70 percent from Friday's New York close. The dollar had traded near the five-week high of 125.03 yen, but quickly fell back after the ISM report. \par \par \uc2  The euro, which fell as low as $0.9856, a four-week low, recouped all of its losses to trade at $0.9960, up 0.18 percent on the day. \par \par \uc2  IMM floor traders noted strong demand for euro futures contracts after the ISM data. December euros had dipped to a one-month low of $0.9854 before retracing. The contract remained negative, however, near $0.9900. \par \par \uc2  The euro hit a high of 124.09 yen , up 1.61 percent on the day and its highest level since August 1999. \par \par \uc2  While recent U.S. economic data have been more positive, barring the ISM report, the data out of Europe has not been uplifting. \par \par \uc2  On Monday, the Reuters Eurozone Purchasing Managers' Index report held below the critical 50 level that divides expansion from contraction. Additionally, euro zone retail sales posted their biggest monthly drop in September since March 2000. \par \par \uc2  The market widely expects the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cut rates by up to 50 basis points when it meets on Thursday. ECB officials have been sending strong signals they are ready to ease for the first time in over a year. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On Wall Street, the Dow Jones industrial average  fell to a loss of\par 0.27 percent, while the S&P 500        held a marginal gain. The Nasdaq\par Composite index         clung to a 1.16 percent gain.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par PLAYING WITH FIRE\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Identifying a target for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates is dangerous for any\par finance minister, but \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's tradition of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  meant\par Shiokawa's comments were taken particularly seriously by the market.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Mainichi Shimbun reported Shiokawa said the MOF could not take action\par that would directly weigh down the yen. "Therefore I hope the private\par sector would try to weigh on the yen," the report quoted him as\par saying.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "I think Shiokawa caught the market leaning toward a positive dollar,\par but this is not something that is so new," said Grant Wilson, senior\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trader at Mellon Bank in Pittsburgh.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Many analysts say a weak-yen policy, which would aid exporters, is one\par of the few options left for Japan to dig itself out of its economic\par slump. Interest rates are already near zero and the government's huge\par debts restrict its ability to spend its way out of trouble.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Senior Japanese Finance Ministry official Zembei Mizoguchi suggested\par Shiokawa may have been referring to purchasing power parity estimates,\par but his comments did little to soften the blow.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "There's little doubt that Japan needs a weaker yen and if Shiokawa put\par a number on it, he did so deliberately," said Neal Kimberley, senior\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  manager at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021202dyc2010f8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
