\par\par \b \uc2  Yen's Dive Into New Range May Fit Shiokawa's Design \b0\par\par\uc2  By John Parry \par \uc2 1,114 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Dezember 2002\par 22:23\par \uc2  Dow Jones Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- A staggering remark from Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa is starting to have the desired effect. \par \par \uc2  In a speech Sunday, Shiokawa said that an appropriate level for the dollar against the yen would be between Y150.00 and Y160.00 - at least 20% weaker than the yen's prevailing level. The dollar traded around Y146 in August 1998, but hasn't hit above Y150 since 1990. \par \par \uc2  Even allowing for Shiokawa's habit of speaking his mind,  these remarks appear to signal that the Japanese government  wants to see an even weaker yen than some had previously thought, U.S.-based \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists said Monday. Some warn that his comments may be paving the way for an eventual  policy shift. \par \par \uc2  "Throwing out numbers like Y150 and Y160 is provocative," said Tim Stewart, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with Morgan Stanley in New York. \par \par \uc2  "I suspect it is part of a broader jawboning effort to   guard against any renewed strength of the yen and push it back (weaker) to where they want to see it, which is around Y130 to the dollar," Stewart added. \par \par \uc2  Nor has Shiokawa been the only representative of his ministry in the last day to shake up perceptions of what the  government and the Bank of Japan may be contemplating. \par \par \uc2  Senior Japanese finance ministry officials Haruhiko Kuroda and Masahiro Kawai, respectively vice minister and deputy vice minister for international affairs, called on the Bank of Japan to set an inflation target of 3% in an article in the international edition of the Financial Times Monday. \par \par \uc2  One feature of any aggressive monetary policy intended to stoke such price pressures would likely be a weaker yen, in order to import inflation. \par \par \uc2  The world's second biggest economy has been trapped in a  deflationary spiral for much of the last decade. The nation's debt-burdened banks - themselves the Achilles heel of the economy - are unable to lend effectively amid stagnant business conditions. \par \par \uc2  Japan's economy and banking minister Heizo Takenaka's efforts made since October to reform the banking sector and force banks aggressively to write off the bulk of their non-performing loans and allow some creditors to fail appear  to be foundering. \par \par \uc2  A much softer \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would at least offer some hope of  supporting Japan's teetering export-led recovery by making Japanese goods more competitively priced abroad, according to analysts. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    New Numbers Took Market Aback\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Yet Shiokawa's remarks were strikingly unusual and caught the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par market off guard. The ministry seldom issues recommendations of specific\par levels at which it would like to see the yen trade.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "It's an open secret they (the Japanese government) would like to see\par a weaker yen," said Paresh Upadhyaya \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  analyst with Putnam\par Investments in Boston. But "what was new was that we got a figure this\par time around," and one which far exceeded the yen's current levels, he\par added.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Japanese government may have timed the signal carefully, choosing\par a moment when sufficient numbers of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market participants\par were making bets that the yen would appreciate - so-called "long"\par positions, Upadhyaya said. Shiokawa's rotund statement calling for a\par steep yen decline sent the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  smartly in the other direction\par because it wrong-footed those who had made such bets, Upadhyaya added.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Although some \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategists are still waiting to see whether\par Shiokawa or his ministry issue some additional, qualifying statements\par about his strikingly unusual remarks, more than a day later, they appear\par to have shunted the yen into new trading ranges against its major\par rivals.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par After making a sharp dive early in Monday's global session, the yen\par was trading at Y124.54 to the dollar mid-afternoon Monday in New York.\par That leaves the dollar still up almost two full yen from its Y122.62\par level late Friday in New York, although off the dollar's session high\par around Y125.01.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Even though the dollar only broke slightly above the Y125.00 mark,\par that resulted in a major liquidation of some options positions, clearing\par the way for the dollar to trade in a higher range against the Japanese\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , said Hugh Walsh, vice president of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  with\par Fortis Financial USA in New York.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The upward momentum of the greenback against the yen "had enough\par ammunition to take out large amounts of option positions," Walsh said.\par He estimated that there were at least $1 billion -worth of mainly exotic\par options - or \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  options with complex structures - at the\par Y125.00 mark.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Even though there was subsequently no dollar follow-through above the\par Y125.00 mark, the trading range of these two major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has\par probably shifted as a result,  Walsh said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    New Ranges\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The yen is now finding new near-term ranges against both  the dollar\par and the euro. The dollar is now in a range between around Y122.00 and\par Y126.00, while the euro is consolidating into a new range between around\par Y123.00 and Y125.00, U.S.-based \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategists consulted for this\par report said Monday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On Friday, U.S.-based \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategists had forecast the dollar\par would trade in a near term range of between around Y121.50 and Y124.00\par this week. Mid-afternoon Monday in New York the euro was trading around\par its highest levels since August 1999 against the yen at Y123.67, very\par steeply up from around Y121.89 late Friday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Underlying the yen's violent dive Monday are \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  traders' fears\par that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , acting via the   Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may move to\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets with the aim of weakening the yen,\par as the BOJ last did at the end of June.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The big question" surrounding Shiokawa's remarks is whether they are\par "the precursor for some kind of policy action that would push the dollar\par a lot higher against the yen," says Stewart. He does not expect an\par imminent shift of  this type, but warns that Shiokawa may be sowing the\par seeds for one.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par That thought may deter \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market participants from\par betting on any marked recovery of the yen from current levels,\par especially as many will be shy to hold major riskier \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market\par positions heading into the turn of the year. Amid thin markets, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central\par bank interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  can cause violent \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market moves.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par With this possible scenario in mind, "I do not want to hold a large\par position (in yen) going into very illiquid conditions," toward the end\par of this calendar year, Upadhyaya said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -By John Parry; Dow Jones Newswires\par    john.parry@dowjones.com; 201-938-2096\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1074 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020021202dyc200bq9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
