\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Japan concerns, US data fears buoy euro vs yen,dlr. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Burton Frierson \par \uc2 783 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 November 2002\par 14:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Nov 19 (Reuters) - The euro rose against the yen and the dollar on Tuesday, garnering support from growing concerns about Japanese banks and hopes of a European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate cut, which increased interest in euro zone bonds. \par \par \uc2  By the European mid-session, the euro was up half a percent against the dollar and had touched one-week highs of $1.0141. It was trading over half a percent higher on the yen and hit a one-month high of 122.80 yen. \par \par \uc2  The yen was undermined by growing concerns about the health of Japanese banks after shares in several big banks hit lifetime lows. The Bank of Japan decided to tweak the focus of a key monetary target to give banks more access to funds, but this failed to soothe fears about the banking system. \par \par \uc2  "It's the yen weakness coming through. People have chosen the euro and sterling to sell the yen against because they are still nervous about buying dollars especially before U.S. inflation and trade data this afternoon," a corporate sales manager at a London-based bank said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers noted trade was very thin with the day's main data - U.S. inflation and trade balance - due out at 1330 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan is also speaking about global finance in Washington at 1800 GMT. Some said the market may also stay cautious ahead of the Philadelphia Federal Reserve's manufacturing survey due on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  This allowed speculative players to push the euro's rally on technical factors, giving it further momentum. \par \par \uc2  "We didn't have any specific news but it's the case that the euro is still in a bullish mode and it's a product of a negative dollar and yen situation. The euro is going to target $1.0210," a European bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  U.S. international trade data is expected to show the U.S. trade gap narrowed to $37.33 billion in September, partly due to fewer imports during a strike in West Coast ports, after hitting a record $38.46 billion in August. \par \par \uc2  The October U.S. Consumer Price Index is expected to rise due to higher energy costs. \par \par \uc2  RISING HOPES OF RATE CUT \par \par \uc2  ECB Governing Council Member Ernst Welteke added to speculation that a rate cut was imminent when he said inflation was close to meeting the criteria for price stability. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the euro may be getting a boost from foreigners in the bond market positioning for an easing, which would boost the price of government debt players buy beforehand. \par \par \uc2  "Particularly Japanese investors are expressing more interest towards euro zone government bonds than they are towards the U.S.. This is partly in anticipation of a rate cut in Europe," said Aziz McMahon, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN AMRO. \par \par \uc2  "A rate cut would be seen as positive for bonds at least short term." \par \par \uc2  News euro zone industrial output suffered its first quarterly contraction of the year in the third quarter did little to slow the euro's advance. Industrial output fell less than expected in September, posting a monthly decline of 0.2 percent and an annual drop of 0.6 percent. \par \par \uc2  Production fell 0.1 percent on a quarterly basis. \par \par \uc2  Euro zone exports outstripped imports by 9.5 billion euros in September, the European Union statistics office said, and August's surplus was revised up to 10 billion euros from a previous estimate of 9.6 billion euros. \par \par \uc2  BOJ TWEAKS, MOF CONFUSES \par \par \uc2  The BOJ said it would aim to keep the amount of financial institutions' current account deposits parked at the BOJ at the upper end of a 15-20 trillion yen ($125-166 billion) range. \par \par \uc2  Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa sowed confusion in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets on Tuesday by making comments that were initially interpreted as hinting at imminent yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ). \par \par \uc2  The remarks, urging the BOJ to leave its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  "unsterilised", were taken by some traders as signalling the authorities were about to step into the market and briefly buoyed the dollar against the yen. \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa later said he was not indicating such action, but both this and the BOJ move were seen as possibly yen-negative. \par \par \uc2  "The two powerful voices here are the BOJ increasing the liquidity and the MOF rhetoric more or less encouraging a weaker yen," Steven Saywell, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Citibank. \par \par \uc2  "What we've got at the moment is more or less a signal (in the BOJ move). Once that liquidity comes into the market, we're likely to see the yen actually weaken on the back of that." \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021119dybj00m2j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
