\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 2-Shiokawa sows confusion in urging more BOJ action. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Shinichi Kishima \par \uc2 668 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 November 2002\par 06:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Nov 19 (Reuters) - Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa sowed confusion in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets on Tuesday by making comments that were initially interpreted as hinting at imminent yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ). \par \par \uc2  The remarks, urging the BOJ to leave its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  "unsterilised", were taken by some traders as signalling the authorities were about to step into the market and briefly buoyed the dollar against the yen. \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa later said he was not indicating such action. \par \par \uc2  Asked by Reuters whether his ministry was considering \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he said: "We are not." \par \par \uc2  Asked if he wanted the BOJ to leave \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds unsterilised if it acted at some stage, he said: "Yes." \par \par \uc2  In "unsterilised" \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the BOJ leaves the yen it sells for other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the banking system instead of absorbing the extra liquidity through money market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa's remarks often perplex traders, media and staff at his own ministry, and that was the effect this time, particularly as the authorities have not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market since July and the yen has retreated by itself from recent highs. \par \par \uc2  "They (BOJ) are buying government bonds and raising the current account deposit balance, but they sterilise it," Shiokawa told reporters in response to a question on monetary policy on the final day of a BOJ policy board meeting. \par \par \uc2  The link between sterilisation and the BOJ's money market policy was unclear. \par \par \uc2  "Reading the comments, I cannot figure out what Minister Shiokawa was trying to say," said a dealer at a Japanese trust bank. \par \par \uc2  EASING OR \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2 ? \par \par \uc2  Initial reports on Shiokawa's comments prompted the dollar to blip up about 0.3 yen and rise above 121 yen as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers took it as a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy statement. At 0540 GMT it had steadied around 120.95 yen. \par \par \uc2  "The first impression was that he was urging further monetary easing," said a trader at a Japanese bank. Monetary easing, in theory, would lead to a weakening of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "But...then we started to wonder why Shiokawa was talking about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  when they haven't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which raised fears of an actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  The BOJ, which expanded its 20-month-old "quantitative easing" policy last month, already floods the market with funds and keeps short-term interest rates at virtually zero. \par \par \uc2  Within hours of Shiokawa's initial comments, the BOJ stepped up its monetary policy support for the frail economy by saying it now aimed for the upper end of the target range for its supply of funds to the system instead of the middle of the range. \par \par \uc2  The range - for the balance of current account deposits that banks keep at the BOJ - was raised last month to 15-20 trillion yen ($125-166 billion) from 10-15 trillion yen and BOJ Governor Masaru Hayami said at the time that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would aim for the middle of it for a while. \par \par \uc2  At 20 trillion yen, the funds in the non-interest-bearing accounts would be more than triple the legally required reserves. \par \par \uc2  MORE CONFUSION \par \par \uc2  The liquidity comment was not the only confusing remark Shiokawa made at the news conference. \par \par \uc2  Speaking about consumer prices, the 81-year-old minister reiterated his view that a return to 1997 levels was desirable but it was unclear whether he was talking about price levels or the rate of price increases. \par \par \uc2  "I have been calling for 1997 levels. Consumer prices were 1.2 (percent) in 1997," Shiokawa said. \par \par \uc2  In the past, he had called for a return to 1997 price levels - not the inflation rate. The same price target had been proposed at a number of BOJ policy meetings by then-BOJ Policy Board member Nobuyuki Nakahara, whose term expired last March. \par \par \uc2  Most other inflation-target advocates call for the BOJ to set an inflation rate objective of, say, two to three percent, rather than absolute price levels, as a way of fighting deflation. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021119dybj0021z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
