\par\par \b \uc2  General Motors/Dollar -2: Decline Not Happening Quickly \b0\par\par\uc2 764 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 November 2002\par 20:29\par \uc2  Dow Jones Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par By John Parry\par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Japanese government policy is the key obstacle\par preventing the dollar from steepening its decline against other major\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , a senior executive with General Motors said Thursday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  "The biggest roadblock to the correction of the dollar is the Japanese government,"  said Mustafa Mohatarem, General Motors Corp (GM)'s chief economist, in an interview. \par \par \uc2  He was speaking about the impact of exchange rate movements on the business of General Motors and other U.S. automakers. The dollar's decline against its major rivals so  far this year "clearly is a very welcome development, but it is not happening as quickly as we would like nor as much as we would like," Mohatarem added. \par \par \uc2  So far this year, the dollar has lost about 11% against the euro and 9% to the yen, but many economists and manufacturers say the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains overvalued. In fact, the dollar's trade-weighted index is still about 23% stronger than in 1995, when the dollar began its bull run. \par \par \uc2  Detroit's Big Three automakers - GM, Ford Motor Co. (F) and DaimlerChrysler AG (DCX) - have been among the most vociferous advocates of an even weaker dollar in order to make their products more competitive both at home and abroad. They have vigorously protested the competitive edge a weaker yen has lent Japanese automakers, who this year, for the first time, account for more than a quarter of the U.S. market. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. companies are tending to place the blame not on  the superior cost structure of the Japanese manufacturers, but the yen's relatively weak exchange rate. \par \par \uc2  "Absent threats of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 " in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar would decline to roughly Y100.00 against the yen from its current level around Y120.33, were \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets left to their own devices, Mohatarem added. \par \par \uc2  A dramatic change of Japan's policy in this area is unlikely, however, given the Japanese authorities' deep opposition to a strong yen. The Bank of Japan - which acts on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  in such \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market  \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  - has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  seven times between May 22 and June 28 in a bid to stall the buoyant yen from snuffing out the nation's nascent export-led economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  With the dollar now again under broad pressure, Japanese  policymakers have been stepping up their verbal warnings during the past few trading sessions, noting their willingness to step into the market again in an effort to weaken the yen. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets should be allowed to push the dollar unimpeded by market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from any government,   Mohatarem added. "I do not want the U.S. government to take any action to weaken the dollar: we should let the market do that," he said. \par \par \uc2  Treasury Secretary Paul O'Neill has repeatedly stressed that markets should set exchange rates, rendering the possibility of dollar-weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  remote in many \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders' minds. \par \par \uc2  Against the euro, the dollar will depreciate over the next year more gently than against the yen, because its exchange rate already reflects the fact that the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  hasn't attempted to check the euro's ascent, Mohatarem said. The euro should rise to around $1.10 in a year's time from its $1.0051 level early Thursday afternoon in New York, he forecasts. \par \par \uc2  The economist expressed the hope that the U.S. government can persuade \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to desist from \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market  \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  because allowing the yen to weaken further would provide an important boost to U.S. companies. \par \par \uc2  Rather than fiscal measures, "if you truly wanted to stimulate the U.S. economy, a weakening of the U.S. dollar would probably have a bigger impact," he said. \par \par \uc2  One key factor Mohatarem cited that is expected to drag the dollar broadly lower is the U.S. current account deficit, currently running at more than 5.0% of gross domestic product. That contrasts with current account surpluses in the euro zone and Japan, which places the greenback at a disadvantage against its two major rivals, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists maintain. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -By John Parry; Dow Jones Newswires\par    john.parry@dowjones.com; 201-938-2096\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par GM economist Mustafa Mohatarem expressed the hope that the U.S.\par government can persuade \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to desist from \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , because allowing the dollar to weaken further would\par provide an important boost to U.S. companies.\par \f28 