\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar Closes Mixed Against Rivals Amid Fed Officials' Remarks \b0\par\par\uc2 829 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 November 2002\par 00:15\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -- The dollar split its performance against its major rivals Tuesday, after trading skittishly in narrow ranges amid a blizzard of remarks by Federal Reserve officials that left \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders with a blurred view of the Federal Reserve's stance on the U.S. economy's prospects. \par \par \uc2  Late Tuesday afternoon in New York, the dollar was trading at 119.62 yen, modestly higher than 119.50 yen late Monday in New York. The euro was trading at $1.0114, a touch higher than $1.0103 late Monday. Sterling was at $1.5880, up from $1.5861. \par \par \uc2  The chorus of Fed officials who spoke Tuesday intimated that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's aggressive easing last week has delivered enough monetary stimulus to the economy, but failed to give the dollar clear direction. \par \par \uc2  "Last week's decision to ease further was aimed at increasing the chances that economic recovery would stay on track and not be derailed by the latest soft spot," said Federal Reserve Vice Chairman Roger Ferguson. \par \par \uc2  Federal Reserve Bank of Minneapolis President Gary Stern said accommodative monetary policy was having a particular effect on the interest-rate sensitive areas of the economy, namely housing and durable goods. \par \par \uc2  Fed speakers "have reiterated the very unspecific phrase of this 'soft spot' we are going through and have made us all even more focused on Greenspan" when he makes a scheduled appearance before the Joint Economic Committee in Washington on Wednesday, said Lara Rhame, senior economist with Brown Brothers Harriman in New York. Mr. Greenspan was silent about the U.S. economic outlook during an appearance in Mexico City on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, the dollar made some tentative gains after Japanese officials stepped up their jawboning with the aim of weakening the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . By contrast with Fed speakers, Japanese officials gave \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets more direction Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  In recent days, several key Japanese finance ministry officials have sought to verbally break the yen's sudden ascent against the dollar, a phenomenon that often tends to preempt actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  via the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But while \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts reckon the comments can't be ignored, most don't believe that the government is set to match words with action just yet. \par \par \uc2  "Japanese officials would have to be really upset, and I think right now they're just trying to prevent the yen from getting too much stronger rather than targeting any specific [exchange-rate] level," said Carl Weinberg, chief economist at High Frequency Economics in Valhalla, N.Y. \par \par \uc2  One sign of the dollar's vulnerability should hostilities begin was its inability to rally convincingly against the euro even after a particularly dismal economic report from the euro-zone's biggest economy, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The Zentrum fuer Europaeische Wirtschaftsforschung, or ZEW, index of economic expectations slumped to 4.2 from 23.4 the previous month. That reading was "atrocious, but one of the reasons why the dollar remains under pressure is the Iraqi issue, which has kept the dollar on edge" said John McCarthy, director of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at ING Barings Capital Markets in New York. \par \par \uc2  Others cited the failure of U.S. equity markets to hold onto most of their earlier gains as contributing to the dollar losing some ground against the euro in late trading. The Dow Jones Industrial Average, up nearly 150 points earlier in the session, ended with a gain of 27.05 at 8386. \par \par \uc2  The dollar has lost considerable ground since the Federal Reserve delivered its surprisingly deep half-percentage-point interest-rate cut Wednesday. That kicked wider the already substantial interest-rate differentials between the U.S. and many other countries, rendering it less attractive to hold dollar-based assets. The easing also stoked concerns about the fragility of the U.S. economy, which have also weighed broadly on the dollar. \par \par \uc2  But the dollar's decline against the yen has been particularly acute. The greenback has dropped 2.5% versus the yen from its low point when the Fed announced its rate cut, compared with just 1.5% against the euro and 1.9% versus the pound. \par \par \uc2  Concerned that a strong yen could undermine the sputtering cyclical recovery in Japan, Japanese finance officials have jumped into action, at least verbally, placing the threat of actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  front and center in traders' minds once again. A weaker yen helps Japanese exporters, which are a linchpin for economic recovery in the world's second largest economy. \par \par \uc2  "If such movements continue to accelerate, we will need to take appropriate steps," said Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa earlier Tuesday. His comments came a day after vice finance minister for international affairs Haruhiko Kuroda and Japanese Finance Ministry international bureau chief Zembei Mizoguchi each made statements in a similar vein. \par \par \uc2  Write to John Parry at john.parry@dowjones.com and Tyler Lifton at tyler.lifton@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 2002 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020021112dybc008sp\par }\pa