\par\par \b \uc2  EUROPEAN MORNING BRIEFING: Iraq Worry Predominates \b0\par\par\uc2 2,651 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 November 2002\par 06:58\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par US SUMMARY: Wall Street Sentiment Still Dicey\par DJIA          8358.95    loss 178.18    dn 2.1%\par NASDAQ        1319.19    loss 40.09     dn 3.0%\par S&P 500       876.19     loss 18.55     dn 2.1%\par Dow Future    8384.00    gain 29.00     up 0.4%\par NASDAQ Future 977.50     loss 3.00      dn 0.3%\par S&P Future    880.30     gain 1.70      up 0.3%\par 10-Yr US Treasury: 3.80% loss 0.05\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- In the absence of a closed Treasurys market, stocks got pummelled. \par \par \uc2  STOCKS: Escalating concerns about a war with Iraq dominated Wall Street Monday, sending stocks sliding Monday in light Veterans Day trading. \par \par \uc2  "If you look at investor sentiment, we've switched to more complacency. Also you now have Iraq taking center stage," said Steven Goldman, chief market strategist at Weeden & Co. "That uncertainty is causing hesitation for investors." \par \par \uc2  "I think we'll have a very choppy environment in the near term," he said. \par \par \uc2  Senior administration officials said President Bush has approved tentative Pentagon plans involving up to 250,000 troops invading Iraq should a new U.N. arms inspection effort fail. \par \par \uc2  "Ideally, I think people would like to see Saddam and his cabinet say, 'Come on in,'" said Charles White, portfolio manager at Avatar Associates. "Realistically, investors are wanting to see, if Iraq refuses, a quick and decisive action, one in which there is a coalition of countries that endorse the action and not the U.S. and Britain acting bilaterally to do this." \par \par \uc2  "The market should not, absent any real material change in the (economic) fundamentals, make much headway in one direction or another," White added. "But we're one geopolitical headline away from seeing the market up for sale again." \par \par \uc2  Decliners included Hewlett-Packard, which dropped $1.83 to $14.85 after its president, Michael Capellas, quit the computer company. The move followed a Wall Street Journal report that he was a frontrunner to become bankrupt WorldCom's new chief executive. \par \par \uc2  BONDS: After the holiday-closed U.S. session, Treasurys were steadier in Tokyo after early gains on Iraq war talk and a rally in European bonds Monday. Further gains this session were viewed as unlikely until Wall Street stocks open later. But experts look for more gains this week in Treasurys on weak economic data later this week. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ASIAN SUMMARY: Tokyo Markets Drift, New Threat Over Yen\par USD-Yen             119.74       gain  0.10    up 0.08%\par AUD-USD             0.5602       loss  0.0013  dn 0.3%\par Nikkei 225          8455.99      loss  3.50    dn 0.04%\par Hang Seng           9637.78      gain  55.90   up 0.6%\par JGB Yield           0.9550% unchanged\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Tokyo share prices drifted Tuesday amid concern\par about the fallout of a possible U.S. war with Iraq.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Major banks Mizuho Holdings, Mitsubishi Tokyo Financial Group, and UFJ\par were heavily sold in morning trading.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Sellers took their cue from Wall Street, where investors worried about\par market risks associated with a possible war with Iraq locked in profits\par on Monday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But buying by public pension funds later supported the Tokyo market,\par the traders said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar stabilized after more official jawboning.\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets if recent volatility\par continues, Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa said Tuesday,\par adding that he believes the yen is currently slightly overvalued against\par the dollar.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "If I may be truthful, I think that the yen is slightly strong"\par against the dollar, Shiokawa said. However, he was quick to add that the\par U.S. also has its own views on the dollar, and that it wasn't\par appropriate for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to have a psychological impact on\par economies.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In any case, "(dollar) movements of Y6 within a span of two weeks is\par too rapid," he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par EUROPEAN OUTLOOK: Shares Seen Lower As Euro Tests Higher\par Euro-USD            1.0091      loss  0.0018  dn 0.2%\par Stlg-USD            1.5851      loss  0.0022  dn 0.1%\par USD-Franc           1.4498      gain  0.0021  up 0.1%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ZURICH (Dow Jones)--European shares should open slightly lower after\par Wall Street's losses, while government bonds start a bit higher and the\par euro probes higher against the dollar.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par STOCKS: European shares are expected to open somewhat lower, with\par techs especially under pressure. The latest round of corporate results\par includes Vodafone, Bayer, Commerzbank and UBS.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par European stocks were weaker Monday as financial stocks slumped on\par fears that some of the sector's biggest names will post record losses\par this week.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Credit Suisse, the second largest Swiss bank, fell heavily Monday\par after weekend reports said the group would post a bigger-than-expected\par third-quarter loss Thursday. Allianz, Europe's biggest insurer, is\par expected to post a hefty loss the same day.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Commerzbank, Germany's third-biggest listed bank, is also set to\par publish another quarterly operating loss Tuesday, adding to the sector's\par woes.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But UBS, reporting Tuesday, should prove to be a bright spot in the\par financial sector's dark outlook.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par BONDS: Euro-zone government bond prices should open slightly higher\par ahead of key German figures and U.S. Federal Reserve speakers later.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Government bonds were lower Monday, on mild profit-taking following\par gains late Friday in line with U.S. Treasurys.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But the short end remained stable on expectations that the European\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will have to cut interest rates by at least 25 basis points\par by the end of the year.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par As a result, the yield curve has actually steepened by one basis\par point, even though the trend points to a flatter curve on the view that\par the ECB would probably only cut rates once in the current cycle.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But the market is expected to perk up Tuesday with the U.S. markets\par reopening and a range of Federal Reserve officials due to speak,\par including Chairman Alan Greenspan and Vice Chairman Alan Ferguson.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par These Fed officials should provide some interest for the short ends of\par both the Treasurys and euro-zone government bond markets Tuesday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Market participants will also be watching the German ZEW index of\par financial analysts' confidence for November, which could be weaker than\par many have expected.\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : The euro opens the session just under $1.01, having essentially\par stalled against the dollar for now. A German ZEW figure wide of\par expectations could lead to a blip in the euro.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets showed a muted reaction to the Iraqi parliament's\par recommendation to reject the new United Nations resolution, which\par demands Iraq cooperate with U.N. inspectors hunting for weapons of mass\par destruction or face "serious consequences."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par CALENDAR: Tuesday, Nov 12: German ZEW; Greenspan, Other Feds\par ET/GMT\par 0200/0700 U.K.         First-Half Earnings\par 0200/0700 Germany      Sep Trade Balance (seen EUR9.5 Bln)\par 0200/0700 Germany      Sep Current Account (seen EUR2.1 Bln)\par 0430/0930 U.K.         Oct Retailers Price Index (seen +0.1%)\par 0615/1115 U.K.         BoE Euro Bill auction results\par 0745/1245 U.S.         BTM-UBS Warburg Chain Store Sales\par 0900/1400 Germany      November ZEW (seen 15.0, last 23.4)\par 0900/1400 U.S.         Redbook Retail Sales for Nov. 9 Week\par 0940/1440 U.S.         Fed Chairman Greenspan speaks\par 0945/1445 U.S.         Fed Vice Chairman Ferguson speaks\par 1000/1500 U.S.         October Richmond Fed Manufacturing\par 1100/1600 U.S.         October Kansas City Fed Manufacturing\par 1200/1700 U.S.         Sep Chicago Fed Midwest Manufacturing\par 1300/1800 U.S.         Fed Governor Bies speaks\par 1300/1800 U.S.         Fed Governor Olson speaks\par 1315/1815 U.S.         Philadelphia Fed President Santomero\par 1850/2350 Japan        July-September Gross Domestic Product\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Dennis Baker; Dow Jones Newswires; 41-1-212-2181;\par dennis.baker@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Vodafone Group (VOD): 1H earnings\par Consensus pretax profit (DJ, 6 analysts): GBP3.52B (GBP3.01B)\par Consensus sales: GBP14.6B (GBP8.9B)\par Note: Analysts concerned about weak revenues from Japan, but per-user\par revenues expected to be flat or up in most other units. Hopes for\par return to growth in Germany.\par Bayer (G.BAY): 3Q Earnings Press Conf., Leverkusen\par Consensus net profit (DJ, 8 analysts): EUR632M (EUR183M)\par Consensus sales: EUR7.78B (EUR6.93B)\par Note: Bayer AG's third quarter net profit is expected to be well up\par over last year on the back of a substantial one-off gain, but analysts\par point to continuing weakness in its core business. Some analysts say\par finding a partner for the business could be difficult, due to the\par company's weak late-stage pipeline.\par Repsol-YPF SA (REP): 3Q Earnings\par Consensus net profit (DJ): EUR325.2M (EUR432M)\par Note: Analysts will be looking beyond the results - saying these are\par largely determined by events beyond the company's control - to focus\par on actions Repsol has taken to reduce its towering debt level. At the\par end of the first half, Repsol said it had net debt of EUR8.96 billion,\par down from EUR20.41 billion at the end of the first half of 2001.\par Commerzbank (G.CBK): 9-Month Earnings\par Consensus net profit (DJ, 5 analysts): EUR350M (EUR184M loss)\par Note: VWD said Monday that Germany's fourth largest bank posted a\par third-quarter pretax loss of just over EUR130 million, compared with a\par pretax loss of EUR279 million in the year-earlier period. Analysts have\par been calling on the bank to get rid of its investment banking\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , so news of cost cutting in that unit will be welcome.\par Further, some analysts worry that the bank may have to write-off\par devaluations in its equity portfolio, since markets hit year-lows in\par the third quarter.\par UBS (Z.UBS): 3Q Earnings\par Consensus net profit (DJ, 5 analysts): CHF862M (CHF903M)\par Consensus sales: CHF7.9B (CHF8.7B)\par Note: "Unfortunately the bank has to publish 3Q results for the period\par ending just when world equity markets were hitting new lows," Sarasin\par analyst says. This'll have a negative effect particularly on\par asset-based fees in Private Banking and securities commissions income.\par UBS had good 2Q, but Sarasin says it "has suffered more from these\par adverse conditions" in 3Q.\par AXA (AXA): 9 months revenue\par Consensus revenue (DJ, 5 analysts): EUR57.4B (EUR55.5B)\par Note: Impact of higher tariffs on property & casualty will be\par particularly eyed, as will impact of falling markets on savings\par division.\par National Bank of Greece (NBG): 9-Mo earnings\par Consensus pretax profit (DJ, 5 analysts): EUR296.5M (EUR570.2M)\par Note: National Bank's heavy dependence on stock market activities seen\par behind the drop. Early retirement scheme of EUR21M seen included in\par data, but as an extraordinary expense, according to bank's officials.\par The bank is overstaffed and more voluntary redundancies are expected\par in future, say analysts.\par OMV (R.OMV): 9 mos earnings\par Consensus net profit (DJ, 3 analysts): EUR81.8M (EUR98M)\par Consensus sales: EUR1.84B (EUR1.86B)\par Note: Analysts cite the considerably higher oil price last year and\par still low margins. What will be interesting will be to hear about\par OMV's acquisition plans, for which it has reportedly set aside about\par EUR1B. OMV is bidding for Croatian oil company INA and is also\par interested in Czech Unipetrol.\par Boehler-Uddeholm (R.BUD): 9 mos earnings\par Consensus net profit (DJ, 3 analysts): EUR39.5M (EUR52.4M)\par Consensus sales: EUR1.09B (EUR1.15B)\par Note: Analysts cite seasonality and continued weak demand for tool\par steel. Say continued pressure on prices will hurt earnings. Important\par focus will be development of earnings in 3Q from 2Q.\par Mediaset (I.MEI) 3Q earnings\par Consensus pretax loss (DJ, 3 analysts): EUR9.4M (EUR13.6M)\par Consensus sales: EUR365M (EUR356M)\par Note: "Slightly increased revenues and the announced cost-cutting\par policy should guarantee improved results," says analyst, adding that\par such results are widely expected and no particular guidance on 03 is\par expected.\par BOC Group (BOX): FY earnings\par Consensus pretax profit (Multex, 17 analysts): GBP411.5M (GBP483.9M)\par Forecast range: GBP362M - GBP505M\par Consensus sales: GBP3.86B (GBP3.77B)\par Enel (EN): Board meets on 3Q Earnings\par Consensus net profit (DJ, 3 analysts): EUR1.55B (EUR3.64B)\par Recoletos (E.RES): 9-Month Earnings\par Consensus pretax profit (DJ, 3 analysts): EUR55.1M (EUR4.9M)\par Consensus sales: EUR167M (EUR180M)\par Note: Business and finance advertising seen very weak. Focus on\par advertising outlook for rest of year key.\par TPI (E.TFP): 9-Month Earnings\par Consensus net profit (DJ, 3 analysts): EUR56.9M (EUR46.4M)\par Note: Morgan Stanley's London-based analyst Javier Marin - with highest\par net profit forecast of EUR62M - cites growth in Chile and sustained\par improvement in Spain. Adds results should be "innocuous" for shares,\par while all eyes will be on the company's analysts conference call Wed.\par Analysts also cite some negative \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  implications on results,\par particularly in Brazil.\par Egis (R.EGS): 3Q earnings\par Consensus net profit (DJ, 8 analysts): HUF1.89B (HUF1.42B)\par Note: Operating margin seen under pressure from weak dollar and\par stagnating active ingredient sales, while sales growth in Russia and\par Central Europe continues to accelerate. Slipping margins seen offset\par by hedging contracts, which should generate a small, HUF300M gain\par after last year's loss.\par SAS (S.SAI): 3Q Earnings\par Consensus pretax profit (DJ, 4 analysts): SEK277M (SEK421M)\par Forecast range: SEK198M - SEK445M\par Note: Focus will be on FY profit forecast and whether cost cuts are on\par track. SAS, which said Fri it will cut another 2,700 jobs on top of\par already announced cost savings, may need to cut costs even more in\par light of weak outlook, says analyst.\par Logista (E.LOG): To Pay 2002 EUR0.38 Dividend\par Note: A gross dividend of EUR0.38 a share for the 2002 financial year\par will be paid out. This is 15% more than the EUR0.33 dividend paid out\par last year, but lower than the EUR0.45 dividend received by\par shareholders in 2000.\par Emap (U.EMA): 1H earnings\par Consensus pretax profit (DJ, 15 analysts): GBP82M (GBP70M)\par Note: Emap said Sep that 1H revenues will be up 5%. Recently returned\par to FTSE-100 after ill-fated purchase and then sale of Petersen.\par Investors will look for news on plans for radio, the search for new\par CEO, update on advertising outlook.\par Luminar (U.LMR): 1H earnings\par Consensus pretax profit (DJ, 3 analysts): GBP32.9M (GBP29.5M)\par Forecast range: GBP32.8M - GBP33M\par Note: Analysts looking at current trading for evidence of deterioration\par since the last reporting period.\par Northern Foods (U.NFD): 1H earnings\par Consensus pretax profit (DJ, 3 analysts): GBP39.5M (GBP38.4M)\par Note: Given 3Q bias to trading, analysts will be looking for hints of\par strong Christmas trading.\par Powderject Pharmaceuticals (U.PJP): 1H earnings\par Note: Analysts aren't giving specific forecasts, but say likely to show\par Powderject on track for its own target of GBP20M FY pretax profit. 1H\par seen stronger than 2H as much revenue come from sales of seasonal flu\par vaccine. Richard Parkes at ING wants clarity on talks with potential\par bidders for Powderject, and whether GlaxoSmithKline (GSK) will\par continue to collaborate on DNA-based vaccines. Adds changing labeling\par on withdrawn tuberculosis vaccine will make little difference to\par financial outlook as Powderject unlikely to regain lost UK government\par tuberculosis contract.\par Air France (F.AFG): 1H revenue\par Generale de Sante (F.GNS): 3Q revenue\par Unilog (F.UNL): 9 months revenue\par Beiersdorf (G.BEI): 3Q Earnings\par CE Consumer Electronics (G.CEL): 3Q Earnings\par Eckert & Ziegler (G.EZS): 3Q Earnings\par GPC Biotech (G.GPC): 9-Mo Earnings\par Stinnes (G.STN): 3Q Earnings\par Elan Corp PLC (ELN): EGM For Sale Of N American Abelcet Rights\par Hunter Douglas (N.HDA): 3Q Earnings\par Priority Telecom (N.PRI): 3Q Earnings\par BRE Bank (R.RBE): 3Q earnings\par Pekao SA (R.BPK): 3Q earnings\par Sika (Z.SIK): Letter to Shareholders\par AIT Group (U.AIG): 1H earnings\par Business Post Group (U.BPG): 1H earnings\par Intechnology (U.ITO): 1H earnings\par MMT Computing (U.MMT): FY earnings\par Netstore (U.NES): 1Q earnings\par Penna Consulting (U.PNA): 1H earnings\par South Staffordshire Group (U.SFF): 1H earnings\par Viridian (U.VRD): 1H earnings\par Whitehead Mann (U.WMN): 1H earnings\par Wyndeham Press Group (U.WYP): 1H earnings\par ICM Computer Group (U.ICM): AGM\par Smiths Group (U.SIN): AGM\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1011 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020021112dybc000ay\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
