\par\par \b \uc2  More Jawboning, But  \highlight22\uc2 Japan Intervention \highlight\uc2  Not Seen Imminent \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tyler Lifton \par \uc2 1,056 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 November 2002\par 21:12\par \uc2  Dow Jones Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- They're baaaaack. \par \par \uc2  Japanese government officials threatening to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen, that is. In recent days, several key Japanese finance ministry officials have sought to verbally break the yen's sudden ascent against the dollar, a phenomenon that often tends to preempt actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  via the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But while \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts reckon the comments can't be  ignored, most don't believe that the government is set to match words with action just yet. \par \par \uc2  "Japanese officials would have to be really upset, and I think right now they're just trying to prevent the yen from getting too much stronger rather than targeting any specific (exchange rate) level," said Carl Weinberg, chief economist at High Frequency Economics in Valhalla, New York. \par \par \uc2  "We probably have a couple of yen still to fall where we could plausibly expect it to happen," he said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar has lost considerable ground since the Federal Reserve delivered a surprise half percentage point interest rate cut on Wednesday. That kicked wider the already substantial interest rate differentials between the U.S. and elsewhere, rendering it less attractive to hold dollar-based assets. The easing also stoked concerns about the vulnerability of the U.S. economy, which have also weighed broadly on the dollar. \par \par \uc2  But the dollar's decline against the yen has been particularly acute. The greenback has dropped 2.5% versus the yen from its low point when the Fed announced its rate cut, compared with just 1.5% against the euro, 1.93% versus the pound and 1.7% against the Swiss franc. \par \par \uc2  Concerned that a strong yen could undermine the sputtering cyclical recovery in Japan, Japanese finance officials have jumped into action, at least verbally, placing the threat of actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  front and center in traders' minds once again. A weaker yen helps Japanese exporters, which are a lynchpin for economic recovery in the  world's second largest economy. \par \par \uc2  "If such movements continue to accelerate, we will need  to take appropriate steps," said Finance Minister Masajuro  Shiokawa earlier Tuesday. His comments came a day after vice finance minister for international affairs Haruhiko Kuroda and Japanese Finance Ministry international bureau chief Zembei Mizoguchi each made statements in a similar vein. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Pace More Important Than Size\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par For now, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  analysts don't view the comments as a sign\par that the BOJ is about to step into the market just yet, although that\par could change it the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appreciated too swiftly.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "If the yen was to continue to move at the speed at which it was\par appreciating last week," the potential for Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would\par be greater, said David Durrant, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Bank Julius\par Baer.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But the latest volley of verbal threats has already slowed down the\par appreciation of the yen. "So that part of it has already been\par successful," Durrant said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Durrant said \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would be more likely between Y116 and Y118.\par If the dollar were to fall to around Y116 in the next week or so, for\par instance, it "would be a pace that  would be alarming" enough to\par potentially trigger \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , concurred Jason Bonanca, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign\par exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  analyst at Credit Suisse First Boston.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par By far the most ardent practitioner of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  among the\par world's most powerful nations, the Bank of Japan - acting on behalf of\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  - has \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  seven times this year to\par stall the yen's rise. The BOJ \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  once during that time via the\par European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and the Federal Reserve.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Japanese authorities have made it clear in the past that they, in\par particular, oppose rapid movements in the yen's rate against the dollar.\par The fact that the dollar appears to have stabilized against the yen now\par makes \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  somewhat less likely.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par What's more, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken the yen now would be  running\par against a tide of broad dollar declines, unlike in previous cases when\par the Japanese government acted to check independent yen strength against\par the dollar.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On Tuesday afternoon in New York, the dollar was worth Y119.59, up\par slightly from Y119.50 late Monday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Further To Fall?\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The yen's sharp rise is partially attributable to the large number of\par bearish positions that had been built up against the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in recent\par months ahead of what many analysts anticipated would be a `hard landing'\par reform program to overhaul the country's struggling banks. Analysts\par feared a wrenching reform program could unleash a wave of bankruptcies\par in Japan, weighing further on the economy and yen in the short term.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In effect however, Japan's new banking czar Heizo Takenaka proposed a\par package of noncommittal measures that was big on ideas but short on\par concrete plans. The lack of a convincing reform package sparked a\par reversal in yen positions in the market that was only exacerbated by the\par dollar's reversal of fortunes.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par If the BOJ were to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the days or weeks ahead,  the overall\par level of uncertainty currently at work in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets will play in\par favor of the BOJ should it \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , according to Bank Julius Baer's\par Durrant.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Speculators would have the tendency not to fight a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 " when\par they lack conviction, Durrant said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "In periods of time when there's more conviction, speculators and\par asset managers are much more lined up in one  direction," presenting a\par more formidable obstacle for a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to overcome, Durrant noted.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But as it stands, short yen positions would probably be removed\par quickly upon word that the BOJ is \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , he said. .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The BOJ has had somewhat of a checkered history with \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . In\par September 2001, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2   repeatedly, buying dollars,\par as well as euro, for yen. Although the bank lost the element of surprise\par by its regular appearances in the market, it did manage to ultimately\par push the dollar higher.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the period between January 1999 and April 2000 however, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central\par bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  stepped into the market at least 18 times, including once via the\par Fed and ECB. But the market  largely ignored the waves of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par and the yen continued to strengthen, hitting almost Y100 to the dollar\par by April 2000.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Tyler Lifton, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-2248\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -tyler.lifton@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020021112dybc00b9m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
