\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar hits 2-month low despite Japanese jawboning. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Hideyuki Sano \par \uc2 793 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 November 2002\par 09:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Nov 11 (Reuters) - The dollar slid to a two-month low against the yen on Monday, weighed down by simmering worries about the U.S. economy and chances of military action by the United States against Iraq. \par \par \uc2  Its recent slide worried Japanese officials, who stepped up verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday morning, but to no avail, with many traders sceptical that they will act soon. \par \par \uc2  The dollar fell to 119.51 yen, beyond Friday's low of 119.54. \par \par \uc2  At 0640 GMT it was quoted at 119.65/70 yen compared with Friday's late U.S. level of 119.79/87 yen. \par \par \uc2  The euro was little changed and was quoted at $1.0124/29 against $1.0129/34, off a three-and-a-half-month high of $1.0149 set on Friday. It fetched 121.27/39 yen against 121.26/43 in late U.S. trade. \par \par \uc2  Dollar sentiment has been deteriorating as the market factors in a series of sell signals, including an increasingly likely war between Iraq and the United States, interest rate spreads and a widening current account gap in the United States. \par \par \uc2  "In short, there is no benefit for the United States to have a strong dollar at the moment," said Koichi Ono, an economist at Daiwa Institute of Research. "The dollar will remain capped for the time being." \par \par \uc2  The dollar's latest fall prompted Japanese officials to step up warnings about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as a stronger yen puts pressure on a frail Japanese recovery that is heavily reliant on exports. \par \par \uc2  Top financial diplomat Haruhiko Kuroda said Japan would take necessary action to correct what he called "totally inappropriate" recent exchange rate moves. \par \par \uc2  "The movements on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market over the past several days run counter to the correction that had been taking place previously to the yen's excessive strength of the past. They are totally inappropriate," Kuroda said. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  SEEN UNLIKELY \par \par \uc2  The reminder that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  stands ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  briefly propelled the dollar above 120 yen, but it soon fell back as bearish fundamentals returned to weigh on it. \par \par \uc2  Many traders see \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  unlikely at this point, saying the dollar's fall so far has been too slow and too limited to trigger an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The government wants to stop the yen's appreciation but it also wants to make \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  effective. So I guess it is now waiting for an opportune time," said Ken Fujii, a \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Mitsubishi Trust Bank. \par \par \uc2  Dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  tend to be effective when traders' positions are skewed to dollar short positions, as an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  causes massive short-squeezing. \par \par \uc2  Traders said market positions appeared more balanced. The U.S. Commodity Futures Trading Commission reported last Friday that speculators in IMM yen futures still had yen short positions on November 5. \par \par \uc2  Still, some traders think Japan could step in as the yen's further rise against the dollar could damage exports - the only bright spot in Japan's shaky recovery. \par \par \uc2  Data on Monday showed Japan's machinery orders rose more strongly than expected in September, jumping 12.7 percent from August. But economist and government officials were cautious alike about the outlook. \par \par \uc2  The government forecasts a 6.5 percent drop in the October-December quarter from the previous quarter. \par \par \uc2  "Looking at the figures, I would conclude that Japan's fragile recovery may have peaked in July-September and is likely to have run out of steam in this quarter," said Yasushi Okada, chief economist at Credit Suisse First Boston. \par \par \uc2  U.S. ECONOMIC HEALTH \par \par \uc2  Looking ahead, the market will be preoccupied by the situation in the Middle East after the U.N. Security Council voted unanimously for a tough U.S.-initiated resolution giving Iraq one last chance to disarm or face the consequences. \par \par \uc2  Iraq's parliament will meet in emergency session on Monday to decide on a response. \par \par \uc2  Also important is data on the health of the U.S. economy this week, including October retail sales and industrial production, as well as the University of Michigan consumer confidence index for November. \par \par \uc2  Another closely watched event will be congressional testimony by Fed Chairman Alan Greenspan on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  In other trade, the dollar was also buried near its lowest level in more than three months against the Swiss franc, quoted at 1.4425/35 francs, little changed from its late U.S. level. \par \par \uc2  Sterling was well-supported near a two-year high at $1.5910/20 against Friday's $1.5910/20. \par \par \uc2  All \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  news ... Debt... Indicators... \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reports.. \par \par \uc2  All spots \par \par \uc2  Tokyo spots \par \par \uc2  Europe spots \par \par \uc2  Volatilities \par \par \uc2  Tokyo \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market info from BOJ \par \par \uc2  World \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  news \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  Official rates...\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2