\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  ready to  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\uc2  as dollar weakens vs yen. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Mariko Hayashibara \par \uc2 689 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 November 2002\par 04:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Nov 11 (Reuters) - Japanese monetary authorities are expected to step into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market soon to try to lift the dollar against the yen so as to help protect Japanese exports, dealers and analysts say. \par \par \uc2  They worry that a stronger yen could choke off growth in exports and derail Japan's export-led economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  The dollar clung above 119.50 yen by midday on Monday, having fallen to a near two-month low of 119.54 yen just before the weekend, leaving the market nervous about when Japanese monetary authorities may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the outlook for the dollar was increasingly bearish due to the U.S. Federal Reserve's surprising half-point rate cut last Wednesday, tensions surrounding Iraq and volatility in the U.S. equity market. \par \par \uc2  "The authorities seem to be seeking the best time now to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as they do not want to do it in vain," said Tomoko Fujii, an economist at Nikko Salomon Smith Barney. \par \par \uc2  Although the dollar's recent fall is due chiefly to factors outside Japan, amid what Fujii called moves led by speculators, she said there were no factors to buy the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the longer term, with the stock market's key Nikkei average barely holding above 8,500, down 19 percent since January. \par \par \uc2  Officials at Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) are growing tense about the yen's current level. \par \par \uc2  Its \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief, Haruhiko Kuroda, said on Monday the yen's recent sharp rise against the dollar had been totally inappropriate, and the ministry would take necessary action on exchange rates. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's level against the yen is already well below Japanese exporters' expectations. \par \par \uc2  Big manufacturers used an average rate of 120.82 yen to the dollar for October-March, the second half of the business year, for their sales and profit forecasts, according to the Bank of Japan's latest "tankan" quarterly survey of corporate sentiment, issued in early October. \par \par \uc2  HOW SOON? \par \par \uc2  Toru Umemoto, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Morgan Stanley Japan, also expects the authorities to step into the market at any time. \par \par \uc2  "Taking into account the comparison of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 -U.S. stock prices and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be well-justified at current levels of the dollar against the yen," Umemoto said. \par \par \uc2  In the April-June quarter Japan spent a total of 4.0162 trillion yen ($33.14 billion) on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, the MOF said last week, the largest amount for any quarter since it started disclosing such data in 1991. \par \par \uc2  The authorities last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on June 28, when the dollar slipped below 119 yen. \par \par \uc2  They have kept out of the market since then, even when the dollar fell to 115.50 yen in mid-July. \par \par \uc2  "At that time, U.S. accounting scandals swirled in the market and Japan was unable to control the problem (of the yen's rise)," said Nikko Salomon's Fujii. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the authorities may be more tense now as Japan could face further deflationary pressure amid efforts to clean up non-performing loans in the financial sector, officially estimated at 52 trillion yen ($433.7 billion). \par \par \uc2  A member of the Bank of Japan's Policy board, Teizo Taya, said on Thursday he could accept a weaker yen if deflationary pressures increased, as a rising yen would likely exacerbate such pressures. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa, for his part, told parliament last Thursday that the government should not interfere with \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, and exchange rates should be determined by markets. \par \par \uc2  Whatever the intention of his words, others see the dollar continuing to fall regardless of whether \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  and buys dollars for yen. \par \par \uc2  "As long as the dollar is falling on its own bearish factors, talking down the yen or actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't work," said Kaneo Ogino, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at HSBC in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  "The market will be watching Japanese machinery orders data for September due out later in the day and July-September growth data (out on Wednesday) to see whether the authorities can justify \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." ($1=119.90 Yen). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021111dybb006y6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
