\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar on defensive on worries over U.S. economy. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Chikafumi Hodo \par \uc2 725 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 November 2002\par 08:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Nov 8 (Reuters) - The dollar stayed on the defensive in Asia on Friday, a pullback from near two-month lows on the yen in early trade failing to dispel bearish sentiment amid concerns over the U.S. economy. \par \par \uc2  The greenback briefly hit 120.81 yen - its lowest level since mid-September - as the Federal Reserve's surprise half-point rate cut on Wednesday failed to boost Wall Street, with key indices seeing their biggest falls in three weeks. \par \par \uc2  Sentiment was also dampened by lingering concerns about military tensions between Iraq and the United States. \par \par \uc2  "Dollar selling is expected to continue," said Kosuke Hanao, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales at the Royal Bank of Scotland (RBS) in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  "(Fed Chairman Alan) Greenspan's view on the U.S. economy appeared very severe, and the Fed also showed concerns about Iraq by mentioning geopolitical risks," he added. \par \par \uc2  As of 0646 GMT, the dollar was quoted at 120.93/98 yen compared with its late Thursday U.S. level of 121.08/13. \par \par \uc2  With the dollar approaching the closely watched 120 yen level, traders were growing nervous about possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Japanese monetary authorities. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Friday it had conducted no \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the July-September quarter. \par \par \uc2  But in April-June it spent a total of 4.0162 trillion yen ($33.14 billion) on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep down the yen, the largest amount spent in any quarter since it started disclosing such data in 1991. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers said the authorities have kept out of the market since June 28. \par \par \uc2  LEAVE RATES TO MARKET \par \par \uc2  Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa told parliament on Thursday that the government should not interfere with \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and that \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates should be determined by the market. The dollar is expected to test 120.50 yen, then 120 yen in the near term, but growing concerns about possible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  provided support. \par \par \uc2  Many dealers noted that officials at \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) were growing tense about the yen's current level. \par \par \uc2  Japanese authorities worry that a stronger yen could choke off growth in exports and derail an export-led economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  A member of the Bank of Japan's Policy board, Teizo Taya, said on Thursday he could accept a weaker yen if deflationary pressures increased from efforts to dispose of Japanese banks' non-performing loans, as a rising yen would likely exacerbate such pressures. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan's latest "tankan" quarterly survey of corporate sentiment, issued in early October, showed Japan's big manufacturers used an average rate of 120.82 yen to the dollar for the October-March second half of the business year for sales and profit forecasts. \par \par \uc2  But the market is not expecting the yen to appreciate too much given Japan's dismal fundamentals, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "It's possible to see the dollar falling to around 118 yen. I'm also not sure whether (the monetary authorities) would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  swiftly when it drops below 120," RBS's Hanao said. \par \par \uc2  "The market has been selling the yen steadily, reflecting Japan's weak fundamentals, and I don't feel that trend will change drastically. That's why the market is still careful about holding fresh yen positions," Hanao said. \par \par \uc2  EURO RISE SUSTAINABLE? \par \par \uc2  Negative factors surrounding the dollar boosted the euro, which shot up as high as $1.0099 a three-and-a-half month high, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Some said a decision by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday to leave rates unchanged sparked flows into higher-yielding euro assets, while others said the European single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise was unsustainable as it was largely due to dollar weakness. \par \par \uc2  "The euro's rise overnight was not because of the euro's own factors, it was merely a benefit from the dollar's weakness," said Mitsuru Yaguchi, a senior economist at Mitsubishi Securities. \par \par \uc2  "As the U.S. economic weakness also points to global and European economic weakness, I expect the euro will be unable to sustain that strength, although it could briefly approach the $1.02 level," he added. \par \par \uc2  The euro was at $1.0075/80 against $1.0093/98 in late Thursday New York trade. \par \par \uc2  Sterling edged back up near $1.58 in late Asian trade, standing at $1.5790/5800. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021108dyb800e1v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
